1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Cassandane
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassandane   was an Achaemenian Persian noblewoman and the "dearly loved" wife of Cyrus the Great. She was a daughter of Pharnaspes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She bore four children for Cyrus the Great (Cyrus II):
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Cambyses II who succeeded his father and conquered Egypt, 
2 CONT * Smerdis (Bardiya) who also reigned as the king of Persia for a short time, a daughter named
2 CONT *  Atossa who later wed Darius the Great (Darius I), and another daughter named
2 CONT *  Roxana.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughter Atossa later played an important role in Achaemenid royal family, as she married Darius the Great and bore him the next Achaemenian king Xerxes I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassandane's daughter, Atossa, had a "great authority" in the Achaemenian royal house and her marriage with Darius I is likely due to her power, influence and the fact that she was a direct descendent of Cyrus the Great (Cyrus II)
2 CONC .[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT  When Cassandane died all the nations of Cyrus' Persian empire observed "a great mourning". This is reported by Herodotus. According to a report in the chronicle of Nabonidus there was a public mourning after her death in Babyloni
2 CONC a lasting for six days. According to a suggestion by M. Boyce, Cassandane's tomb is located at Pasargadae.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassandane
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Cassandane fue un noble persa aqueménida, esposa de, Ciro .  Hija de Pharnaspes . Tuvo a  cuatro hijos de Ciro el Grande (Ciro II):  Cambises II , que sucedió a su padre y conquistó Egipto, Esmerdis ( Bardiya ) que también reinó c
2 CONC omo el rey de Persia por un corto tiempo, nombre de la hija Atossa más tarde para casarse con Darío el Grande (Darius I) y otra hija llamada Roxana. Su hija Atossa tarde jugó un papel importante  se casó con Darío el Grande y le d
2 CONC io a luz el próximo rey aqueménida Jerjes I . la hija de Cassandane, Atosa, tenía un "gran autoridad" en la casa real aqueménida y su matrimonio con Darío .Cuando murió 538 ac.  Cassandane, todas las naciones del imperio persa ...
2 CONC observaron "un gran duelo".. ... De acuerdo con una sugerencia de M. Boyce, la tumba de Cassandane se encuentra en Pasargadae . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fuente  H. de Grecia ;Herodoto- M. Boyce
