1 NOTE Cambyses I (flourished 6th century bc), Persian king of the Achaemenid 
2 CONT dynasty who ruled (circa 600-559 bc) over Anshan (now Baghdad Province, 
2 CONT Iraq) as a vassal of the Medes, then preeminent in Persia. Cambyses I was 
2 CONT the son of Cyrus I (flourished 7th century bc), whom he succeeded, and the 
2 CONT father of Cyrus the Great, who founded the Persian Empire. According to 
2 CONT the Greek historian Herodotus, Cambyses I married the daughter of 
2 CONT Astyages, king of the Medes (reigned about 584-550 bc).
