1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damaspia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Damaspia''' (from Old Persian *Jāmāspi- [1] or *ðāmāspyā-[2]) was a queen of Persia, wife of King Artaxerxes I, and mother of Xerxes II, his legitimate heir. She was Persian.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Greek historian Ctesias of Cnidus, King Artaxerxes and his wife died the same day (424 BC, perhaps during a military expedition), and their corpses were carried to Persia. Xerxes succeeded his father, but was murd
2 CONC ered not much later (423 BC) by his half-brother Sogdianus.
2 CONT
2 CONT The epitome made by Photius of Ctesias' book is the only source that mentions Damaspia by name.[3] Documents from Babylon dating in Artaxerxes' reign, refer to certain estates as "the house of the woman of the palace". This anonym
2 CONC ous woman could be Damaspia, or the Queen Mother Amestris.[4]
