1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Amynander''' (Greek: Ἀμύνανδρος, Amynandros, in Polybios also Amynas) was king of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athamanians Athamanes] in south Epirus, following his predecessor Theodorus of Athamania. He w
2 CONC as a brother-in-law of the Illyrian king [https://www.geni.com/people/Scerdilaidas/6000000205410358821 '''Scerdilaidas'''] and first appears in history as a mediator between [https://www.geni.com/people/Philip-V-king-of-Macedon/60
2 CONC 00000002447257896  '''Philip V of Macedon'''] and the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aetolia Aetolians].
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amynander_of_Athamania En]
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Romans were about to wage war on Philip, they sent ambassadors to Amynander to inform him of their intention. On the commencement of the war, he came to the camp of the Romans and promised them assistance: the task of bri
2 CONC nging over the Aetolians to an alliance with the Romans was assigned to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 198 BC Amynander took the towns of Phoca and Gomphi, and ravaged Thessaly. He was present at the conference between Flamininus and Philip, and during the short truce was sent by the former to Rome. He was again present at the c
2 CONC onference held with Philip after the battle of Cynoscephalae. On the conclusion of peace, he was allowed to retain all the fortresses which he had taken from Philip.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the war which the Romans, supported by Philip, waged with Antiochus III, Amynander was induced by his brother-in-law, Philip of Megalopolis, to side with Antiochus, to whom he rendered active service. But in 191 BC he was drive
2 CONC n from his kingdom by Philip and fled with his wife and children to Ambracia. The Romans required that he should be delivered up, but their demand was not complied with, and with the assistance of the Aetolians he recovered his ki
2 CONC ngdom. He sent ambassadors to Rome and to the Scipiones (generals Scipio Africanus and Lucius Cornelius Scipio, who would earn the agnomen "Asiaticus" from this war) in Asia, to treat for peace, which was granted him. (b. c. 189.)
2 CONC  He afterwards induced the Ambraciots to surrender to the Romans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amynander married Apama, the daughter of Alexander of Megalopolis. Regarding his death we have no accounts. 
