1 NOTE {geni:about_me} TharrhypasFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia  (Redirected from Tharypus) Jump to: navigation, search  Tharrhypas[1] (Greek: Θαρύπας, 430 - 392 BC) was a king of the Molossians. He is mentioned by Thucydides[2] a
2 CONC s a minor in 429 BC. He was the father of Alcetas, and is said to have been the first to introduce Hellenic civilization among his subjects[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References1.^ Alternative forms: Tharrypas, Tharyps, Tharrytas, Tharybas 2.^ History of the Peloponnesian War 2. 80) 3.^ Pausanias 1. 11 , Plutarch Pyrrhus Preceded by Admetus King of Epirus 430– 392 BC Succeeded by Alcetas
2 CONC  I  Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tharrhypas" Categories: Rulers of Ancient Epirus | 5th-century BC Greek people | 5th-century BC rulersPersonal tools Log in / create accountNamespaces ArticleDiscussionVariantsViews
2 CONC  ReadEditView historyActions Search
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Tharrhypas=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Tharrhypas (Greek: Θαρύπας, 430-392 BC) was a king of the Molossians and ancestor of Alexander the Great. He is mentioned by Thucydides as a minor in 429 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the father of Alcetas I, and is said to have been the first to introduce southern Greek (namely Attic) cultural traits among Molossians.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
