1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Antiochus III the Great, (Greek Ἀντίoχoς Μέγας; ca. 241–187 BC, ruled 222–187 BC), younger son of Seleucus II Callinicus, became the 6th ruler of the Seleucid Empire as a youth of about eighteen in 223 BC. Ascendin
2 CONC g the throne at young age, Antiochus was an ambitious ruler. Although his early attempts in war against the Ptolemaic Kingdom were unsuccessful, in the following years of conquest Antiochus proved himself as the most successful Se
2 CONC leucid King after Seleucus I himself. His traditional designation, the Great, reflects an epithet he briefly assumed after his Eastern Campaign (it appears in regnal formulas at Amyzon in 203 and 202 BC, but not later). Antiochos 
2 CONC also assumed the title "Basileus Megas" (which is Greek for Great King), the traditional title of the Persian kings, which he adopted after his conquest of Koile Syria.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antiochus_III_the_Great
2 CONT
2 CONT Main deeds:
2 CONT
2 CONT April-June 222: Comes to power after the assassination of his elder brother, Seleucus III Keraunos, who has unsuccessfully tried to recover territories that had been lost to king Attalus I Soter of Pergamon 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus' general Achaeus has more success, but proclaims himself king 
2 CONT
2 CONT 222: Wedding; Antiochus marries Laodice III 
2 CONT
2 CONT 222-220: Antiochus suppresses the revolt of Molon in Media and Persis 
2 CONT
2 CONT 219: outbreak of the Fourth Syrian War against king Ptolemy IV Philopator; Antiochus reconquers Seleucia (the port of Antioch, which had been conquered by Ptolemy III in the Third Syrian War) and proceeds to the south, capturing T
2 CONC yre 
2 CONT
2 CONT 217, 13 June: Ptolemy's army defeats the Seleucid army at Raphia with an army that consists partly of Egyptian soldiers 
2 CONT
2 CONT October 217: peace is concluded; the Seleucid Empire keeps Seleucia 
2 CONT
2 CONT 216: Alliance with Attalus against Achaeus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 216-213: Antiochus defeats Achaeus and captures Sardes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 212-205: Antiochus reconquers the independent kingdoms in Parthia and Bactria and Gandara; he is called Megas, 'the great' 
2 CONT
2 CONT 205: Ptolemy IV Philopator falls ill; Antiochus and Philip V of Macedonia agree to attack Egypt 
2 CONT
2 CONT 204: Birth of Cleopatra I Syra 
2 CONT
2 CONT 204, September: Ptolemy IV succeeded by Ptolemy V Epiphanes 
2 CONT
2 CONT 202, May: Outbreak of the Fifth Syrian War; renewed attempt to conquer southern Syria 
2 CONT
2 CONT 200: Battle of Paneion: Ptolemy V loses his Asian territories; Antiochus' daughter Cleopatra Syra marries to the Egyptian king 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rome declares war against Macedonia (which leaves the war against the Ptolemies) and orders Antiochus to keep their hands off Egypt, which is vital for Rome's food supply 
2 CONT
2 CONT 199-197: Antiochus cancels his invasion of Egypt, and instead attacks Ptolemaic possessions in Cilicia 
2 CONT
2 CONT 196: Antiochus appointed as successor; he marries his sister Laodice IV 
2 CONT
2 CONT 196: Conquest of Thrace, which is governed by Antiochus' son Seleucus 
2 CONT
2 CONT 194: The Pergamene king Eumenes II Soter refuses an alliance; Ariarathes IV Eusebes of Cappadocia marries to Antiochis 
2 CONT
2 CONT 193: Death of his crown prince Antiochus 
2 CONT
2 CONT 192-188: Syrian War against Rome and its allies Pergamon and Rhodos; the Carthaginian general in Seleucid service, Hannibal Barca, and Antiochus are defeated 
2 CONT
2 CONT 191: Marries Euboea of Chalcis 
2 CONT
2 CONT 189: Seleucus made co-ruler; he probably marries his sister Laodice IV, widow of Antiochus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peace of Apamea: cedes all territory north of the Taurus to the Roman ally Pergamon and agrees to pay an indemnity to Rome; his youngest son Antiochus is sent to Italy as hostage 
2 CONT
2 CONT 187 Antiochus visits Babylon 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 July 187: in an attempt to obtain money, Antiochus attacks a temple in Susa, but is killed 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
