1 NOTE Antiochus III, called The Great (242-187 bc), king of Syria (223-187 bc), 
2 CONT the son of Seleucus II and brother of Seleucus III, whom he succeeded. He 
2 CONT was the most distinguished of the Seleucids. Having made vassal states out 
2 CONT of Parthia and Bactria, he warred successfully against the Egyptian king 
2 CONT Ptolemy V and in 198 bc obtained possession of all of Palestine and 
2 CONT Lebanon. He later became involved in a conflict with the Romans, who 
2 CONT defeated him at Thermopylae in 191 bc and at Magnesia (now Manisa, Turkey) 
2 CONT in 190 bc. As the price of peace, he was forced to surrender all his 
2 CONT dominions west of the Taurus Mountains and to pay costly tribute. 
2 CONT Antiochus, who early in his reign had restored the Seleucid Empire, 
2 CONT finally forfeited its influence in the eastern Mediterranean by his 
2 CONT failure to recognize the rising power of Rome.
1 NOTE SOURCE NOTES:
2 CONT http://www.american-pictures.com/genealogy/persons/per00859.htm#0
1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT "the Great"; King of Syria 223-187
1 NOTE KNOWN AS "THE GREAT""MEGAS"; KING OF SYRIA 223-187BC; DEFEATED BY ROMANS AT
2 CONT MAGNESIA 190 BC; MURDERED WHILE ROBBING A TEMPLE IN THE ELYMAEAN HILLS;
2 CONT b.242-d.187 BC
1 NOTE OR "CERAUNOS""SOTER"; KING OF SYRIA 225-223 BC; MURDERED 223 BC
