1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ID: I82409 Name: Isias Philostorgos Given Name: Isias Surname: Philostorgos Sex: F _UID: 650DC6351EA9A84FAE0B3B7A6D16EB9BA282 Change Date: 26 Nov 2005 Death: Y
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Samus I of Commagene Married: Children
2 CONT  Mithridates I of Commagene
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source: http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=jdp-fam&id=I82409
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isias:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isias From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Isias Philostorgos Queen consort of Commagene Tenure	70 BC - 38 BC (32 years) Spouse	King Antiochus I Theos of Commagene Issue	Mithridates II of Commagene Laodice, Queen of Parthia Princ
2 CONC e Antiochus II Princess Antiochis Athenais, Queen of Media Atropatene Full name Isias Philostorgos House	House of Ariarathid (by birth) Orontid Dynasty (by marriage) Father	King Ariobarzanes I of Cappadocia Mother	Athenais Philost
2 CONC orgos I Died	late 30s/early 20s BC Burial	Kahta Isias, surnamed Philostorgos or Philostorgus (Greek: η Ισίας Φιλόστοργος, meaning Isias the loving one) was a Princess of Cappadocia who lived in the 1st century BC. Through her marr
2 CONC iage to King Antiochus I Theos of Commagene, she became the Queen of Commagene. Very little is known on her. She was half Persian and half Greek. Isias was the daughter of King Ariobarzanes I of Cappadocia and his wife Queen Athen
2 CONC ais Philostorgos I, while her brother was King Ariobarzanes II of Cappadocia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isias and Antiochus I had five children who were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Son, Mithridates II of Commagene - who succeeded Antiochus I after his death in 38 BC Daughter, Laodice, who married King Orodes II of Parthia Son, Antiochus II of Commagene Daughter, Antiochis of Commagene Daughter, Athenais who 
2 CONC married King Artavasdes I of Media Atropatene She appeared to have died of unknown causes sometime between the late 30s or early 20s BC. Isias was buried along with her daughter and her granddaughter on a burial site. This burial 
2 CONC site was a monument known as the Karakush or Karakus Tumulus, also known as The Black Bird. The monument received its name because there is a column topped by an eagle, which has earned the mound name.
2 CONT
2 CONT This burial sanctuary was constructed and built by her son King Mithridates II of Commagene. Mithridates II built this sanctuary to bury and honour the lives and the memories of Isias, her daughter Antiochis and her granddaughter 
2 CONC Aka I of Commagene. It is located 12 km or 7.5 miles from Kahta, Turkey. Each tumulus is surrounded by groups of three Doric Columns. Each column was about 9 metres or 29.5 feet high. It is topped with steles, reliefs and statues 
2 CONC of a bull, lion and eagle.
2 CONT
2 CONT This monument has Greek honorific inscriptions, which provides information about this site. It is inscribed on the external face of the two drums of the central column of the Northeast. Skipping a couple of phrases where restorati
2 CONC on has been doubtful, the inscription reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT This is the hierothesion [sacred site or foundation] of Isias, whom the great King Mithridates (she being his own mother)…deemed worthy of this final hour. And…Antiochis lies herein, the king’s sister by the same mother, the most 
2 CONC beautiful of women, whose life was short but her honours long-enduring. Both of these, as you see, preside here, and with them a daughter’s daughter, the daughter of Antiochis, Aka. A memorial of life with each other and of the ki
2 CONC ng’s honour. Isias’ name also appears in another honorific inscription dedicated by Mithridates II at the tomb of her other daughter Laodice:
2 CONT
2 CONT The great King Mithridates, the son of the great king Antiochus and queen Isias, dedicated this image to the unfading memory of queen Laodice, the king’s sister and the wife of Orodes, the king of kings, and to her own honour. Aft
2 CONC er the Kingdom of Commagene was annexed in 72 by the Roman Emperor Vespasian, the vault of the tomb has been looted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources[edit] http://www.guide-martine.com/southeastern3.asp http://www.ancientlibrary.com/smith-bio/0412.html Campbell-Scott, Roger. "Nimrud Dagh - A Sacred Mountain in Anatolia", in Vanished Civilizations: The Hidden Secrets of 
2 CONC Lost Cities and Forgotten Peoples, pp. 194–197. Reader’s Digest Services P/L, Hong Kong, 1988. ISBN 0-276-42658-4.
