2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Laodice III=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Laodice III (in Greek Λαοδικη, died about 191 BC), was a Princess of Pontus and a Seleucid Queen. She was a daughter born to King Mithridates II of Pontus and his wife Laodice. Her sister was Laodice of Pontus and her brother was 
2 CONC Mithridates III of Pontus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laodice married her maternal first cousin, the Seleucid King Antiochus III the Great soon after his accession about 222 BC in a ceremony at Zeugma. She was proclaimed queen by Antiochus III at Antioch before he set out in his expe
2 CONC dition against Molon. The birth of their eldest child Antiochus, took place during the absence of the king on that expedition. Laodice’s name does not appear in any sources; however her name is recorded in a number of inscriptions
2 CONC  alongside her husband’s in the later years of her husband’s reign at Sardis, Herakleia-by-Latmos (see Latmus), Iasos and Antioch. When she died Antiochus III developed a cult for her and was granted divine honors alongside her hu
2 CONC sband at Teos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Laodice III bore Antiochus III eight children who were: Antiochus, Seleucus IV Philopator, Ardys, an unnamed daughter who was engaged to Demetrius I of Bactria, Laodice IV, Cleopatra I Syra, Antiochis and Antiochus IV Epiphanes bo
2 CONC rn as Mithridates. After her death, Antiochus III married to Euboea of Chalcis, by whom he had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laodice_III
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Laodice of Pontus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Laodice (in Greek Λαοδικη; lived in the 3rd century BC), was a princess of Pontus and was one of the daughters of Mithridates II of Pontus and Laodice. Her sister was Laodice III, the first wife of Antiochus III the Great , and he
2 CONC r brother was Mithridates III of Pontus. She married her distant maternal cousin, the Seleucid general Achaeus. When Achaeus fell into the power of Antiochus III (213 BC), Laodice was left in possession of the citadel of Sardis, i
2 CONC n which she held out for a time, but she was quickly compelled by the dissensions among her own troops to surrender to Antiochus III. Polybius incidentally mentions that she was brought up before her marriage at Selge, in Pisidia 
2 CONC (modern Turkey), under the care of Logbasis, a citizen of that place.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laodice_of_Pontus
