1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sanatruces of Parthia
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Coin of Sanatruces of Parthia from the mint at Rhagae. The reverse shows a seated archer carrying a bow. The Greek inscription reads ΒΑΣΙΛΕΩΣ ΜΕΓΑΛΟΥ [Ε]ΥΕΡΓΕΤΟΥ ΕΠΙΦΑΝΟΥΣ ΦΙΛΕΛΛΗΝΟ[Σ] (great king Arsaces, civilized, friend of Gre
2 CONC eks).  King Sanatruces of Parthia (also Sinatruces or Sanatruk, ca. 157 BC – 70 BC) ruled the Parthian Empire from c. 77 to c. 70 BC. He was a member of the Arsacid house, who, according to work attributed to Lucian, in the troubl
2 CONC ed times after the death of Mithridates II in ca. 88 BC was made king by the Sacaraucae Scythians or Saka, an Indo-European tribe akin to the Parthians who had invaded Iran in about 77 BC[1][2].
2 CONT  "Sinatroces, king of Parthia, was restored to his country in his eightieth year by the Sacauracian Scyths, assumed the throne and held it seven years." Makrobioi, 15.[3]  He died circa 70 BCE and was succeeded by his son Phraates
2 CONC  III.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Sanatruces in Byzantine tradition
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Sanatruces (Sanatrucius), the son of Mithridates IV is mentioned as an ephemeral Parthian king in AD 115 by John Malalas, in his Chronographia.
2 CONT
