2 CONC  A.0.I01.65:3'. 5.^ RIMA 3 A.0.102.2 ii 37. 6.^ RIMA 2 A.0.96.2001 clay cylinder. 7.^ Synchronistic Kinglist, Ass 14616c (KAV 216), iii 7. [edit] References1.^ A. Fuchs (1998). "Aššur-rabi II". In K. Radner. The Prosopography of t
2 CONC he Neo-Assyrian Empire, Volume 1, Part I: A. The Neo-Assyrian Text Corpus Project. p. 209. 2.^ Martin Sicker (2000). The Pre-Islamic Middle East. Praeger. p. 48. 3.^ Wayne T. Pitard (1987). Ancient Damascus: A Historical Study of 
2 CONC the Syrian City-State from Earliest Times until Its Fall to the Assyrians in 732 B.C.E.. Eisenbrauns. p. 91. 4.^ Stephen W. Holloway (1997). "Assyria and Babylonia in the Tenth Century". In Lowell K. Handy. The Age of Solomon: Sch
2 CONC olarship at the Turn of the Millennium. Brill. p. 2009. 5.^ Hartmut Kühne, ed. (2010). "Production and Consumption at Dūr-Katlimmu, A Survey of the Evidence". Dūr-Katlimmu 2008 and Beyond. Otto Harrassowitz Verlag. p. 69. 6.^ J. N
2 CONC eumann, S. Parpola (Jul., 1987). "Climatic Change and the Eleventh-Tenth-Century Eclipse of Assyria and Babylonia". Journal of Near Eastern Studies 46 (3): 180. http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ashur-rabi_II
