2 CONT The genealogical table shows the relationship between the various members ofs the Herodian family mentioned in the New Testament.
2 CONT      The Herodian family were Idumaens by birth, but had become converts to the Jewish faith.  Their object was to found, under the protection of Rome, a semi-independent kingdom.  By his marriage with Mariamne Herod the Great all
2 CONC ied himself with the family of the Maccabees, who had been for several generations the leaders of the patriotic party among the Jews. Horod was a successful ruler and was on terms of friendship with Augustus, the Roman Emperor.  I
2 CONC n order to gain favor with his subajectys, with whom he was most unpopular, he rebuilt the te4mple at an immense cost.  (See Temple of Herod in
2 CONT  the Bible Dictionary.)  His reign was disgraced by many acts of cruelty.  In a fit of jealousy he had his wife, whom he dearly loved, put to death; later on he had her two sons, Alexander and Aristobulus, also murdered.  In the s
2 CONC ame year in which he gave the order for the massacre of the infants at Bethlehem, he had Antipater, anothaer of his own sons, put to death.  A few months later Herod himself died.  His kingdom was then divided between three of his
2 CONC  sons: Archelaus, who received Judaea, Idumaea, and Samaria; Antipas, who had Galilee and Peraea; and Philip, who had the northeast districts of Palestine.
2 CONT      After a reign of nine years Archelaus was deposed by Ausustus, and Judaea was attached to the Roman province of Syria, being governed by as Procurator.  Antipas (called in the New Testament "Herod the tetratch") built as his 
2 CONC capital Tiberias on the Sea of Galilee; he is frequently mentioned in the Gospels (Matt. 14: 1; Mark 6: 14; Luke 9: 7; 13: 31;23:7-15). He took as his wife Herodias, the wife of his half-brother Philip.  He was deposed by the empe
2 CONC ror Caligula and banished to Lugdunum in Gaul, A.D. 39.  Philip made Caesarea Philippi (previously called Panias) his capital, and remained in possession of his tetrarchy until his death in A.D. 33.  His terrirtory then became par
2 CONC t of the province of Syria, but in A.D. 37 it was gaiven by Caligula, along with Abilene (the tetratchy of Lysanias), to Agrippa, who was allowed to assume the title of king.  On the deposition of Antipas he obtained the tetratchy
2 CONC  of Galilee, and in A.D. 41 on the accession of the Emperor Claudius, he received Judaea and Sammaria as well, and so became ruler of the whole territory governed by his grandfather.  He lived in jerusalem and was anxious to be re
2 CONC garded as an orthodox Jew.  He began a persecution of the Church, and put James to death, Peter escaping by a miracle (Acts 12: 1-23). His death is described in Acts 12:20-23.  His son Agrippa II, was allowed by the Emperor Claudi
2 CONC us to succeed to only a small part oof his father's dominions.  He is mentioned in Acts 25: 13.  He was the last of the Herods.
2 CONT
