1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Julius_Alexander '''Marcus Julius Alexander''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Julius Alexander (flourished 1st century, died before August 44) was a distinguished and wealthy Alexandrian Jewish Merchant.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ancestry and Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus was born and raised in Alexandria, Egypt. He was either born in the late reign of Emperor Augustus 27 BC-14 or the reign of the Emperor Tiberius 14–37. Marcus was born as the second son to Alexander the Alabarch, a wealthy 
2 CONC Jewish Pro-Roman aristocrat and his brother was Tiberius Julius Alexander. His paternal uncle was the Jewish Historian, Philosopher Philo and Lysimachus.
2 CONT
2 CONT He came from an aristocratic family who lived in Alexandria for generations. His ancestors and family were contemporaries to the rule of the Ptolemaic dynasty and the rule of the Seleucid Empire. Marcus came from a family who were
2 CONC  noble, honourable and wealthy. It was either his paternal grandfather or paternal great grandfather who was granted Roman citizenship from Roman dictator Gaius Julius Caesar. His ancestors and family had social ties and connectio
2 CONC ns to the Priesthood in Judea; Hasmonean Dynasty; Herodian Dynasty and Julio-Claudian dynasty in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus along with his family were contemporaries to the life of Jesus of Nazareth and the lives of The Apostles of Jesus. Marcus along with his brother received a thorough education. They were educated in the Egyptian, Jewish, Gre
2 CONC ek and Roman cultures, particularly in the traditions of Judaism, the study of the Old Testament and in Greek Philosophy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus with his brother followed their father into business, becoming partners in an import-export firm. The Petrie Ostraca, mentions Marcus’ activities at Myos Hormos and Berenice Troglodytica, ports located in the Red Sea betwee
2 CONC n 37-44. He had agent called Hermias, who represented him at an unnamed port.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus’ father and Herodian King Agrippa I were long time friends. Agrippa I - as an elegant way to give something back to Alexander the Alabarch, who supported Agrippa I in the past - arranged for his daughter, princess Berenice,
2 CONC  to marry Marcus. In 41, Marcus married Berenice as her first husband. This marriage shows that there were good relations between Marcus’ family and the Herodian Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, this marriage was short lived, as Marcus died before August 44. Marcus had no children with Berenice. Berenice’s father later arranged for her to marry her paternal uncle Herod of Chalcis in 44. http://en.wikipedia.
2 CONC org/wiki/Marcus_Julius_Alexander
2 CONT >>>________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Wagner, Sir Anthony Richard; ‘Pedigree and Progress: Essays in the Genealogical Interpretation of History’
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>____________
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/1055-alabarch
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Marcus Julius Alexander=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Julius Alexander (16 - 44 CE), the son of Alexander the Alabarch and brother of Tiberius Julius Alexander, was a distinguished and wealthy Alexandrian Jewish merchant.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ancestry and Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus was born and raised in Alexandria, Egypt. Marcus was born as the second son to Alexander the Alabarch, a wealthy Jewish aristocrat, and his older brother was Tiberius Julius Alexander. His paternal uncle was the exegete and
2 CONC  philosopher Philo.
2 CONT
2 CONT He came from an aristocratic family who lived in Alexandria for generations. His ancestors and family were contemporaries to the rule of the Ptolemaic dynasty and the rule of the Seleucid Empire. Marcus came from a family who were
2 CONC  noble, honourable and wealthy. It was either his paternal grandfather or paternal great grandfather who was granted Roman citizenship from Roman dictator Gaius Julius Caesar. His ancestors and family had social ties and connectio
2 CONC ns to the Priesthood in Judea; Hasmonean Dynasty; Herodian Dynasty and Julio-Claudian dynasty in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus along with his family were contemporaries to the life of Jesus of Nazareth and the lives of The Apostles of Jesus. Marcus along with his brother received a thorough education. They were educated in the Egyptian, Jewish, Gre
2 CONC ek and Roman cultures, particularly in the traditions of Judaism, the study of the Old Testament and in Greek Philosophy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus’ father and Herodian King Agrippa I were long time friends. Agrippa I as an elegant way to give something back to Alexander the Alabarch, who supported Agrippa I in the past, arranged for his daughter princess Berenice to m
2 CONC arry Marcus. In 41, Marcus married Berenice as her first husband. This marriage shows that there were good relations between Marcus’ family and the Herodian Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, this marriage was short lived as Marcus died before August 44. Marcus had no children with Berenice. Berenice’s father later arranged for her to marry her paternal uncle Herod of Chalcis in 44.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Julius_Alexander
