1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Tiberius Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus or Claudius I (August 1, 10 BC – October 13, AD 54) (Tiberius Claudius Drusus from birth to AD 4, then Tiberius Claudius Nero Germanicus from then until his accession) w
2 CONC as the fourth Roman Emperor of the Julio-Claudian dynasty, ruling from January 24, AD 41 to his death in AD 54. Born in Lugdunum in Gaul (modern-day Lyon, France), to Drusus and Antonia Minor, he was the first Roman Emperor to be 
2 CONC born outside Italia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Claudius was considered a rather unlikely man to become emperor. He was reportedly afflicted with some type of disability, and his family had virtually excluded him from public office until his consulship with his nephew Caligula 
2 CONC in AD 37. This infirmity may have saved him from the fate of many other Roman nobles during the purges of Tiberius' and Caligula's reigns; potential enemies did not see him as a serious threat to them. His very survival led to his
2 CONC  being declared emperor after Caligula's assassination, at which point he was the last adult male of his family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his lack of political experience, Claudius proved to be an able administrator and a great builder of public works. His reign saw an expansion of the empire, including the conquest of Britain. He took a personal interest in
2 CONC  the law, presided at public trials, and issued up to twenty edicts a day; however, he was seen as vulnerable throughout his rule, particularly by the nobility. Claudius was constantly forced to shore up his position—resulting in 
2 CONC the deaths of many senators. Claudius also suffered tragic setbacks in his personal life, one of which may have led to his murder. These events damaged his reputation among the ancient writers. More recent historians have revised 
2 CONC this opinion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Read more: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claudius 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanicus
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT (41-ass.54 apr JC) (°10 av) 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Claudius I (EMPEROR) of ROME aka Tiberius Claudius Nero Germanicus; aka Tiberius Nero DRUSUS
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: Gaul 1 Aug 10 BC Died: 13 Oct 54 poisoned by wife
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT ·He was not all as he seemed. His family members mistook these physical debilities as reflective of mental infirmity and generally kept him out of the public eye as an embarrassment. A sign of this familial disdain is that he rema
2 CONC ined under guardianship, like a woman, even after he had reached the age of majority. Suetonius, in particular, preserves comments of Antonia, his mother, and Livia, his grandmother, which are particularly cruel in their assessmen
2 CONC t of the boy. From the same source, however, it emerges that Augustus suspected that there was more to this idiot than met the eye.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, Claudius spent his entire childhood and youth in almost complete seclusion. The normal rites de passage of an imperial prince came and went without official notice, and Claudius received no summons to public office o
2 CONC r orders to command troops on the frontiers. When he assumed the toga virilis, for instance, he was carried to the Capitol in a litter at night; the normal procedure was to be led into the Forum by one's father or guardian in full
2 CONC  public view. How he spent the voluminous free time of his youth is revealed by his later character: he read voraciously. He became a scholar of considerable ability and composed works on all subjects in the liberal arts, especial
2 CONC ly history; he was the last person we know of who could read Etruscan. These skills, and the knowledge of governmental institutions he acquired from studying history, were to stand him in good stead when he came to power.
2 CONT
2 CONT ·the son of Nero Claudius Drusus, a popular and successful Roman general, and the younger Antonia, he was the nephew of the emperor Tiberius and a grandson of Livia Drusilla, the wife of the emperor Augustus
2 CONT
2 CONT 0042 AD, January . ·added Pater Patriae to his titles ·called Tiberius Claudius Drusus at his birth
2 CONT
2 CONT 0004 AD . ·called Tiberius Claudius Nero Germanicus on the adoption of his elder brother, Germanicus, by the Emperor Tiberius
2 CONT
2 CONT 0041 AD, January 24 . ·called Tiberius Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus, Pontifex Maximus, on his accession
2 CONT
2 CONT 0037 AD . ·drawn out of obscurity by his nephew, Caligula, who made him a senator and consul
2 CONT
2 CONT 0041 AD, January 24 . ·granted Tribunician power on his accession, and renewed annually on the 25th of January,
2 CONT
2 CONT 0041 AD, January 24 . ·Imperator for the first time on his accession ·held at his death the name and titles: Tiberius Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus, Pontifex Maximus, Tribuniciae potestatis XIV, Consul V, Imperator XXVII, Pa
2 CONC ter Patriae
2 CONT
2 CONT 0050 AD . ·persuaded to officially adopt Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus, his fourth wifes son by an earlier marriage, as an heir
2 CONT
2 CONT Attributes title Emperor title Emperor , in Roman Empire. 0041-0054 AD .
2 CONT
2 CONT description tall but not slender, with an attractive face, becoming white
