2 CONT * Agrippina the Younger was portrayed by Betty Lou Gerson in the August 31st, 1953 episode of the CBS radio program Crime Classics that was entitled "Your Loving Son, Nero." The episode chronicles the killing of Agrippina by her s
2 CONC on Nero who was portrayed by William Conrad.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Perspectives on Agrippina's personality'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Ancient''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Note that most ancient Roman sources are quite critical of Agrippina the Younger, because she was seen as stepping outside the conservative Roman ideals regarding the roles of women in society.
2 CONT * Tacitus: Critical view, considered her vicious and had a strong disposition against her due to her femininity and influential role in politics. Perhaps the most comprehensive of Ancient sources. Others are Suetonius and Cassius 
2 CONC Dio.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Modern''
2 CONT
2 CONT * (French) Girod, Virginie, Agrippine, sexe, crimes et pouvoir dans la Rome impériale , Paris, Tallandier, 2015, 300 p.
2 CONT * (French) Minaud, Gérard, Les vies de 12 femmes d'empereur romain - Devoirs, Intrigues & Voluptés , Paris, L'Harmattan, 2012, ch. 3, La vie d'Agrippine, femme de Claude, p. 65-96.
2 CONT * E. Groag, A. Stein, L. Petersen – e.a. (edd.), Prosopographia Imperii Romani saeculi I, II et III, Berlin, 1933 –. (PIR2)
2 CONT * Scullard: A critical view of Agrippina, suggesting she was ambitious and unscrupulous and a depraved sexual psychopath. "Agrippina struck down a series of victims; no man or woman was safe if she suspected rivalry or desired the
2 CONC ir wealth."
2 CONT * Ferrero: Sympathetic and understanding, suggesting Agrippina has been judged harshly by history. Suggesting her marriage to Claudius was to a weak emperor who was, because of his hesitations and terrors, a threat to the imperial
2 CONC  authority and government. She saw it her duty to compensate for the innumerable deficiencies of her strange husband through her own intelligence and strength of will. Pages 212ff.; 276ff.
2 CONT * Barrett: A reasonable view, comparing Scullard's criticisms to Ferrero's apologies. (See Barrett, Anthony A., Agrippina: Sex, Power and Politics in the Early Roman Empire, Yale University Press, New Haven, 1996.)
2 CONT * Annelise Freisenbruch "The first ladies of Rome"
2 CONT * Wood, S. "Diva Drusilla Panthea and the Sisters of Caligula American Journal of Archaeology, Vol. 99, No. 3 (July 1995)
2 CONT * Rogers, R. S. The Conspiracy of Agrippina Transactions and Proceedings of the American Philological Association, Vol. 62 (1931)
2 CONT * Godolphin, F. R. B. A Note on the Marriage of Claudius and Agrippina Classical Philology, Vol. 29, No. 2 (April 1934)
2 CONT * Grimm-Samuel, V On the Mushroom That Deified the Emperor Claudius The Classical Quarterly, New Series, Vol. 41, No. 1 (1991)
2 CONT * McDaniel, W. B Bauli the Scene of the Murder of Agrippina The Classical Quarterly, Vol. 4, No. 2 (April 1910)
2 CONT * Salmonson, Jessica Amanda. (1991) The Encyclopedia of Amazons. Paragon House. Pages 4–5.
2 CONT * Donna Hurley, Agrippina the Younger (Wife of Claudius).
