1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''אריסטובולוס השני''' ( 100 - 49 לפנה"ס) היה מלך יהודה וכהן גדול במשך ארבע שנים (63-67 לפנה"ס) והיה טוען לכתר גם אחר כך. בנם הצעיר של מלכי יהודה אלכסנדר ינאי ושלומציון אלכסנדרה.
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aristobulus_II '''Aristobulus II'''] was the Jewish High Priest and King of Judea, 66 BC to 63 BC, from the Hasmonean Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Aristobulus was the younger son of Alexander Jannaeus, King and High Priest, and Alexandra Salome. After the death of Alexander in 76 BC, his widow succeeded to the rule of Judea and installed her elder son Hyrcanus II as High Pri
2 CONC est. When Salome died in 67 BC, Hyrcanus succeeded to the kingship as well. Aristobulus felt he should rule because he was a better leader and fighter than Hyrcanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rebellion'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Hyrcanus shared his mother's religious views, sympathetic to the Pharisees. In contrast to this, Alexander Jannaeus had supported the Saducees.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aristobulus agreed with his father's Sadducean stance and rebelled against his elder brother. Hyrcanus advanced against him at the head of his mercenaries and his followers. The brothers met in battle near Jericho and many of Hyrc
2 CONC anus' soldiers went over to Aristobulus, and thereby gave the latter the victory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hyrcanus took refuge in the citadel of Jerusalem; but the capture of the Temple by Aristobulus compelled Hyrcanus to surrender. A peace was then concluded, according to the terms of which Hyrcanus was to renounce the throne and th
2 CONC e office of high priest, but was to enjoy the revenues of the latter office.
2 CONT
2 CONT This agreement however did not last, as Hyrcanus feared that Aristobulus was planning his death and took refuge with Aretas III, King of the Nabataeans. The Nabataeans advanced toward Jerusalem with an army of 50,000 and besieged 
2 CONC the city for several months.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Roman intervention'''
2 CONT
2 CONT During this civil war, the Roman general Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus defeated the Kingdoms of Pontus and the Seleucids. He sent his deputy Marcus Aemilius Scaurus to take possession of Seleucid Syria.
2 CONT
2 CONT As the Hasmoneans were allies of the Romans, both brothers appealed to Scaurus, each endeavoring by gifts and promises to win him over to his side. Scaurus, moved by a gift of 400 talents, decided in favor of Aristobulus and order
2 CONC ed Aretas to withdraw his army. During his retreat, the Nabateans suffered a crushing defeat at the hands of Aristobulus.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Pompey arrived in Syria in 63 BCE, both brothers and a third party that desired the removal of the entire dynasty, sent their delegates to Pompey, who however delayed the decision. He favoured Hyrcanus II over Aristobulus II,
2 CONC  deeming the elder, weaker brother a more reliable ally of the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aristobulus and his son were captured in 63 BCE. Marc Antony had been commander of the cavalry under Gabinius, consul of the Roman province of Syria. Marc Antony was the man who scaled Aristobulus' fortification and subdued his fo
2 CONC rces with several men. This is the point that Aristobulus II and his son were taken prisoner. However, Aristobulus II escaped in 57 BCE.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aristobulos, suspicious of Pompey, entrenched himself in the fortress of Alexandrium, but when the Romans summoned their army, he surrendered and undertook to deliver Jerusalem over to them. However, since many of his followers ho
2 CONC wever were unwilling to open the gates, the Romans besieged and captured the city by force, badly damaging city and temple. Hyrcanus was restored as High Priest, but deprived of political authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aristobulus was on his way to Judaea with his son Alexander, in 49 BC when "he was taken off by poison given him by those of Pompey's party". His son Alexander was beheaded by the Roman commander Scipio at Antioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Antigonus led a rebellion against Rome 40 BC but was defeated and killed in 37 BC.
2 CONT
