1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ==Helena of Adiabene==
2 CONT '''''' (Hebrew: הלני מלכת חדייב‎) (d. ca. 50–56 CE) was an Ashkenazi-Riphatean[1][2] queen of Adiabene (modern-day Erbil, Iraqi Kurdistan) and Edessa (modern-day Urfa, Turkey) as the sister-wife of Monobaz I, and later the chief w
2 CONC ife of Abgar V, King of Osrhoene. With her husband, Monobaz I, she was the mother of Izates II and Monobaz II. Helena became a convert to Judaism about the year 30 CE.[3] According to Josephus, Helena was the daughter of King Izat
2 CONC es,[4] and according to both Josephus and Moses of Chorene, she was the chief wife of Abgar V king of Edessa.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT 1	Sources of information 2	Biography 3	Jerusalem palace of the Adiabenes 4	References 5	Further reading Sources of information What is known of Helena is based on the writings of Flavius Josephus, Moses of Chorene, Kirakos Ganjake
2 CONC ts, and the Talmud. Josephus, although younger, was almost contemporary with Helena, living in Jerusalem at the time when she lived and was buried there, and he wrote substantial parts of his work from first-hand knowledge. The ea
2 CONC rliest parts of the Talmud, while based on older sources, were compiled and redacted from around the year 200 onward.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Helena of Adiabene was noted for her generosity; during a famine at Jerusalem in 45–46 CE she sent to Alexandria for corn (grain) and to Cyprus for dried figs for distribution among the sufferers from the famine.[7] In t
2 CONC he Talmud, however (Bava Batra 11a), this is laid to the credit of Monobaz I; and though Brüll[8] regards the reference to Monobaz as indicating the dynasty, still Rashi maintains the simpler explanation—that Monobaz himself is me
2 CONC ant. The Talmud speaks also of important presents which the queen gave to the Temple at Jerusalem.[9] "Helena had a golden candlestick made over the door of the Temple," to which statement is added that when the sun rose its rays 
2 CONC were reflected from the candlestick and everybody knew that it was the time for reading the Shema'.[10] She also made a golden plate on which was written the passage of the Pentateuch[11] which the rabbi read when a wife suspected
2 CONC  of infidelity was brought before him.[12] In the Jerusalem Talmud, tractate Yoma iii. 8 the candlestick and the plate are confused.
2 CONT
2 CONT The strictness with which she observed the Jewish law is instanced in the Talmud:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Her son [Izates] having gone to war, Helena made a vow that if he should return safe, she would become a Nazirite for the space of seven years. She fulfilled her vow, and at the end of seven years went to Judah. The Hillelites to
2 CONC ld her that she must observe her vow anew, and she therefore lived as a Nazirite for seven more years. At the end of the second seven years she became ritually impure, and she had to repeat her Naziriteship, thus being a Nazarite 
2 CONC for twenty-one years. Judah bar Ilai, however, said she was a Nazirite for fourteen years only."[13] "Rabbi Judah said: 'The sukkah [erected for the Feast of Tabernacles] of Queen Helena in Lydda was higher than twenty ells. The r
2 CONC abbis used to go in and out and make no remark about it'."[14] Helena moved to Jerusalem, where she is buried in the pyramidal tomb which she had constructed during her lifetime, three stadia north of Jerusalem.[15] The catacombs 
2 CONC are known as "Tombs of the Kings." A sarcophagus bearing two inscriptions was found there, the funerary epigram reading: Ṣaddan Malkata (Palmyrene: צדן מלכתא), and Ṣaddah Malkatah (Aramaic: צדה מלכתה), interpreted by scholars to m
2 CONC ean: "Our mistress, the Queen."[16][17] The sarcophagus was discovered by Louis Felicien de Saulcy in the nineteenth century and later taken to France. It is thought to be that of Queen Helena of Adiabene.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jerusalem palace of the Adiabenes The royal palace of Queen Helena is believed to have been discovered by archaeologist Doron Ben-Ami during excavations in the City of David in 2007.[18][19] According to Josephus, the palace was b
2 CONC uilt by (the otherwise unknown) "Grapte, a kinswoman" of Izates.[20] It was a monumental building located in the City of David just to the south of the Temple Mount and was destroyed by the Romans in 70 CE. The ruins contained dat
2 CONC able coins, stone vessels and pottery as well as remnants of ancient frescoes. The basement level contained a mikveh (ritual bath).[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  In the Targum to Jeremiah li. 27, Ararat, Minni, and Ashkenaz are paraphrased by Kordu, Harmini, and Hadayab, i.e., Corduene, Armenia, and Adiabene; while in Ezekiel xxvii. 23 Harran, Caneh, and Eden are interpreted by the Aramai
2 CONC c translator as "Harwan, Nisibis, and Adiabene."
2 CONT  “‘The descendants of Gomer: Ashkenaz, Riphath, and Togarmah (Genesis 10:3)’: Asia, Adiabene, and Germania. Rabbi Berechya said: ‘Germanica’.” https://www.sefaria.org/Bereishit_Rabbah.37.1?ven=Sefaria_Community_Translation&with=al
