1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT =Drusilla=
2 CONT ==(sister of Caligula)==
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Julia Drusilla''' (Classical Latin: IVLIA•DRVSILLA[1]) (September 16, 16–June 10, 38) was the second daughter and fifth living child of Germanicus and Agrippina the Elder. Drusilla had two sisters (Julia Livilla and the Empress
2 CONC  Agrippina the Younger) and three brothers (Nero, Drusus, and the Emperor Caligula). She was a great-granddaughter of the Emperor Augustus, great-niece and adoptive granddaughter of the Emperor Tiberius, sister of the Emperor Cali
2 CONC gula, niece and sister-in-law of the Emperor Claudius, and maternal aunt of the Emperor Nero.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reputation
2 CONT
2 CONT Drusilla was reportedly her brother's favorite. There are also rumors that she was also his lover. If true, that role likely gained her influence over Caligula. Though the activities between the brother and sister might have been 
2 CONC seen as incest by their contemporaries, it is not known whether the two actually had any sexual relations. Drusilla herself earned a rather poor reputation because of the close bond she shared with Caligula and was even likened to
2 CONC  a prostitute by later scholars, in an attempt to discredit Caligula.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some historians suggest that Caligula was motivated by more than mere lust or love in pursuing relations with his sisters. He might instead have deliberately patterned himself after the Hellenistic monarchs of the Ptolemaic dynast
2 CONC y where marriages between jointly ruling brothers and sisters had become tradition rather than sex scandals. This has also been used to explain why his despotism was apparently more evident to his contemporaries than those of his 
2 CONC predecessors Caesar Augustus and Tiberius.
2 CONT
2 CONT The source of many of the rumors surrounding Caligula and Drusilla may be derived from formal Roman dining habits.[10] It was customary in Patrician households for the host and hostess of a dinner (or in other words, the husband a
2 CONC nd wife in charge of the household) to hold the positions of honor at a banquet at their residence. In the case of a young bachelor being the head of the household (Caligula), the female position of honor was to be held by his sis
2 CONC ters (Agrippina the Younger, Drusilla, and Julia Livilla), taking turns sitting in the place of honor. Caligula apparently broke with this tradition in that rather than having his sisters take turns at the place of honor, the plac
2 CONC e was reserved exclusively for Drusilla. Caligula was thus, in a manner of speaking, publicly proclaiming that Drusilla was his wife, the female head of the household.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death and Aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on June 10, 38, probably of fever which was rampant in Rome at the time. Caligula was said never to have left her side, and after she had died, he would not let anyone take her body away.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caligula never really recovered from the loss. He buried his sister with the honors of an Augusta, acted as a grieving widower, and had the Roman Senate declare her a Goddess as "Diva Drusilla", deifying her as a representation of
2 CONC  the Roman goddess Venus or Greek goddess Aphrodite. Drusilla was consecrated as Panthea, most likely on the anniversary of the birthday of Augustus.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT A year later, Caligula named his only known daughter Julia Drusilla after his late favorite sister. Meanwhile, her widowed husband Marcus Amelius Lepidus reportedly became a lover to her sisters Livilla and Agrippina in an apparen
2 CONC t attempt to gain their support in succeeding Caligula. The political conspiracy was discovered by Caligula while in Germania Superior during the fall. Lepidus was swiftly executed, while Livilla and Agrippina were exiled to the P
2 CONC ontine Islands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drusilla_(sister_of_Caligula)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT JULIA GERUNDA verch TIBERIUS CLAUDIUS II DRUSUS NERO
2 CONT
2 CONT 	BIRTH: 10 A.D. in Lugundum, Gaul - now Lyons, Rhone-Alpes, France
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH:
2 CONT
2 CONT FATHER: Tiberius Claudius II ap DRUSUS NERO - in Lugundum, Gaul - now Lyons, Rhone-Alpes, France
2 CONT
2 CONT MOTHER: Agrippina II verch GERMANICUS in Lugundum, Gaul - now Lyons, France
2 CONT
