2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonia_(idősebb)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Antonia the Elder=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonia Major also known as Julia Antonia Major (Latin: Antonia Maior, PIR2 A 884) (born August / September 39 BC), also known as Antonia the Elder, was a daughter of Triumvir Mark Antony and Octavia the Younger and a relative of 
2 CONC the first Roman emperors of the Julio-Claudian dynasty. She was a niece of the first emperor Augustus, step cousin of the emperor Tiberius, paternal great-aunt of the emperor Caligula, maternal aunt and great-aunt-in law of the em
2 CONC peror Claudius, and paternal grandmother and maternal great-great aunt of the emperor Nero.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonia was born in Athens, Greece and after 36 BC her mother, along with her siblings and herself were brought to Rome. She was raised by her mother, her uncle and her aunt Livia Drusilla. According to Cassius Dio after her fathe
2 CONC r died, Augustus allowed her and her younger sister Antonia Minor to benefit from their father's estate in Rome. Although little is known of her, Antonia was held in high regard like her sister Antonia Minor, the mother of the emp
2 CONC eror Claudius, who was celebrated for her beauty and virtue.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Progeny'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 22 BC Antonia married the consul, Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus. Antonia bore Lucius three children:
2 CONT * Domitia Lepida the Elder - ancient sources refer to her as Domitia. She married the consul Decimus Haterius Agrippa and bore him a son Quintus Haterius Antoninus. Domitia later married Gaius Sallustius Crispus Passienus, consul 
2 CONC suffect in 27, proconsul of Asia and consul in AD 44.
2 CONT * Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (PIR2 d. AD 127) - consul in AD 32, he married his cousin Germanicus' daughter Agrippina the Younger in 28. Agrippina and Domitius were the parents of the emperor Nero. He was accused by Tiberius, but
2 CONC  saved by that emperor's death (Suet. Nero 5) and lived a few years longer under Caligula's reign until he died in AD 40.
2 CONT * Domitia Lepida the Younger (PIR2 d. AD 180) - she first married her cousin, the consul Marcus Valerius Messalla Barbatus to whom she bore a daughter, the empress Valeria Messalina, third wife of the emperor Claudius. After the d
2 CONC eath of her first husband, she married Faustus Cornelius Sulla, cos. suff. in AD 31 and gave him a son, Faustus Cornelius Sulla Felix (who would become consul in AD 52). At the beginning of Claudius' reign, she married Appius Juni
2 CONC us Silanus, cos. in AD 28, who was put to death in AD 42. She outlived her daughter, Messalina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many scholars think the Ara Pacis (an altar from the Augustan Era), displays Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus and his elder sister Domitia. The woman behind Domitia and Domitius is allegedly their mother Antonia Major and the man next 
2 CONC to Antonia Major is her husband Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaius Stern and Sir Ronald Syme both dispute this claim, whose chief argument in its favor is that "it was written in German 100 years ago". First of all, the young Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus was born after the monument was compl
2 CONC eted. He cannot possibly be on the Ara Pacis. His father was governor of Africa in 13 and was not in Rome for the Ara Pacis ceremony. Additional arguments against can be found in Stern, "Nero's Father and Other Romantic Figures on
2 CONC  the Ara Pacis Augustae, CAMWS 2003; Syme, "Neglected Children on the Ara Pacis," AJA 88 (1984), The Augustan Aristocracy (1985) 142, 155, 166-67. An image can be seen at.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a possibility that Antonia may have died before AD 25. From observations of Syme (Sen. Rh. Controv. 9.4.18) indicates that Antonia was alive after 33.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonia_the_Elder
