2 CONT Tiberius Claudius Nero, often known as Tiberius Nero and Nero[1] (85–33 BC) was a politician who lived in the last century of the Roman Republic. He was the natural father of the second Roman emperor Tiberius, who became the steps
2 CONC on of the emperor Augustus and was adopted by Augustus as his heir. Nero was a member of the republican Claudian family of Rome.[2] He was a descendant of the first named Tiberius Claudius Nero, a consul and great-grandson of Appi
2 CONC us Claudius Caecus (censor in 312 BC).[3] Nero was the son of Drusus[4] and his mother was a descendant of the Claudian family. His father Drusus served under Pompey in 67 BC, battling the pirate menace. Drusus was famous for reco
2 CONC mmending that the members of the Second Catilinarian Conspiracy be confined.[5] Nero had a sister called Claudia, who married the prefect Quintus Volusius Nero had served as a quaestor to Julius Caesar in 48 BC, commanding his fle
2 CONC et in the Alexandrian War. Having achieved victory over the Egyptian navy, he was rewarded with a priesthood.[8] Julius Caesar had sent Nero to create Roman colonies in Gaul and in other provinces.[9] Despite his service with Juli
2 CONC us Caesar, Nero was an Optimate at heart. After the murder of Julius Caesar in 44 BC, when it seemed that the assassins were triumphant, he suggested that they be rewarded for their services to the state. However, due to his previ
2 CONC ous alliance with the Roman dictator, Nero was allowed to be elected praetor in 42 BC.[10] Around this time Nero married his relative Livia Drusilla, whose father Marcus Livius Drusus Claudianus was from the same gens. His son of 
2 CONC the same name was born November 16, 42 BC, at Fondi Italy. Shortly afterwards, the Second Triumvirate began to break down, causing a dangerous situation in Rome as the triumvirs went to battle with each other. Nero was forced to c
2 CONC hoose sides and in his distrust of Octavian, he cast his lot with Mark Antony. In 41 BC, he fled Rome with Livia and Tiberius in tow, joining Antony's brother Lucius in Perusia. Perusia was besieged by Octavian's men by the time N
2 CONC ero arrived, and when the town fell in 40 BC, he was forced to flee first to Praeneste, and then Naples.[11] In 40 BC, Octavian and Mark Antony finally reconciled. Nero died in 33 BC. After his death, his sons went to live with th
2 CONC eir mother and stepfather. The younger Tiberius aged 9, delivered his funeral eulogy on the Rostra in Rome.[16] When the future Roman emperor Tiberius celebrated his coming of age, Tiberius staged two gladiatorial contests. One wa
2 CONC s held at the Forum in memory of his father and the other held at the amphitheatre in memory of his grandfather Drusus.
