2 CONT Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo (c. 7 - 67 A.D.) Corbulo's early career is unknown but he was consul in 39 during the reign of Caligula, his brother-in-law through Caligula's marriage to Corbulo's half-sister Milonia Caesonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Descent'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Corbulo was born in Italy into a senatorial family. His father (who entered the senate as a formal praetor under Tiberius) had the same name and his mother was named Vistilia, who came from a family who held the praetorship.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Military & Political Career'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Reign of Caligula'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The early career of Corbulo is unknown but he was consul in 39 during the reign of Caligula, his brother-in-law through Caligula's marriage to Corbulo's half-sister Milonia Caesonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In Germania Inferior'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After Caligula's assassination, Corbulo's career went into a halt until, in 47, when the new Emperor Claudius made him commander of the Germania Inferior armies, with base camp in Cologne.
2 CONT
2 CONT The new assignment was a difficult one and Corbulo had to deal with major rebellions and violence outbreaks coming from Cherusci and Chauci Germanic tribes. During his stay in Germania, the general ordered the construction of a ca
2 CONC nal between the rivers Rhine and Meuse [1]. Parts of this engineering work, known as Fossa Corbulonis or Corbulo's Canal, have been found at archeological digs. Its course is about identical to the Vliet, which connects the modern
2 CONC  towns of Leiden (ancient Matilo) and Voorburg (Forum Hadriani).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In the east.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Corbulo returned to Rome, where he stayed until 52, when he was named governor of the province of Asia. Following Claudius' death in 54, the new emperor Nero sent him to the eastern provinces to deal with the Armenian question. Af
2 CONC ter some delay, he took the offensive in 58, and, reinforced by troops from Germany, attacked Tiridates, king of Armenia and brother of Vologases I of Parthia. Artaxata and Tigranocerta were captured by his legions (III Gallica, V
2 CONC I Ferrata, and X Fretensis), and Tigranes, who had been brought up in Rome and was the obedient servant of the government, was installed king of Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 61 Tigranes invaded Adiabene, an integral portion of the Parthian Kingdom, and a conflict between Rome and Parthia seemed unavoidable. Vologases, however, thought it better to come to terms. It was agreed that both the Roman an
2 CONC d Parthian troops should evacuate Armenia, that Tigranes should be dethroned, and the position of Tiridates recognized. The Roman government declined to accede to these arrangements, and Lucius Caesennius Paetus, governor of Cappa
2 CONC docia, was ordered to settle the question by bringing Armenia under direct Roman administration.
2 CONT
2 CONT The protection of Syria in the meantime claimed all of Corbulo's attention. Lucius Caesannius Paetus, a weak and incapable commander, who "despised the fame acquired by Corbulo (2), suffered a severe defeat at Rhandeia (62), where
2 CONC  he was surrounded and forced to capitulate to the Parthians and evacuate to Armenia. The command of the troops was again entrusted to Corbulo. In 63 AD, with a strong army, he crossed the Euphrates, but Tiridates declined to give
2 CONC  battle and arranged a peace. At Rhandea he laid down his diadem at the foot of the emperor's statue, promising not to resume it until he received it from the hand of Nero himself in Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Fall and death'''
2 CONT
2 CONT After two failed plots by noblemen and senators, including Corbulo's son-in-law Roman Senator Lucius Annius Vinicianus, to overthrow Nero in 62, he became suspicious of Corbulo and his support among the Roman masses. In 67 disturb
2 CONC ances broke out in Judaea and Nero, ordering Vespasian to take command of the Roman forces, summoned Corbulo, as well as two brothers who were the governors of Upper and Lower Germany, to Greece. On his arrival at Cenchreae, the p
2 CONC ort of Corinth, messengers from Nero met Corbulo, and ordered him to commit suicide, which he loyally obeyed by falling on his own sword, saying, "Axios!". 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Works'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Corbulo wrote an account of his Asiatic experiences, which is now lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and issue'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Corbulo married Cassia Longina, a Roman woman from a senatorial family, daughter of Gaius Cassius Longinus and wife Junia Lepida. Cassia bore Corbulo two daughters. The elder daughter Domitia Corbula married the senator Lucius Ann
2 CONC ius Vinicianus and their second daughter Domitia Longina became a Roman Empress and married the future Roman Emperor Domitian. Through Junia Lepida, a great-great granddaughter of Augustus, both of Cassia's daughters by Corbulo we
2 CONC re direct descendants of the first Roman emperor and thus, the surviving members of the Imperial Julio-Claudian family.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In popular culture'''
2 CONT
2 CONT     The 2012 live-action video web series Forward Unto Dawn takes place in the fictional Corbulo Academy of Military Science,[4] which is named after General Corbulo. The academy's motto is Axios!, which Corbulo said before killin
2 CONC g himself.
2 CONT     The 2012 historical novel, "Avenger of Rome", by Douglas Jackson, deals with the fictional last battle of Corbulo.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Notes''
2 CONT
2 CONT     Tacitus Annales XI 20.
2 CONT     Goldsworthy, Adrian (2003). In the Name of Rome: The men who won the Roman Empire. Great Britain: Orion Publishing Group. p. 268. ISBN 0-297-84666-3.
2 CONT     "The game of death in ancient Rome: arena sport and political suicide"
2 CONT     "Halopedia: Corbulo Academy of Military Science"
2 CONT
