1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scribonia_(wife_of_Octavian)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Scribonia''' (68 BC - AD 16) was the second wife of the Roman Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Augustus-Roman-Emperor/6000000001336717255 Augustus] and the mother of his only natural child, [http://www.geni.com/people/Julia-
2 CONC Caesaris-Major/6000000007234813810 Julia the Elder]. She was the mother-in-law of the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Tiberius-Roman-Emperor/6000000008122648505 Tiberius], great-grandmother of the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/Caligula-Emperor-of-the-Roman-Empire/6000000006101240586 Caligula] and Empress [http://www.geni.com/people/Julia-Agrippina-Minor-of-Rome/4994272588560101934 Agrippina the Younger], grandmother-in-law of the Emperor [http://
2 CONC www.geni.com/people/Claudius-I-Roman-Emperor/6000000002119140027 Claudius], and great-great grandmother of the Emperor [http://www.geni.com/people/Nero-Roman-Emperor/6000000000424698537 Nero].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life===
2 CONT
2 CONT Scribonia was the daughter of a Lucius Scribonius Libo, probably the praetor of that name of 80 BC. Her brother of the same name was consul and died in 34 BC. The name of her mother was Sentia. According to Suetonius, Scribonia's 
2 CONC first two marriages were to former consuls. Her first husband is unknown, although it had been suggested that he was Gnaeus Cornelius Lentulus Marcellinus (consul 56 BC), as there is an inscription that refers to freedmen (post 39
2 CONC  BC) of Scribonia and her son Cornelius Marcellinus, indicating that she had a son from her previous marriage and that he was living with her after she divorced her third husband. He may have died young and ignored by historians. 
2 CONC Her second husband perhaps was Publius Cornelius Scipio Salvito, a supporter of Pompey. They had a daughter Cornelia Scipio who married the censor Lucius Aemilius Paullus. Scribonia may have also been the mother to Publius Corneli
2 CONC us Scipio, consul in 16 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 40 BC Scribonia was forced to divorce her husband and marry Octavian, who was younger than she was by several years. Octavian in turn divorced his wife Clodia Pulchra, marrying Scribonia to cement a political alliance with her 
2 CONC niece Scribonia's husband Sextus Pompey. Their daughter Julia the Elder was born in 39 BC, probably in October, and on that very same day Octavian divorced her. Their marriage had not been a happy one; Octavian felt she nagged him
2 CONC  too much. She never remarried. Cassius Dio and Marcus Velleius Paterculus says that when her youngest child, Julia, was sent into exile for adultery and treason, she requested that she be allowed to accompany her.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Emperor Tiberius came into power, he separated Scribonia from her daughter, and allegedly starved Julia to death. When Scribonia died is unknown. It is mainly placed two years after Julia and Augustus. In Seneca, she is menti
2 CONC oned as being alive and in full possession of her wits as late as the end of 16 when she tried to convince her nephew Marcus Scribonius Libo not to commit suicide and face his punishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scribonia's image as a shrew is probably the product of propaganda to divert the potentially scandalous circumstances of her divorce from Augustus. Seneca describes her as a gravis femina; gravis meaning “dignified” and “severe”. 
2 CONC Modern scholars are divided on her character; while some describe her as "tiresome" and "morose" most others view her as an ideal example of a Roman matron as she clearly had the "composure" and "calmness" to look after depressed 
2 CONC and suicidal characters such as her daughter and nephew. Sextus Propertius praises her motherhood referring to her as "sweet mother Scribonia" in Cornelia Scipio's funeral elegy in 16 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
