1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucilla
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Lucilla=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Annia Aurelia Galeria Lucilla or Lucilla (March 7, 148 or 150-182) was the second daughter and third child of Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius and Roman Empress Faustina the Younger and an elder sister to future Roman Emperor Commodu
2 CONC s. Commodus ordered Lucilla's execution after a failed assassination and coup attempt when she was about 33 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Born and raised in Rome into an influential political family, Lucilla was a younger twin with her elder brother Gemellus Lucillae, who died around 150. Lucilla’s maternal grandparents were Roman Emperor Antoninus Pius and Roman Em
2 CONC press Faustina the Elder and her paternal grandparents were Domitia Lucilla and praetor Marcus Annius Verus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriages and ascension to Empress'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 161, when she was between 11 and 13 years old, Lucilla's father arranged a marriage for her with his co-ruler Lucius Verus. Verus, 18 years her senior, became her husband three years later in Ephesus in 164. At this marriage sh
2 CONC e received her title of Augusta and became a Roman Empress. At the same time, Marcus Aurelius and Lucius Verus were fighting a Parthian war in Syria.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucilla and Lucius Verus had three children:
2 CONT * Aurelia Lucilla was born in 165 in Antioch
2 CONT * Lucilla Plautia
2 CONT * Lucius Verus
2 CONT
2 CONT Aurelia and the boy died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucilla was an influential and respectable woman and she enjoyed her status. She spent much time in Rome, while Verus was away from Rome much of the time, fulfilling his duties as a co-ruler. Lucius Verus died around 168/169 while
2 CONC  returning from the war theater in the Danube region, and as a result, Lucilla lost her status as Empress.
2 CONT
2 CONT As an unattached link to Emperor Aurelius and to the late co-Emperor Verus and because of her royal-born offspring, Lucilla was not destined for a long widowhood, and thus, a short time later, in 169, her father arranged a second 
2 CONC marriage for her with Tiberius Claudius Pompeianus Quintianus from Antioch. He was a Syrian Roman who was twice consul and a political ally to her father, but Lucilla and her mother were against the marriage as a less than ideal m
2 CONC atch, partly because Quintianus was at least twice Lucilla's age, but also because he was not of her own Roman nobilis social rank though he was descended from rulers in the East. They married nonetheless and, about a year later, 
2 CONC in 170, Lucilla bore him a son named Pompeianus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rise of Commodus'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 172, Lucilla and Quintianus accompanied Marcus Aurelius to Vindobona (now Vienna) in support of the Danube military campaign and were with him in March 17, 180, when Aurelius died and Commodus became the new emperor. The change
2 CONC  meant that any hope of Lucilla becoming Empress again was lost and she and Quintianus returned to Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucilla was not happy living the quiet life of a private citizen in Rome, and hated her sister-in-law Bruttia Crispina. Over time, Lucilla became very concerned with her brother Commodus' erratic behaviour and its resulting effect
2 CONC  on the stability of the empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Plot to assassinate Commodus'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In light of her brother's unstable rule, in 182 Lucilla became involved in a plot to assassinate Commodus and replace him with her husband and herself as the new rulers of Rome. Her co-conspirators included Tarrutenius Paternus, t
2 CONC he head of the Imperial Guard, her daughter Plautia from her first marriage, a nephew of Quintianus also called Quintianus, and her paternal cousins, the former consul Marcus Ummidius Quadratus Annianus and his sister Ummidia Corn
2 CONC ificia Faustina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Quintianus’ nephew, brandishing a dagger or sword, bungled the assassination attempt trying to kill Commodus. As he burst forth from his hiding place to commit the deed, he boasted to Commodus "Here is what the Senate sends to you
2 CONC ", giving away his intentions before he had the chance to act. Commodus's guards were faster than Quintianus and the would-be assassin was overpowered and disarmed without injuring the emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Commodus ordered the deaths of Quintianus’ nephew and of Marcus Ummidius Quadratus Annianus, and banished Lucilla, her daughter and Ummidia Cornificia Faustina to the Italian island of Capri. He sent a centurion there to execute t
2 CONC hem later that year. Her son Pompeianus was later murdered by Caracalla.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In popular culture'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * In the 1964 film The Fall of the Roman Empire, Lucilla was played by Sophia Loren.
2 CONT * In the 2000 film Gladiator, Lucilla was played by Connie Nielsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucilla
