2 CONT =Lucius Marcius Philippus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Marcius Philippus was a Roman politician who was elected suffect consul in 38 BC. He was step-brother to the future emperor Augustus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the plebeian branch of the Marcia family, Philippus was the son of Lucius Marcius Philippus, the consul of 56 BC. By 50 BC, he had possibly become an Augur, one of the priests of ancient Rome. In 49 BC he was elected a
2 CONC s Plebeian Tribune, where he vetoed the proposal to send Faustus Sulla, Pompey’s son-in-law, as propraetor to Mauretania, to persuade kings Bocchus II and Bogud to side with Pompey and abandon Julius Caesar. In 44 BC he was electe
2 CONC d praetor, and although he was granted a province to administer after his term had finished, he refused to accept the validity of the allotment of provinces agreed to in a Senate meeting of November 28, 44 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his father’s marriage to Atia Balba Caesonia, he became step-brother to Octavianus, Julius Caesar’s heir. His father used his influence to help Philippus to obtain the consulate as one of the suffect consuls of 38 BC; neverth
2 CONC eless, during his consulate Philippus did not declare himself openly for his step-brother in Octavianus’s rivalry with Mark Antony. By 35 BC, he was appointed the proconsular governor of one of the two provinces of Hispania. After
2 CONC  serving there for two years, he returned to Rome, where he was awarded a triumph which he celebrated on April 27, 33 BC for his actions while governor. With the spoils of his victories, he restored the temple of Hercules and the 
2 CONC Muses in the Circus Flaminius.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippus did not appear to have any living sons to succeed him. Philippus married Atia, daughter of Julia Caesaris and Marcus Atius Balbus and maternal aunt of Augustus. She bore him a daughter Marcia, who later married Paullus F
2 CONC abius Maximus. Marcia had one son and possibly one daughter: Paullus Fabius Persicus and Fabia Numantina, who may have been the daughter of Maximus' brother Africanus Fabius Maximus.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Marcius_Philippus_(consul_38_BC)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the plebeian branch of the Marcia family, Philippus was the son of Lucius Marcius Philippus, the consul of 56 BC. By 50 BC, he had possibly become an Augur, one of the priests of ancient Rome.[1] In 49 BC he was electe
2 CONC d as Plebeian Tribune, where he vetoed the proposal to send Faustus Sulla, Pompey’s son-in-law, as propraetor to Mauretania, to persuade kings Bocchus II and Bogud to side with Pompey and abandon Julius Caesar.[2] In 44 BC he was 
2 CONC elected praetor, and although he was granted a province to administer after his term had finished, he refused to accept the validity of the allotment of provinces agreed to in a Senate meeting of November 28, 44 BC.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT With his father’s marriage to Atia Balba Caesonia, he became step-brother to Octavianus, Julius Caesar’s heir. His father used his influence to help Philippus to obtain the consulate as one of the suffect consuls of 38 BC; neverth
2 CONC eless, during his consulate Philippus did not declare himself openly for his step-brother in Octavianus’s rivalry with Mark Antony.[4] By 35 BC, he was appointed the proconsular governor of one of the two provinces of Hispania.[5]
2 CONC  After serving there for two years, he returned to Rome, where he was awarded a triumph which he celebrated on April 27, 33 BC for his actions while governor.[6] With the spoils of his victories, he restored the temple of Hercules
2 CONC  and the Muses in the Circus Flaminius.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippus did not appear to have any living sons to succeed him.[8] Philippus married Atia, daughter of Julia Minor and Marcus Atius Balbus and maternal aunt of Augustus.[9] She bore him a daughter Marcia, who later married Paullu
2 CONC s Fabius Maximus. Marcia had one son and possibly one daughter: Paullus Fabius Persicus and Fabia Numantina, who may have been the daughter of Maximus' brother Africanus Fabius Maximus.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Marcius_Philippus_(consul_38_BC)]
