1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Lucius Scribonius Libo==
2 CONT '''From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia''': [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Scribonius_Libo]
2 CONT
2 CONT Several men of plebeian status were named Lucius Scribonius Libo during the Roman Republic and Roman Empire; they were members of the gens Scribonia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''L. Scribonius Libo (praetor 204 BC)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo was a tribune of the plebs in 216 BC, during the Second Punic War. A question arose pertaining to the ransoming of Roman captives; he referred the matter to the Senate.[1] He was one of the three men appoint
2 CONC ed triumviri mensarii, a commission created by a Lex Minucia, possibly to deal with a shortage of silver;[2] the full range of their financial activities is unclear.[3] He was praetor peregrinus in 204 and sent to Cisalpine Gaul.[
2 CONC 4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''L. Scribonius Libo (tribune 149 BC)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo (tribune of the plebs 149 BC) was a member of a Roman Senatorial family. He accused Servius Sulpicius Galba for the outrages against the Lusitanians. He might have been the Scribonius who consecrated the Put
2 CONC eal Scribonianum often mentioned by ancient writers, which was located in the forum close to the Arcus Fabianus. It was called Puteal as it was opened at the top, like a well. Years later it would be repaired and dedicated by anot
2 CONC her Libo, praetor of 80 BC.[5]
2 CONT L. Scribonius Libo (praetor 80 BC)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo (fl. 1st century BC) was a member of a Roman Senatorial family, and held the office of praetor urbanus in 80 BC.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is known to have had at least one son of the same name, who became consul in 34 BC. He is also cited as the most likely candidate for the father of Scribonia, second wife of Augustus and mother of his only biological child, Jul
2 CONC ia the Elder. If so, then he would have married at least once to a woman of the Sentia gens, whose ancestors had been directors of the mint.[7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 62 BC Scribonius was made monetalis during which he repaired the Puteal Scribonianum and issued coins to commemorate the event.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''L. Scribonius Libo (consul 34 BC)'''
2 CONT Main article: Lucius Scribonius Libo (consul 34 BC)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo (fl. 1st century BC) was the son of the above, and possibly the elder brother or half-brother of Scribonia, first wife of Augustus. His wife was a member of the gens Sulpicia.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the civil war broke in 49 BC he sided with Pompey and was in command of Etruria. Afterward he accompanied Pompey to Greece. Following the death of Bibulus he was given command of the Pompeian fleet. During the civil wars that
2 CONC  occurred after the assassination of Julius Caesar, he sided with his son-in-law Sextus Pompey who married his daughter Scribonia. In 40 BC Octavian, married his sister and she bore him his only natural child Julia. After this mar
2 CONC riage a peace was made between the Triumvirs (second triumvirate) and Sextus with the Pact of Misenum in 39 BC. After the war was renewed in 36 BC, Scribonius felt the cause was lost and abandoned Sextus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 34 BC he was consul with Mark Antony.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''L. Scribonius Libo (consul 16)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo (died 16) was a consul in 16. This nobleman was brother of Marcus Scribonius Libo Drusus, who was accused of planning to revolt against the Roman emperor Tiberius, who also charged Libo in planning a revolt 
2 CONC against the emperor and stabbed him to death in 16. They were likely the sons or paternal grandsons of Marcus Livius Drusus Libo (adopted brother of empress Livia). It is possible that he was Marcus Livius nephew whom was adopted.
2 CONC [10] 
2 CONT
