1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Salonius_Matidius_Patruinus '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gaius Saloninus (or Salonius) Matidius Patruinus''' (died 78) was a Roman Senator that lived in the Roman Empire during the 1st century during the reign of Vespasian (r. 69-79).
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=
2 CONT
2 CONT Patruinus came from a wealthy family of Praetorian rank from Vicetia (modern Vicenza, northern Italy). Around 63, he married a noble Spanish Roman woman called Ulpia Marciana, the eldest sister of the future Emperor Trajan. On 4 J
2 CONC uly 68, Marciana bore him a daughter and their only child Salonina Matidia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as a praetor and, through this position, became a senator. In 70/71, Patruinus possibly served as governor of Germania Superior. Upon his death, in 78, in Rome, Patruinus was a priest and served as a member of the Arval 
2 CONC Brethren. After that, Marciana and Matidia went to live with Trajan and his wife Pompeia Plotina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Patruinus was the late maternal grandfather to Matidia’s daughters from her three marriages: Roman Empress Vibia Sabina, Hadrian's wife, and the nobles Matidia Minor (or Mindia Matidia) and Rupilia Faustina.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Italian village of Matigge (ancient Matidiae) is named after him and his second granddaughter Matidia Minor. The Roman Emperors Marcus Aurelius and his successor Commodus are among his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * "Ulpia Marciana". livius.org. Retrieved 11 January 2015.
2 CONT * Bennett, Julian (1997). Trajan: Optimus Princeps: A Life and Times. London: Routledge. ISBN 978-0-415-16524-2.
2 CONT * Jones, Brian W. (1992). The Emperor Domitian. London: Routledge. ISBN 978-0-415-04229-1.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Gaius Salonius Matidius Patruinus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaius Saloninus (or Salonius) Matidius Patruinus (died 78) was a Roman Senator that lived in the Roman Empire during the 1st century during the reign of Vespasian (r. 69-79).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''History'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Patruinus came from a wealthy family of Praetorian rank from Vicetia (modern Vicenza, northern Italy). Around 63, he married a noble Spanish Roman woman called Ulpia Marciana, the eldest sister of the future Emperor Trajan. On 4 J
2 CONC uly 68, Marciana bore him a daughter and their only child Salonina Matidia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as a praetor and, through this position, became a senator. In 70/71, Patruinus possibly served as governor of Germania Superior. Upon his death, in 78, in Rome, Patruinus was a priest and served as a member of the Arval 
2 CONC Brethren. After that, Marciana and Matidia went to live with Trajan and his wife Pompeia Plotina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Patruinus was the late maternal grandfather to Matidia’s daughters from her three marriages: Roman Empress Vibia Sabina, Hadrian's wife, and the nobles Matidia Minor (or Mindia Matidia) and Rupilia Faustina.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Italian village of Matigge (ancient Matidiae) is named after him and his second granddaughter Matidia Minor. The Roman Emperors Marcus Aurelius and his successor Commodus are among his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
