2 CONT =Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum=
2 CONT '''Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum''' (died 141 BC) was a Roman statesman and member of the gens Cornelia. Corculum was the son of '''Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica''' (himself consul in 191 BC), and was thus a first cou
2 CONC sin once removed of the Roman general '''Scipio Africanus'''. His mother is unknown, but is given by some sources as '''Caecilia Metella''', possibly daughter or other relative of '''Quintus Caecilius Metellus''' (consul 206 BC) w
2 CONC hose descendants dominated the consulships in the Late Republic.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT '''Corculum''' was married to his second cousin '''Cornelia Africana Major,''' eldest daughter of '''Scipio Africanus''', with whom he was father of '''Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio''' (who became consul in 138 BC and wh
2 CONC o succeeded his father as Pontifex Maximus in 141 BC). No other children are known, nor is the date of the marriage or Cornelia's death. However, the marriage apparently took place around 184-183 BC, in the lifetime of her father 
2 CONC Scipio Africanus.[2] This marriage between second cousins was possibly one of the first marriages that took place within a Roman gens.[3][4][verification needed] His son '''Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio''' became consul 
2 CONC in 138 BC; his grandson '''Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica (IV)''' also became consul. The last consul in this line was his great-great-grandson '''Metellus Scipio''' (father-in-law of '''Pompey the Great'''). The last living desc
2 CONC endant in the direct line was '''Cornelia Metella''', widow of '''Pompey the Great'''. Other descendants, if any, are unknown. [edit]See also
2 CONT
2 CONT *Scipio-Paullus-Gracchus family tree: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scipio-Paullus-Gracchus_family_tree
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum (died 141 BC) was a Roman statesman and member of the gens Cornelia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Corculum was the son of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica (himself consul in 191 BC), and was thus a first cousin once removed of the Roman general Scipio Africanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 168 BC Corculum fought under Africanus's brother-in-law Lucius Aemilius Paulus Macedonicus in Macedon. He commanded one of the wings, as described by Livy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Corculum became consul for the first time in 162 BC but he and his colleague Gaius Marcius Figulus abdicated when something went wrong with the auspices.
2 CONT
2 CONT Corculum was elected censor in 159 BC with Marcus Popillius Laenas despite his abortive consulship. During his censorship he decreed that no statues of public officials may be erected on the forum without public approval of the Se
2 CONC nate or the people.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his second consulship in 155 BC, Corculum defeated the Dalmatians and was granted a triumph.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became pontifex maximus in 150 BC (after a two-year interregnum after the death of the previous pontiff Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (consul 187 and 175 BC) and princeps senatus in 147 BC. (Lepidus had served as both during his life
2 CONC time; Scipio Africanus had been chosen princeps senatus but was never a pontifex maximus).
2 CONT
2 CONT During the period 159-149 BC, Corculum was a political opponent of Marcus Porcius Cato and pleaded that Rome not destroy Carthage. According to Plutarch's conjecture and Appian's later definite assertion, that was because Corculum
2 CONC  feared that the destruction of Rome's main rival would lead to the decline of Roman morals and discipline. When Cato insisted "Carthago delenda est" (Carthage must be destroyed), Corculum responded that Carthage must be saved. Ho
2 CONC wever, even as princeps senatus, Corculum lost political influence when Carthage plunged into war with Massinissa of Numidia, a war which was against the provisions of the fifty-year treaty signed that Rome considered permanent (b
2 CONC ut that Greek jurists considered had expired). The reality was that Rome wanted to destroy Carthage, seeing how rapidly the city had rebounded from severe fines and years of warfare, and thus encouraged the Numidians to repeatedly
2 CONC  attack Carthaginian territory. Nevertheless, Scipio Corculum was discredited as a champion of Carthage and his political opponent Cato was triumphant. Corculum lived through the Third Punic War (ended by his kinsman Scipio Aemili
2 CONC anus); Cato lived to see that war being declared.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Corculum was succeeded as pontifex maximus in 141 BC by his own son, it is reasonable to assume he died that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Corculum was married to his second cousin Cornelia Africana Major, eldest daughter of Scipio Africanus, with whom he was father of Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio (who became consul in 138 BC and who succeeded his father a
2 CONC s Pontifex Maximus in 141 BC). No other children are known, nor is the date of the marriage or Cornelia's death. However, the marriage apparently took place around 184-183 BC, in the lifetime of her father Scipio Africanus.This ma
2 CONC rriage between second cousins was the first known marriage that took place within a Roman gens.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Serapio became consul in 138 BC; his grandson Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica (IV) also became consul. The last consul in this line was his great-great-grandson Metellus Scipio (father-in-la
2 CONC w of Pompey the Great). The last living descendant in the direct line was Cornelia Metella, widow of Pompey the Great. Other descendants, if any, are unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Publius_Cornelius_Scipio_Nasica_Corculum
