2 CONC rontcover]
2 CONT * Uberto Decembrio. Four Books on the Commonwealth: De re publica libri IV. Paolo Ponzù Donato, ed. & trans. Boston, MA | Leiden, Netherlands: Brill, 2020. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Uberto_Decembrio_Four_Books_on_the_C
2 CONC ommo/p8y8DwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Cornelianus+Scipio+Nasica&pg=PA277&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT * Plutarchus. Plutarch’s Lives, Vol IV. A. H. Clough trans., William W. Goodwin ed. New York: Little, Brown, & Co., 1909. [https://archive.org/details/plutarchslives04ahcl/page/n9/mode/2up?q=Metellus]
2 CONT * Harris, Robert. Imperium Roman. München: Penguin Random House Deutschland, 2006. ISBN 978-3-641-10832-8. Note: The following German and English translation can be found at this URL: [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Imperium
2 CONC /TqJbhtmkuegC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Cornelianus+Scipio+Nasica&pg=PT79&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Nachdem Cato geganen war, sagte Cicero zu mir: “Der junge Mann ist so begierig darauf, jedem seine Prinzipientreue vor Augen zu führen, wie ein Säufer auf eine Kneipenschlägerei aus ist.” Dennoch war Cato einverstanden gewesen, 
2 CONC dass Cicero sich in seinem Namen an Scipio wante. Ich sah Cicero an, das die Gelegenheit, den Aristokraten persönlich unter die Lupe nehmen zu können, ganz nach seinem Geschmack war. Es gab buchstäblich keinen Mann in Rom, dessen 
2 CONC Stammbaum imposanter war als der des Quintus Caecilius Mettelu Pius Cornelianus Scipio Nasica – Nasica bedeutet “Spitznase”, die er im Übrigen sehr hoch trug. Er war nicht nur der leibliche Sohn eines Scipio, sonder nauch der Adop
2 CONC tivsohn von Metellus Pius, dem nominellen Oberhaupt ddes Geschlechts dder Metelli. Vater und Adoptivsohn waren erst kürzlich aus Spanien zurückgekehrt und hielten sich derzeit auf Pius’ riesigem Landgut in Tibur auf. Man erwartete
2 CONC , das sie bei Pompeius’ Triumph am neunundzwanzigsten. Dezember direct hinter dem General in die Stadt reiten würden. Cicero beschloss, ein Treffen für den dreißigsten zu vereinbaren.''
2 CONT
2 CONT After Cato left, Cicero said to me, "The young man is as eager to demonstrate his principles to everyone as a drunkard is to a bar fight." Nevertheless, Cato had agreed that Cicero wanted to approach Scipio on his behalf. I looked
2 CONC  at Cicero, feeling that the opportunity to scrutinize the aristocrat in person was to his liking. There was literally no man in Rome whose pedigree was more imposing than that of Quintus Caecilius Mettelus Pius Cornelianus Scipio
2 CONC  Nasica - Nasica means "pointed nose", which incidentally he carried very high. He was not only the biological son of a Scipio, but also the adopted son of Metellus Pius, the nominal head of the family of the Metelli. The father a
2 CONC nd adopted son had recently returned from Spain and were currently staying at Pius' vast estate in Tibur. It was expected at Pompey's triumph on the twenty-ninth. December would ride right behind the general into town. Cicero deci
2 CONC ded to set a meeting for the thirtieth.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In the above work''', “Metellus” frequently is the shortened name used. See: [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Imperium/TqJbhtmkuegC?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=Metellus]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Stevenson, Tom. Julius Caesar and the Transformation of the Roman Republic. New York, Routledge, 2015. See subheading “Pompey’s Sole Consulship (52 BCE)” [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Julius_Caesar_and_the_Transformation
2 CONC _of/0xkhBQAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Scipio&pg=PT267&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT * Gagarin, Michael and Elain Fanthan, eds. The Oxford Encyclopedia of Ancient Greece & Rome. New York: Oxford University Press, 2010. Vol. 5:241; Vol 6:371. [https://www.oxfordreference.com/view/10.1093/acref/9780195170726.001.000
2 CONC 1/acref-9780195170726]
2 CONT * Lumberopoulou, Angeliki, ed. Cross-Cultural Interaction Between Byzantium and the West, 1204-1669: Whose Mediterranean Is It Anyway? New York: Routledge, 2018. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Cross_Cultural_Interaction_Bet
2 CONC ween_Byzan/hLJSDwAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Scipio&pg=PT382&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT
2 CONT “The iconographic tradition associate with Scipio is linked to the image of an African elephant. This visual association can be seen on the reverse of a denarius struck by Quintus Saecilius Metellus Pius Scipio (100/90-46 BCE), a 
2 CONC descendant of Scipio.This Iconography can also be found in a series of denarii struck around 81 BCE of Quintus Saecilius Metellus Pius (130-63 BCE), of the Caecilius Metellus gens, the adoptive gens of Pietas on the the obverse an
2 CONC d of an African elephant on the reverse.”
2 CONT
2 CONT * ''The Encyclopædia Britannica'', Vol. XVIII, 1th ed. Cambridge, England: Cambridge University Press,1911 [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Encyclopædia_Britannica/4FEEAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+P
2 CONC ius+Scipio&pg=PA258&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT * Goldsworthy, Adrian. Caesar: ''Life of a Colossus.'' New Haven CT: Yale University Press, 2006. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Caesar/aSmr_bVR2-kC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Quintus+Caecilius+Metellus+Pius+Scipio&pg=PA581&printsec=f
2 CONC rontcover]
