1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Appius Claudius Caudex=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Appius Claudius Caudex (f. 264 BC) was a patrician member of the Claudii. He was the grandson of Appius Claudius Caecus through his father Gaius Claudius Centho, and served as consul in 264 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT In that year, he drew Rome into conflict with Carthage over possession of Sicily. In 265 BC, Hiero II of Syracuse had attacked Messana (modern Messina) in an attempt to capture it from the Mamertines, mercenaries from Campania who
2 CONC  had taken it some years before. The Mamertines allied with a nearby Carthaginian fleet and held off the Syracusans, but when the Carthaginians did not leave, the Mamertines appealed to Rome in 264 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some senators were opposed to helping them, but Appius Claudius persuaded the citizens to support them. He led a force to Messina and, as the Mamertines had convinced the Carthaginians to withdraw, he met with only a symbolic resi
2 CONC stance. The Mamertines handed the city over to Appius Claudius, but the Carthaginians returned and laid siege to Messana. The Syracusans, meanwhile, were also stationed outside the city. Claudius tried to send ambassadors to both 
2 CONC the Carthaginians and the Syracusans, but he was ignored. He then led his troops outside the city, defeated the Syracusans in battle, and Hiero retreated back to Syracuse. The next day Claudius also defeated the Carthaginians. Fol
2 CONC lowing these victories Appius laid siege to Echetla but after the loss of many troops returned to Messana.
2 CONT
2 CONT This dispute was one of the immediate causes of the First Punic War.
2 CONT
2 CONT His surname, "Caudex", means "blockhead" or "idiot" in Latin.
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother was Publius Claudius Pulcher, consul in 249 BC.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appius_Claudius_Caudex
