1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''
2 CONT =Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica (b. 227 BC fl. 171 BC) (Nasica meaning "pointed nose") was a consul of ancient Rome in 191 BC. He was a son of Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus. Sometimes referred to as Scipio Nasica the First to dis
2 CONC tinguish him from his son and grandson, he was a cousin of Scipio Africanus.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the request of the Senate, he journeyed with the Roman matrons to receive the statue of Magna Mater in 204 when it arrived from Anatolia at Ostia. According to Livy and Ovid's Fasti we are told that he was chosen for this duty 
2 CONC because he was the best of the Roman community. He was later aedile in 197. As praetor in Hispania Ulterior (194), he defeated the Lusitanians at Ilipa, and as consul subjugated the Boii. He was not chosen as censor despite standi
2 CONC ng in both the elections of 189 and 184, a failure marking the decline of the influence of the Scipiones in Rome. He went on to help found Aquileia in 181, and appears in an inquiry of 171.
2 CONT
2 CONT This Scipio Nasica was the father of the Scipio Nasica who opposed Cato the Censor for several years on the question of Carthage. Both father and son were distinguished jurists; the father was reportedly given a house in the cente
2 CONC r of Rome by the Senate to make his advice more accessible to the Senate and people of Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Publius_Cornelius_Scipio_Nasica
