2 CONT 16870305536220234        i.  Ruler Of Rome Mark Anthony III ROMAN EMPIRE (born in 83 B.C. in Rome, Italy - died in 30 B.C. in Alexandria, Egypt) 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~mgholler/Caden/a55.htm#i547458954 Marcus Antonius Creticus (flourished 2nd century BC and 1st century BC) was a Roman politician, member of the Antonius family. Creticus was son of Marcus A
2 CONC ntonius Orator and by his marriage to Julia Antonia he had three sons: Triumvir Marcus Antonius, Gaius Antonius and Lucius Antonius. He was elected praetor in 74 BC and received an extraordinary commission, similar to that bestowe
2 CONC d upon triumvir Pompey by the Gabinian law years later and on his father three decades before, to clear the Mediterranean Sea of the threat of piracy, and thereby assist the operations against King Mithridates VI of Pontus. Cretic
2 CONC us not only failed in the task, but plundered the provinces he was supposed to protect from robbery.[1] He attacked the Cretans, who had made an alliance with the pirates, but was totally defeated, most of his ships being sunk. Di
2 CONC odorus Siculus states that he only saved himself by a disgraceful treaty.[2] As a result of this defeat he was mockingly given the byname Creticus, which means "conqueror of Crete", and also "man made of chalk", when translated fr
2 CONC om Latin. He died soon afterwards (72-71) in Crete. Most authorities are agreed as to his avarice and incompetence, but the biographer Plutarch describes him as friendly, honest and generous man.[3]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''
2 CONT =Marcus Antonius Creticus=
2 CONT '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus Antonius Creticus (flourished 1st century BC) was a Roman politician, member of the Antonius family. Creticus was son of Marcus Antonius Orator and, by his marriage to Julia Antonia, he had three sons: Triumvir Marcus Anton
2 CONC ius, Gaius Antonius and Lucius Antonius.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected praetor in 74 BC and received an extraordinary commission, similar to that bestowed upon triumvir Pompey by the Gabinian law 7 years later in 67 BC, and that conveyed on his father three decades before in 102 BC, to
2 CONC  clear the Mediterranean Sea of the threat of piracy, and thereby assist the operations against King Mithridates VI of Pontus. Creticus not only failed in the task, but plundered the provinces he was supposed to protect from robbe
2 CONC ry. He attacked the Cretans, who had made an alliance with the pirates, but was totally defeated, most of his ships being sunk. Diodorus Siculus states that he only saved himself by a disgraceful treaty. As a result of this defeat
2 CONC  he was mockingly given the byname Creticus, which means "conqueror of Crete", and also "man made of chalk", when translated from Latin. He died soon afterwards (72-71 BC) in Crete. Most authorities are agreed as to his avarice an
2 CONC d incompetence, but the biographer Plutarch describes him as a friendly, honest and generous man.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source : '''
2 CONT
