2 CONT Helen (Helena) of the Cross, called also "Britannica", born in 248, died
2 CONT
2 CONT in 328. The arms of Colchester
2 CONT
2 CONT were "a cross with three crowns."
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the first wife of Constantius I. Chlorus (Falvius Valerius
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantius), governor of Dalmatia, appointed Caesar to rule Gaul and
2 CONT
2 CONT Britain March 1, 293. He was the son of Eutropious, a Dardanian nobleman
2 CONT
2 CONT descended from the Gordiani, and his wife, Claudia, daughter of Claudius
2 CONT
2 CONT II. (Marcus Aurelius Flavius Claudius Gothicus), a virtuous and worthy
2 CONT
2 CONT Roman Emperor (268-270), who was a soldier, statesman, and a
2 CONT
2 CONT distinguished officer. Born in Illyria 214, he was trained in the hard
2 CONT
2 CONT school of warfare on the Danube frontier, and died of the Plague in 270,
2 CONT
2 CONT aged 55, whereupon his brother Marcus Aurelius Claudius Quintillus became
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantius I became Emperor of Rome in May 305, and in right of his
2 CONT
2 CONT wife, King of England. He was born in 242 and died at Eboracum (present
2 CONT
2 CONT day York, England) on July 25, 306. He married (2) Theodora, daughter of
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximinus, Roman Emperor. The son of Helen and Constantius I. was
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine the Great.
