2 CONT Sources: Universal Standard Encyclopedia, v.6; Kraentzler 1669, 1795.   K: Constantine "The Great," Emperor of Rome and Britain. Also Flavius Constantine "the Great,"  Emperor of Britain, Gaul and Rome.   Universal: Constantine I,
2 CONC  in full Flavius Valerius Aurelius Constantinus (about 280-337 A.D.) Roman Emperor, born at Naissus (modern Nish) in the Province of Moesia (now Yugoslavia). (See Smallwood, v2, pages 230-231 for more from the encyclopedia, which 
2 CONC also gives dates of about 250-306). He prepared to conquer the Picts in Scotland. He died at Eboracum (modern York) during the campaign.
1 NOTE Constantine the Great (about ad 274-337), Roman emperor (306-37), the 
2 CONT first Roman ruler to be converted to Christianity. He was the founder of 
2 CONT Constantinople (present-day Ìstanbul), which remained the capital of the 
2 CONT Eastern Roman (Byzantine) Empire until 1453. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine was born Flavius Valerius Constantinus at Nis in what is now 
2 CONT Yugoslavia, son of the commander Constantius Chlorus (later Constantius I) 
2 CONT and Helena (later Saint Helena), a camp follower. Constantius became 
2 CONT co-emperor in 305. Constantine, who had shown military talent in the East, 
2 CONT joined his father in Britain in 306. He was popular with the troops, who 
2 CONT proclaimed him emperor when Constantius died later the same year. Over the 
2 CONT next two decades, however, Constantine had to fight his rivals for the 
2 CONT throne, and he did not finally establish himself as sole ruler until 324. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the example of his father and earlier 3d-century emperors, 
2 CONT Constantine in his early life was a solar henotheist, believing that the 
2 CONT Roman sun god, Sol, was the visible manifestation of an invisible “Highest 
2 CONT God” (summus deus), who was the principle behind the universe. This god 
2 CONT was thought to be the companion of the Roman emperor. Constantine's 
2 CONT adherence to this faith is evident from his claim of having had a vision 
2 CONT of the sun god in 310 while in a grove of Apollo in Gaul. In 312, on the 
2 CONT eve of a battle against Maxentius (circa 250-312), his rival in Italy, 
2 CONT Constantine is reported to have dreamed that Christ appeared to him and 
2 CONT told him to inscribe the first two letters of his name (XP in Greek) on 
2 CONT the shields of his troops. The next day he is said to have seen a cross 
2 CONT superimposed on the sun and the words “in this sign you will be the 
2 CONT victor” (usually given in Latin, in hoc signo vinces). Constantine then 
2 CONT defeated Maxentius at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge, near Rome. The 
2 CONT Senate hailed the victor as savior of the Roman people. Thus, Constantine, 
2 CONT who had been a pagan solar worshiper, now looked upon the Christian deity 
2 CONT as a bringer of victory. Persecution of the Christians was ended, and 
2 CONT Constantine's co-emperor, Licinius (270?-325), joined him in issuing the 
2 CONT Edict of Milan (313), which mandated toleration of Christians in the Roman 
2 CONT Empire. As guardian of Constantine's favored religion, the church was then 
2 CONT given legal rights and large financial donations. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sole Ruler
2 CONT
2 CONT A struggle for power soon began between Licinius and Constantine, from 
2 CONT which Constantine emerged in 324 as a victorious Christian champion. Now 
2 CONT emperor of both East and West, he began to implement important 
2 CONT administrative reforms. The army was reorganized, and the separation of 
2 CONT civil and military authority, begun by his predecessor, Diocletian, was 
2 CONT completed. The central government was run by Constantine and his council, 
2 CONT known as the sacrum consistorium. The Senate was given back the powers 
2 CONT that it had lost in the 3d century, and new gold coins (solidi) were 
2 CONT issued, which remained the standard of exchange until the end of the 
2 CONT Byzantine Empire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine intervened in ecclesiastical affairs to achieve unity; he 
2 CONT presided over the first ecumenical council of the church at Nicaea in 325. 
2 CONT He also began the building of Constantinople in 326 on the site of ancient 
2 CONT Greek Byzantium. The city was completed in 330 (later expanded), given 
2 CONT Roman institutions, and beautified by ancient Greek works of art. In 
2 CONT addition, Constantine built churches in the Holy Land, where his mother 
2 CONT (also a Christian) supposedly found the True Cross on which Jesus was 
2 CONT crucified. The emperor was baptized shortly before his death, on May 22, 
2 CONT 337. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Evaluation
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantine unified a tottering empire, reorganized the Roman state, and 
2 CONT set the stage for the final victory of Christianity at the end of the 4th 
2 CONT century. Many modern scholars accept the sincerity of his religious 
2 CONT conviction. His conversion was a gradual process; at first he probably 
2 CONT associated Christ with the victorious sun god. By the time of the Council 
2 CONT of Nicaea (325), however, he was completely Christian, but still tolerated 
2 CONT paganism among his subjects. Although criticized by his enemies as a 
2 CONT proponent of a crude and false religion, Constantine strengthened the 
2 CONT Roman Empire and ensured its survival in the East. As the first emperor to 
2 CONT rule in the name of Christ, he was a major figure in the foundation of 
2 CONT medieval Christian Europe.
1 NOTE He ruled from 409 to 425.
