1 CONT Cyrus 2 the Great 
1 CONT
1 CONT Persian: korosh or khorvash
1 CONT
1 CONT (600?- 530 BCE) Persian king (550- 530 BCE) and part of the Achaemenid Dynasty. We do not know whether Cyrus was a title or a personal name.
1 CONT Cyrus was undoubtedly a clever leader and a great military leader. He appears also to have been a mild leader. Greek historians present him as a good ruler, generous, tolerant and in many cases mild. On the cylinder on the photo h
1 CONC e has recorded the first declaration of human rights. In the Bible he is mentioned as the king that allowed the Jews living in Babylonia to return to Israel.
1 CONT Cyrus chose to respected the culture and religion of the people he conquered. He also had several capitals; Ectabana, Pasargadae and Babylon as winter capital. As he conquered new lands, he did not call these Persia (or Anshan) or
1 CONC  provinces of Persia but he added them to the list of lands he ruled as a king.
1 CONT Cyrus gave the different lands of the empire status as provinces, called satrapies. The satraps enjoyed great independence, and many of them were guilty of nothing more than paying part of their tax income and contributing with so
1 CONC ldiers to the army.
1 CONT Cyrus is often ranked as the first great king of Persia, and the one who made it into an empire though his great conquests. The kingdom he inherited was Anshan, corresponding to southwestern Iran. 
1 CONT BIOGRAPHY
1 CONT
1 CONT 600 BCE?: Born as son of King Cambyses 1, and according to legends, grandson of King Astyages of Medes. Other suggestions to his birth year go as late as 580.
1 CONT 558: Cyrus becomes king over Anshan.
1 CONT 550: Leads a rebellion against the Medes and his alleged grandfather King Astyages, adding Medes to his own kingdom.
1 CONT — Becomes ruler over the Persian Empire.
1 CONT 546: Defeats Lydia and its king, Croesus after a surprise attack on the capital, Sardis.
1 CONT 539 October Takes control over the territories of the Babylonian Empire (including Mesopotamia, Syria and Palestine), after defeating King Nabonidus at Otis. The victory is recorded on a cylinder (see photo) with cuneiform writing
1 CONC , which has survived into modern times.
1 CONT 530's: Leads several campaigns to east, the lands that represent important parts of modern Iran.
1 CONT 530: Makes his son Cambyses 2 co-regent and embarks on a campaign against Queen Massangtae in the east.
1 CONT 528: Cyrus dies and is buried at Pasargadae. 
0 @N6000000002187600896@ NOTE Artaxerxes I of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Artaxerxes I was king of the Persian Empire from 464 BC to 424 BC. He belonged to the Achaemenid dynasty and was the successor of Xerxes I. He is mentioned in two books of the Bible, Ezra and Nehemiah. He allowed the Jews to rebui
1 CONC ld Jerusalem. He was followed on the throne by his son Xerxes II.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name as given is the Greek form; the Persian form is Artakhshathra. He was later called Ardeshir.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Persia had been defeated at Eurymedon, military action between Greece and Persia had come to a standstill. When Artaxerxes I took power, he began a new tradition of drawing off the Athenians by funding their enemies in Greec
1 CONC e. This indirectly caused the Athenians to move the treasury of the Delian League from the island of Delos to the Athenian acropolis. This funding practice inevitably prompted renewed fighting in 450 BC, where the Greeks attacked 
1 CONC at the Battle of Cyprus. After Cimon's failure to attain much in this expedition, "The Peace of Callias" was agreed between Athens, Argos and Persia in 449.
0 @N6000000002187599978@ NOTE Haman (Bible)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  The Punishment of Haman, by Michaelangelo.Haman (or Haman the Agagite ??? ?????) (possibly Persian "magnificent") was a 6th Century BCE Persian noble and vizier of the empire under Persian King Ahasuerus, generally identified as 
1 CONC Xerxes I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Haman in the Hebrew Bible 
1 CONT 2 Haman in other sources 
1 CONT 2.1 Midrash 
1 CONT 2.2 Josephus 
1 CONT 2.3 Qur'an 
1 CONT 3 Purim traditions 
1 CONT 4 Modern usage 
1 CONT 5 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Haman in the Hebrew Bible
1 CONT Haman is the villain in the Book of Esther. He is described as the son of Hammedatha the Agagite, presumably indicating his descent from Agag, king of Amalek.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the story, Haman and his wife Zeresh instigate a plot to kill all the Jews of ancient Persia. Haman attempts to convince Ahasuerus to order the killing of Mordechai and all the Jews of the lands he ruled. The plot is foiled by 
1 CONC Queen Esther, the king's recent wife, who is herself a Jew. Haman and his 10 sons are hanged from the gallows that had originally been built to hang Mordechai.
1 CONT
1 CONT After these things king Ahasuerus promoted Haman the son of Hammedatha the Agagite, and advanced him, and set his seat above all the princes who were with him. And all the king's servants, who were in the king's gate, bowed, and d
1 CONC id obeisance to Haman; for the king had so commanded concerning him. But Mordechai did not bow, or do him obeisance. (Esther, 3:1-2) 
1 CONT And When Haman saw that Mordechai did not bow or do him obedience, then Haman was full of wrath. But he disdained to lay hands on Mordechai alone; for they had told him of the people of Mordechai; so that Haman sought to destroy a
1 CONC ll the Jews who were throughout the whole kingdom of Ahasuerus, the people of Mordechai. (Esther, 3:5-6) 
1 CONT Later, Queen Esther learns that her people are in danger so she risks her own life on behalf of the salvation of the Hebrews living in Ancient Persia.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Xerxes asked Queen Esther, "Who is he? Where is the man who has dared to do such a thing?" 6 Esther said, "The adversary and enemy is this vile Haman." Then Haman was terrified before the king and queen. 7 The king got up in 
1 CONC a rage, left his wine and went out into the palace garden. But Haman, realizing that the king had already decided his fate, stayed behind to beg Queen Esther for his life. 8 Just as the king returned from the palace garden to the 
1 CONC banquet hall, Haman was falling on the couch where Esther was reclining. The king exclaimed, "Will he even molest the queen while she is with me in the house?" 9 As soon as the word left the king's mouth, they covered Haman's face
1 CONC . 9 Then Harbona, one of the eunuchs attending the king, said, "A gallows seventy-five feet [b] high stands by Haman's house. He had it made for Mordecai, who spoke up to help the king." 10 The king said, "Hang him on it!" 10 So t
1 CONC hey hanged Haman on the gallows he had prepared for Mordecai. Then the king's fury subsided. (Esther, 7:6-10)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Haman in other sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Midrash
1 CONT In Rabbinical tradition, Haman is considered an archetype of evil and persecutor of the Jews. Having attempted to exterminate the Jews of Persia, and rendering himself thereby their worst enemy, Haman naturally became the center o
1 CONC f many Talmudic legends. Being at one time in extreme want, he sold himself as a slave to Mordechai (Meg. 15a). He was a barber at Kefar Karzum for the space of twenty-two years (ib. 16a). Haman had an idolatrous image embroidered
1 CONC  on his garments, so that those who bowed to him at command of the king bowed also to the image (Esth. R. vii.).
1 CONT
1 CONT Haman was also an astrologer, and when he was about to fix the time for the massacre of the Jews he first cast lots to ascertain which was the most auspicious day of the week for that purpose. Each day, however, proved to be under
1 CONC  some influence favorable to the Jews. He then sought to fix the month, but found that the same was true of each month; thus, Nisan was favorable to the Jews because of the Passover sacrifice; Iyyar, because of the small Passover.
1 CONC  But when he arrived at Adar he found that its zodiacal sign was Pisces, and he said, "Now I shall be able to swallow them as fish which swallow one another" (Esth. R. vii.; Targ. Sheni iii.).
1 CONT
1 CONT Haman had 365 counselors, but the advice of none was so good as that of his wife, Zeresh. She induced Haman to build a gallows for Mordechai, assuring him that this was the only way in which he would be able to prevail over his en
1 CONC emy, for hitherto the just had always been rescued from every other kind of death. As God foresaw that Haman himself would be hanged on the gallows He asked which tree would volunteer to serve as the instrument of death. Each tree
1 CONC , declaring that it was used for some holy purpose, objected to being soiled by the unclean body of Haman. Only the thorn-tree could find no excuse, and therefore offered itself for a gallows (Esth. R. ix.; Midr. Abba Gorion vii.,
1 CONC  ed. Buber, Wilna, 1886; in Targum Sheni this is narrated somewhat differently).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Josephus
1 CONT Haman is mentioned by Josephus in his Antiquities of the Jews. Josephus' account of the story is drawn from the Septuagint translation of the Book of Esther and from other Greek and Jewish sources, some no longer extant.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Qur'an
1 CONT Template:Haman (Islam) Haman is also mentioned six times in the Qur'an, as an intimate of Pharaoh in the time of Moses. While they are obviously different, elements of the Qur'an's version of Haman may come from the Persian Haman 
1 CONC including his name and position as a royal builder.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Purim traditions
1 CONT Main article: Purim
1 CONT The Jewish holiday of Purim commemorates the story of the deliverance of the Jews and the defeat of Haman. On that day, the Book of Esther is read publicly and much noise and tumult is raised at every mention of his name. A specia
1 CONC l noisemaker called a Grogger is used to express disdain for Haman. Pastry known as Hamantaschen are traditionally eaten on this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Modern usage
1 CONT To "hang higher than Haman" is a phrase that has come to mean someone who has committed an act so egregious as to be destined for a very terrible punishment.
1 CONT
1 CONT Haman is also mentioned in South Park episode #309, Jewbilee.
1 CONT
1 CONT A major antagonist of Zeta Gundam, and the main antagonist in its sequel series, Gundam ZZ, is Haman Karn, a Zeon regent who usurps the power of the rightful ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1901–1906 Jewish Encyclopedia, a publication now in the public domain. 
