0 @N6000000002187617966@ NOTE Demetrius I of Macedon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius I (337-283 BC, Greek: ??µ?t????), surnamed Poliorcetes ("Besieger"), son of Antigonus I Monophthalmus and Stratonice, was a king of Macedon (294 - 288 BC). He belonged to the Antigonid dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the age of twenty-two he was left by his father to defend Syria against Ptolemy the son of Lagus; he was totally defeated in Battle of Gaza, but soon partially repaired his loss by a victory in the neighbourhood of Myus.
1 CONT
1 CONT After an unsuccessful expedition against Babylon, and several campaigns against Ptolemy on the coasts of Cilicia and Cyprus, Demetrius sailed with a fleet of 250 ships to Athens. He freed the city from the power of Cassander and P
1 CONC tolemy, expelled the garrison which had been stationed there under Demetrius of Phalerum, and besieged and took Munychia (307 BC). After these victories he was worshipped by the Athenians as a tutelary deity under the title of Sot
1 CONC er ("Preserver").
1 CONT
1 CONT In the campaign of 306 BC against Ptolemy he defeated Menelaus, Ptolemy's brother, in the naval Battle of Salamis, completely destroying the naval power of Egypt. In 305 BC, now bearing the title of king bestowed upon him by his f
1 CONC ather, he endeavoured to punish the Rhodians for having deserted his cause; his ingenuity in devising new siege engines in his unsuccessful attempt to reduce the capital gained him the title of Poliorcetes. Among his creations wer
1 CONC e a battering ram 180 feet long, requiring 1000 men to operate it; and a wheeled siege tower named "Helepolis" (or "Taker of Cities") which stood 125 feet tall and 60 feet wide, weighing 360,000 pounds.
1 CONT Coin of Demetrius I (337-283 BC). Greek inscription reads ??S???OS ????????? ([coin] of King Demetrius).
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Coin of Demetrius I (337-283 BC). Greek inscription reads ??S???OS ????????? ([coin] of King Demetrius).
1 CONT
1 CONT He returned a second time to Greece as liberator. But his licentiousness and extravagance made the Athenians long for the government of Cassander. He also roused the jealousy of Alexander's Diadochi; Seleucus, Cassander and Lysima
1 CONC chus united to destroy him and his father. The hostile armies met at the Ipsus in Phrygia (301 BC). Antigonus was killed, and Demetrius, after sustaining a severe losses, retired to Ephesus. This reversal of fortune stirred up man
1 CONC y enemies against him—the Athenians refused even to admit him into their city. But he soon afterwards ravaged the territory of Lysimachus and effected a reconciliation with Seleucus, to whom he gave his daughter Stratonice in marr
1 CONC iage. Athens was at this time oppressed by the tyranny of Lachares, but Demetrius, after a protracted blockade, gained possession of the city (294 BC) and pardoned the inhabitants for their former misconduct.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the same year he established himself on the throne of Macedonia by murdering Antipater II, the son of Cassander. But his new position was continually threatened by Pyrrhus, who took advantage of his occasional absence to ravage
1 CONC  the defenceless part of his kingdom (Plutarch, Pyrrhus, 7 if.); at length, the combined forces of Pyrrhus, Ptolemy and Lysimachus, assisted by the disaffected among his own subjects, obliged him to leave Macedonia in 288 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT He passed into Asia and attacked some of the provinces of Lysimachus with varying success. Famine and pestilence destroyed the greater part of his army, and he solicited Seleucus' support and assistance. But before he reached Syri
1 CONC a hostilities broke out, and after he had gained some advantages over his son-in-law, Demetrius was totally forsaken by his troops on the field of battle and surrendered to Seleucus. His son Antigonus offered all his possessions, 
1 CONC and even his person, in order to procure his father's liberty. But all proved unavailing, and Demetrius died after a confinement of three years (283 BC). His remains were given to Antigonus and honoured with a splendid funeral at 
1 CONC Corinth. His descendants remained in possession of the Macedonian throne till the time of Perseus, when Macedon was conquered by the Romans.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Antigonus I Monophthalmus Antigonid dynasty Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antigonus II Gonatas
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Antipater II of Macedon King of Macedon
1 CONT 294 BC–288 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Lysimachus and Pyrrhus of Epirus
0 @N6000000001353854508@ NOTE Birth: in c. 276 BC 1
1 CONT Death: in c. 222 BC 2
1 CONT Event: Ancestor M
1 CONT Event: Ruled 246 - 222 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt 3
1 CONT Event: Throne Name Iwa-en-netjerwy-senwy Sekhem-ankh-re Setep-amun 4
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Ptolemy III Euergetes...succeeded to the throne at the age of 30. [Chronicle of the Pharaohs, Peter A. Clayton, Thames and Hudson, Ltd., London, 1994, p. 210]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Ptolemy III EUERGETES (Greek: Benefactor) (fl. 246-221 BC), Macedonian king of Egypt, son of Ptolemy II; he reunited Egypt and Cyrenaica and successfully waged the Third Syrian War against the Seleucid kingdom.
1 CONT Almost nothing is known of Ptolemy's youth before 245, when, following a long engagement, he married Berenice II, the daughter of Magas, king of Cyrene; thereby he reunited Egypt and Cyrenaica, which had beendivided since 258. Sho
1 CONC rtly after his accession and marriage, Ptolemy invaded Coele Syria, to avenge the murder of his sister, the widow of the Seleucid king Antiochus II. Ptolemy's navy, perhaps aided by rebels in the cities, advanced against Seleucus 
1 CONC II's forces as far as Thrace, across the Hellespont, and also captured some islands off the AsiaMinor coast, but were checked c. 245. Meanwhile, Ptolemy, with the army, penetrated deep into Mesopotamia, reaching at least Seleucia 
1 CONC on the Tigris, near Babylon. According to classical sources he was compelled to halt his advance because of domestic troubles. Famine and a low Nile, as well as the hostile alliance between Macedonia, Seleucid Syria, and Rhodes, w
1 CONC ere perhaps additional reasons. The war in Asia Minor and the Aegean intensified as the Achaean League, one of the Greek confederations, allied itself to Egypt, while Seleucus II secured two allies in the Black Sea region. Ptolemy
1 CONC  was pushed out of Mesopotamia andpart of North Syria in 242-241, and the next year peace was finally achieved. Ptolemy managed to keep the Orontes River region and Antioch,both in Syria; Ephesus, in Asia Minor; and Thrace and per
1 CONC haps also Cilicia.
1 CONT Within Egypt, Ptolemy continued the colonization of al-Fayyum (the oasis-like depression southwest of Cairo), which his father had developed. He also reformed the calendar, adopting 311 as the first year of a "Ptolemaic Era." The 
1 CONC Canopus decree, a declaration published by a synod of Egyptian priests, suggests that the true duration of the year (365 1/4 days) was now recognized, for an extra day was added to the calendar every four years. The new calendar f
1 CONC ailed, however, to achieve popular acceptance. The priests and classical sources also credited Ptolemy with the restoration of the divine statues plundered from the temples during Persian rule. In addition, the King initiated cons
1 CONC tructionat Edfu, the Upper Egyptian site of a great Ptolemaic temple, and made donations to other temples.
1 CONT Ptolemy avoided involvement in the wars that continued to plague Syria and Macedonia. He did, however, send aid to Rhodes, after earthquakes devastated the island, but he refrained from subsidizing the schemes of the Spartan king 
1 CONC against Macedonia, though he granted him asylumin 222. In Asia Minor, when a pretender to one of the kingdoms, who was the instigator of much of the trouble there, sought asylum in Ptolemaic territory, Ptolemy promptly interned hi
1 CONC m. His policy was to maintain an equilibrium of power, guaranteeing the safety of his own territory. After declaring his son his successor, Ptolemy died, leaving Egypt at the peak of its political power, and internally stable and 
1 CONC prosperous
