0 @N6000000002187614915@ NOTE Adad-nirari III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Adad-nirari III (also Adad-narari) was King of Assyria from 811 to 783 BC. He was the son and successor of Shamshi-Adad V, and was apparently quite young at the time of his accession, because for the first five years of his reign 
1 CONC his mother Sammuramat acted as regent, which may have given rise to the legend of Semiramis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Adad-nirari's youth, and the struggles his father had faced early in his reign, caused a serious weakening for the Assyrian rulership over Mesopotamia, and gave way to the ambitions of the most high officers, the governors and the
1 CONC  local rulers.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Adad-nirari's inscriptions, he led several military campaigns with the purpose of regaining the strength Assyria enjoyed in the times of his grand-father, Shalmaneser III. According to the eponym canon, he campaigned 
1 CONC in all directions until the last of his 28 years of reign, and he was the builder of the temple of Nabu at Nineveh. Among his actions was a siege of Damascus in the time of Ben-Hadad III in 796 BC, which led to the eclipse of the 
1 CONC Aramaean Kingdom of Damascus and allowed the recovery of Israel under Jehoash (who paid the Assyrian king tribute at this time) and Jeroboam II.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spite of Adad-nirari's vigor, Assyria entered a several decades long period of weakness following his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Shamshi-Adad V King of Assyria
1 CONT 811–783 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shalmaneser IV
0 @N6000000002187614877@ NOTE Ashur-dan III
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-dan III was King of Assyria from 773 to 755 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-dan III was the son of Adad-nirari III, and succeeded his brother Shalmaneser IV in 773 BC. Ashur-dan's reign was a difficult age for the Assyrian monarchy. The rulership was severely limited by the influence of court dignit
1 CONC aries, particularly that of Shamshi-ilu, who was the commander-in-chief (turtanu). According to the eponym canon, in 765 BC Assyria was hit by a plague, and the following year the king could not campaign (it was customary for the 
1 CONC Assyrian king to lead a military expedition every year). In 763 BC a revolt broke out, which lasted until 759 BC, when another plague hit the land. Ashur-dan was succeeded by another brother, Ashur-nirari V.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Shalmaneser IV King of Assyria
1 CONT 773–755 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-nirari V
0 @N6000000002187614858@ NOTE Ashur-nirari V
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-nirari V was King of Assyria from 755 to 745 BC. He was succeeded by Tiglath-Pileser III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-nirari V was a son of Adad-nirari III, and succeeded his brother, Ashur-dan III. He inherited a difficult situation from his predecessor. The Assyrian rulership was severely limited by the influence of court dignitaries, par
1 CONC ticularly that of Shamshi-ilu, who was the commander-in-chief (turtanu). According to the eponym canon, for four years the king was compelled to stay "in the land". It was customary for the Assyrian king to campaign every year, an
1 CONC d such an indication usually meant the kingship had been seriously weakened. In his fourth and fifth regnal years, however, he campaigned to Namri. In 746 BC a revolt broke out again, and the following year the throne was seized b
1 CONC y Tiglath-pileser III, who may have been his brother or his son, or, alternately, a usurper with no relationship to the previous royal house at all.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ashur-dan III King of Assyria
1 CONT 755–745 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tiglath-Pileser III
0 @N6000000002187614896@ NOTE Shalmaneser IV
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Shalmaneser IV was king of Assyria (783 - 773 BC). He succeeded his father Adad-nirari III, and was succeeded by his brother Ashur-dan III. Very little information about his reign has survived.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the eponym canon, he led several campaigns against Urartu (mod. Armenia). His rulership was severely limited by the growing influence of high dignitaries, particularly that of Shamshi-ilu, who was then commander-in-ch
1 CONC ief of the army.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Adad-nirari III King of Assyria
1 CONT 783–773 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-dan III
0 @N6000000002187720044@ NOTE Phthia of Epirus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Phthia (in Greek F??a; lived 4th century BC) was a daughter of Menon of Pharsalus, the Thessalian hipparch, and wife of Aeacides, king of Epirus, by whom she became the mother of the celebrated Pyrrhus, as well as of two daughters
1 CONC : Deidamia, the wife of Demetrius Poliorcetes, and Troias, of whom nothing more is known.1 Her portrait is found on some of the coins of her son Pyrrhus.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Phthia (1)", Boston, (1867) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Note
1 CONT 1 Plutarch, Parallel Lives, "Pyrrhus", 1 
0 @N6000000002187720015@ NOTE Pyrrhus of Epirus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pyrrhus (318 BC - 272 BC) (Greek: ?????? - "the color of fire", "red-blonde", Latin Pyrrhus) - the Molossian king from ca. 297, the king of Epirus from 306 - 301 BC and in 297 BC - 272 BC and of Macedon in 288 - 284 and in 273 - 2
1 CONC 72 BC, one of the strongest opponents of Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Pyrrhus of EpirusPrince of one of the Alexandrian successor states, Pyrrhus' childhood and youth went past in unquiet conditions. He was only two years old when his father was dethroned and the family had to repair with Glaukias, 
1 CONC king of the Taulanti, one of the biggest of the Illyrian tribes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later the Epirotes called him back, but he was dethroned again at the age of 17 when he left his kingdom to attend the wedding of Glaukias' son in Illyria. In wars of the diadochi Pyrrhus fought beside his brother-in-law Demetrius
1 CONC  I of Macedon in battle of Ipsus (301 BC). Later, he become hostage of Ptolemy I of Egypt in treaty between Ptolemy I and Demetrius. Pyrrhus married Ptolemy's I stepdaughter Antigone and in 297 BC restored his kingdom of Epirus. N
1 CONC ext, he went to war against his former ally, Demetrius. By 286 BC he had deposed his former brother-in-law and took control over the Kingdom of Macedonia. Pyrrhus was driven out of Macedonia by Lysimachus, his former ally, in 284 
1 CONC BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 281 the Greek city of Tarentum, in southern Italy, fell out with Rome, and was faced with a Roman attack and certain defeat. Rome had already made itself into a major power, and poised to subdue all the Greek cities in Magna Gr
1 CONC aecia or Southern Italy. The Tarentines begged Pyrrhus to intervene and save them from Roman conquest.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Most important places in the Pyrrhic War (280-275 BC)Pyrrhus was encouraged to aid the Tarentines by an oracle from Delphi. His goals were not, however, selfless. He recognized the possibility of carving out an empire for himself 
1 CONC in Italy. He made an alliance with Ptolemy Ceraunus, King of Macedon and his most powerful neighbor, and arrived in Italy in 280 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT He entered in Italy with forces of 3,000 cavalry, 2,000 archers, 500 slingers, 20,000 infantry and 19 war elephants in a bid to subdue the Romans.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to his superior cavalry and his elephants he defeated in battle of Heraclea the Romans under their consul Publius Valerius Laevinus in 280 BC. Romans lost about 7,000 and Pyrrhus 4,000 soldiers. Though his casulties were high,
1 CONC  this battle is not usually considered a "pyrrhic" victory. Several tribes (the Lucanians, Bruttians, and Messapians) and the Greek cities of Croton and Locri joined Pyrrhus. He then offered Romans a peace treaty, which was reject
1 CONC ed. Pyrrhus spent winter in Campania.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Pyrrhus invaded Apulia (279 BC), the two armies met in the Battle of Asculum where Pyrrhus won a very costly victory. The Romans had lost 6,000 men and Pyrrhus 3,500.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 278, Pyrrhus received two offers simultaneously. The Greek cities in Sicily asked him to come and drive out Carthage (with Rome, one of the two great powers in the Western Mediterranean). At the same time, the Macedonians, whos
1 CONC e King Ceraunus had been killed by invading Gauls, asked Pyrrhus to ascend the throne of Macedon. Pyrrhus decided that Sicily offered him a greater opportunity, and transferred his army there.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pyrrhus was proclaimed king of Sicily. He was already making plans for his son Helenus to inherit the kingdom of Sicily, and his other son Alexander to be given that of Italy. In 277 Pyrrhus captured Eryx, the strongest Carthagini
1 CONC an fortress in Sicily. This prompted the rest of the Carthaginian-controlled cities to defect to Pyrrhus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 276, Pyrrhus negotiated with the Carthaginians. Although they were inclined to come to terms with Pyrrhus, supply him money and send him ships once friendly relations were established, he demanded that Carthage abandon all Sici
1 CONC ly and make the Libyan Sea a boundary between themselves and the Greeks. Meanwhile, he had begun to display despotic behavior towards the Sicilian Greeks, and soon Sicilian opinion became inflamed against him. Though he defeated t
1 CONC he Carthaginians in another battle, he was forced to abandon Sicily and return to Italy.
1 CONT
1 CONT When he returned, he fought a last inconclusive battle at Beneventum (275 BC) in southern Italy. This time there was not even a "pyrrhic" victory.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pyrrhus abandoned the campaign in Italy and returned to Epirus. Though his western campaign had taken a heavy toll on his army as well as his treasury, Pyrrhus yet again went to war. Attacking King Antigonus II Gonatas he won an e
1 CONC asy victory and seized the Macedonian throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 272, Cleonymus, a Spartan of royal blood but hated in Sparta, asked Pyrrhus to attack Sparta and place him in power. Pyrrhus agreed to the plan, intending to win control of the Peloponnese for himself, but unexpectedly strong r
1 CONC esistance thwarted his assault on Sparta. He was immediately offered an opportunity to intervene in a civic dispute in Argos. Entering the city with his army by stealth, he found himself caught in a confused battle in the narrow c
1 CONC ity streets. During the confusion, an old woman watching from a rooftop threw a roofing tile which stunned him, allowing an Argive soldier to kill him (some reports claim he was poisoned by a servant).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT A coin from Epirus. On left is the head of Pyrrhus of Epirus' mother, Phthia. On the right is Athena, shield and spear in hands with a battle stance.While he was a mercurial and often restless leader, and not always a wise king, h
1 CONC e was considered one of the greatest military commanders of his time, ranked by Hannibal himself to be the second greatest commander the world had seen after Alexander the Great. Pyrrhus was also known to be very benevolent. As a 
1 CONC general, Pyrrhus' greatest political weaknesses were the failure to maintain focus and the failure to maintain a strong treasury at home (many of his soldiers were costly mercenaries).
1 CONT
1 CONT His name is famous for the phrase "Pyrrhic victory" which refers to an exchange after the Battle of Asculum. In response to congratulations for winning a costly victory over the Romans, he is reported to have said: "One more such 
1 CONC victory and I shall be lost!" (In Greek: ?? ?t? µ?a? µ???? ????s?µe?, ?p????aµe?.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Pyrrhus wrote Memoirs and several books on art of war. These have since been lost although Hannibal was influenced by them and they received praise from Cicero.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT Plutarch's biography 
1 CONT Pyrrhus of Epirus Article from Livius.Org 
1 CONT A detailed biography of Pyrrhus 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Bibliography
1 CONT Pyrrhus, King of Epirus by Petros E. Garoufalias ISBN 090574313X 
1 CONT The Pyrrhus Portrait by Rolf Winkes, in: The Age of Pyrrhus, Proceedings of an International Conference held at Brown University April 8-10, 1988 (Archaeologia Transatlantica XI), Providence 1992, pages 175-188. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
