0 @N6000000002187622684@ NOTE Archelaus I of Macedon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Archelaus I was king of Macedon from 413 to 399 BC, following the death of Perdiccas II. The son of Perdiccas by a slave woman, Archelaus obtained the throne by murdering his uncle, his cousin, and his half-brother, the legitimate
1 CONC  heir, but proved a capable and beneficent ruler, known for the sweeping changes he made in state administration, the military, and commerce.
1 CONT
1 CONT Almost immediately after he took power, Archelaus was faced with a situation which allowed him to completely reverse Macedon's relationship with Athens, which had been a major threat for the past half century. The Athenians experi
1 CONC enced a crushing defeat at Syracuse in late 413 during which most of their ships were destroyed. This left the Athenians in desperate need of a huge amount of timber to build new ships and Archelaus in a position to set the price.
1 CONC  Archelaus generously supplied the Athenians with the timber they needed. In recognition of this, the Athenians honored Archelaus and his children with the titles of proxenos and euergetes of the people.
1 CONT
1 CONT Archelaus went on to institute many internal reforms. He issued an abundance of good quality coinage. He built strongholds, cut straight roads (important for movement of the military), and improved the organization of the military
1 CONC , particularly the cavalry and hoplite infantry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Archelaus was also known as a man of culture and extended cultural and artistic contacts with southern Greece. In his new palace at Pella (where he moved the capital from the old capital at Aigai), he hosted great poets, tragedian
1 CONC s, including Euripides (who wrote his tragedies Archelaus and The Bacchae while in Macedon), musicians, and painters, including Zeuxis (the most celebrated painter of his time). Archelaus reorganized the Olympia, a religious festi
1 CONC val with musical and athletic competitions honoring Olympian Zeus and the Muses at Dion, the Olympia of Macedon. The greatest athletes and artists of Greece came to Macedon to participate in this event.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Aelian, Archelaus was killed in 399 B.C. during a hunt, by one of the royal pages, his eromenos Craterus. The claim is that Archelaus had promised his beloved the hand of one of his daughters in marriage, and when he 
1 CONC reneged on the promise the boy became enraged and acted.[1] It is also possible that this was part of a conspiracy, although nothing was ever proven. By the time that he died, Archelaus had succeeded in converting Macedon into a s
1 CONC ignificantly stronger power. Thucydides credited Archelaus with doing more for his kingdom than all of his predecessors together.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes and references
1 CONT
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Aelian, Varia Historia, 8.9
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
