0 @N6000000002187624482@ NOTE Vonones I of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vonones I of Parthia (??O??S on his coins) ruled the Parthian Empire from about 8 to 12. He was the eldest son of Phraates IV of Parthia (ruled c. 37–2 BC) and was sent to Rome as a hostage in the 20s BC as surety for a treaty his
1 CONC  father made with Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the assassination of Orodes III in about 6, the Parthians applied to Augustus for a new King from the house of Arsaces. Augustus sent them Vonones I, but he could not maintain himself as King; he had been educated as a Roman
1 CONC , and was despised by the Parthian nobility as a slave of the Romans. Another member of the Arsacid house, Artabanus II (ruled c. 10–38), who was living among the Dahan nomads in the east of Parthia, was invited to the throne. In 
1 CONC a civil war he defeated and expelled Vonones I.
1 CONT
1 CONT The coins of Vonones I date from 8 to 12 and bear the inscription "King Vonones, conqueror of Artabanus" commemorating a temporary victory over his rival. Those of Artabanus II begin in 10. In about 12 Vonones I fled into Armenia 
1 CONC and became King there. But Artabanus II demanded his deposition, and as Augustus did not wish to begin a war with the Parthians he removed Vonones I into Syria, where he was kept in custody, though in a kingly style. Later he was 
1 CONC moved to Cilicia, and when he tried to escape in about 19, he was killed by his guards.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Orodes III King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artabanus II 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT Hon. Ana. 5, 9. 
1 CONT Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews, xviii, 2, 4. 
1 CONT Tacitus, Annals, ii, 4, 58, 68. 
0 @N6000000002187624452@ NOTE Orodes III of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Orodes III of Parthia was raised to the throne of the Parthian Empire by the magnates after the death of Phraates V, c. AD 6. He was killed after a short reign "on account of his extreme cruelty" (Josephus). After his death, 
1 CONC Phraates' brother Vonones I tried to assume the thone, but a civil war with Artabanus II followed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Musa and Phraates V King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vonones I 
