0 @N6000000002187718516@ NOTE Sanatruces of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Sanatruces of Parthia (also Sinatruces or Sanatruk) ruled the Parthian Empire from c. 77 to c. 70 BC. In the troubled times after the death of Mithridates II in c. 88 BC he was made king by the Sacaraucae, a Mongolian tribe w
1 CONC ho had invaded Iran in about 77 BC. He was eighty years old and reigned seven years; his successor was his son Phraates III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another Sanatruces (Sanatrucius), the son of Mithridates IV is mentioned as an ephemeral Parthian king in AD 115 by John Malalas, in his Chronographia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Unknown king King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Phraates III 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT Lucian, Macrob., 15; 
1 CONT Phlegon of Tralles, The Olympiads, preserved in Photius, Bibliotheca, 9
1 CONT Appian, Mithridates, 104. 
1 CONT Dio Cassius, xxxvi. 45. 
0 @N6000000002187718901@ NOTE Philip V of Macedon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip V was king of Macedonia from 221 BC to 179 BC. He belonged to the Antigonid dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Demetrius II and Chryseis, Philip was nine years old at his father's death in 230-229 BC. His cousin, Antigonus Doson, administered the kingdom as regent until his death in 221 BC-220 BC, when Philip was eighteen years 
1 CONC old.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip then ascended the throne and reigned until 179 BC. His reign was occupied in the vain struggle to maintain the old Macedonian supremacy in the Balkan peninsula, which became hopeless after the intervention of Rome, during t
1 CONC he First Macedonian War, and the decisive battle of Cynoscephalae (197 BC), during the Second Macedonian War.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his eldest son, Perseus who ruled as the last king of Macedon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Antigonus III Doson King of Macedon
1 CONT 221–179 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Perseus 
0 @N6000000002187718762@ NOTE Prusias I of Bithynia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prusias I Chlorus (c.230 BC - 182 BC) was the king of Bithynia. The son of Ziaelas, he formed a marriage alliance with Philip V of Macedon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prusias fought a war against Byzantium (220 BC), then defeated the Gauls that Nicomedes I had invited across the Bosporus (218). He expanded the territories of Bithynia in a series of wars against Attalus I of Pergamum and Heracle
1 CONC a Pontica on the Black Sea. Philip V granted him the ports of Cius and Myrleia in 202, which he renamed Prusa and Apameia respectively.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although he granted sanctuary to Hannibal, he remained neutral during Rome's war with Antiochus III the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his son Prusias II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ziaelas King of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Prusias II 
0 @N6000000002187718729@ NOTE Prusias II of Bithynia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prusias II Cynegus (c.182 - 149 BC) was the king of Bithynia. He succeeded his father Prusias I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prusias joined with Eumenes of Pergamon in a war against Pharnaces I of Pontus (181 - 179 BC). He later invaded the territories of Pergamon (156 - 154 BC), only to be defeated, and the Pergamenes insisted on heavy reparations. He 
1 CONC sent his son Nicomedes II to Rome to ask their help in reducing the amount of these reparations, but Nicomedes revolted and became King.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Prusias I King of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Nicomedes II 
0 @N6000000002187617101@ NOTE Ashur-rabi II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-rabi II was one of the longest-reigning kings of Assyria, reigning for 41 years. Little is known about his reign, of which few records survive. He was apparently a younger son of Ashurnasirpal I. Following the reigns of his 
1 CONC elder brother, Shalmaneser II, and his nephew Ashur-nirari IV, he became king in 1013 BC, and reigned until his death in 972 BC, when he was succeeded by his son Ashur-resh-ishi II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ashur-nirari IV King of Assyria
1 CONT 1013–972 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-resh-ishi II 
0 @N6000000003949970731@ NOTE Mithridates of Armenia (flourished 1st century) was an Iberian prince and a king of Armenia under the protection of the Roman Empire.  Mithridates was installed by his brother Pharasmanes I of Iberia who, encouraged by Tiberius, i
1 CONC nvaded Armenia and captured its capital Artaxata in 35. When the Parthian prince Orodes attempted to dispossess Mithridates of his newly-acquired kingdom, Pharasmanes assembled a large army, with which he totally defeated the Part
1 CONC hians in a pitched battle (Tacitus, Annals. vi. 32-35). At a later period (c. 37), the new emperor Caligula had Mithridates arrested, but Claudius restored him on the Armenian throne c. 42. Subsequently, Mithridates's relations wi
1 CONC th Pharasmanes deteriorated and the Iberian king instigated his son, Rhadamistus, to invade Armenia and overthrow Mithridates in 51. Betrayed by his Roman commanders, Mithridates surrendered, but was put to death by Rhadamistus, w
1 CONC ho usurped the crown.
0 @N6000000002187718740@ NOTE Nicomedes II Epiphanes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT This article contains information that has not been verified and thus might not be reliable. If you are familiar with the subject matter, please check the article for inaccuracies and modify as needed, citing sources.
1 CONT Nicomedes II, Epiphanes, was the king of Bithynia, from 149 to 91 BC. He was fourth in descent from Nicomedes I and was the son of Prusias II.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was so popular with the people that his father sent him to Rome to limit his influence. However in Rome, he also gained favor from the Roman Senate, forcing Prusias to send an emissary with secret orders to assassinate him. But
1 CONC  the emissary revealed the plot, and persuaded the prince to rebel against his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Supported by Attalus II, king of Pergamum, he was completely successful, and ordered his father to be put to death at Nicomedia. During his long reign Nicomedes adhered steadily to the Roman alliance, and assisted them against Ari
1 CONC stonicus of Pergamum.
1 CONT
1 CONT He made himself for a time master of Paphlagonia, and in order to have a claim on Cappadocia married Laodice (the widow of Ariarathes VI), who had fled to him when Mithradates the Great endeavoured to annex the country.
1 CONT
1 CONT When her two sons died, Nicomedes brought forward an impostor as a claimant to the throne; but the plot was detected. The Romans refused to recognize the claim, and required Nicomedes to give up all pretensions to Cappadocia and t
1 CONC o abandon Paphlagonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Prusias II King of Bithynia
1 CONT  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Nicomedes III 
0 @N6000000002187705427@ NOTE Ashurnasirpal I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashurnasirpal I was king of Assyria from 1050 BCE to 1031 BCE. Ashurnasirpal ruled during a troubled period of Assyrian history, marked by famine and war with nomads from the deserts to the west. He succeeded his father Shamshi-Ad
1 CONC ad IV and was succeeded by his son Shalmaneser II. He was also the father of a later king, Ashur-rabi II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Shamshi-Adad IV King of Assyria
1 CONT 1050–1031 BCE Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shalmaneser II 
0 @N6000000002187705443@ NOTE Shalmaneser II
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Shalmaneser II was King of Assyria from 1031 BC to 1019 BC. He succeeded his father, Ashurnasirpal I and was succeeded by his son, Ashur-nirari IV, but beyond this little is known of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ashurnasirpal I King of Assyria
1 CONT 1031–1019 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-nirari IV 
0 @N6000000002187622357@ NOTE Koinos of Macedon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT King Koinos, after Karanus, was the second king of the ancient kingdom of Macedonia.
0 @N6000000002187624868@ NOTE Pharnavaz I of Iberia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Pharnavaz I (also written Parnavaz, P’arnawaz, or Farnavaz) (Georgian: ?????????) (ca 335-237 BC) was the first king of the ancient Georgian kingdom of Iberia (Kartli) in ca 302-237 BC. Founder of the royal dynasty of Pharnavazian
1 CONC s, he is also said to have established a new official religion, a variation of Zoroastrianism, and to have reformed a Georgian alphabet and written language.
1 CONT
1 CONT Descriptions of the epoch of Pharnavaz provided by the early-medieval Georgian sources, is a mixture of fact and legend. There is also an inconsistency in the dates of his life and reign, which seems implausibly long for this peri
1 CONC od.
1 CONT
1 CONT The legend has it that he was a nephew of Samara, a chief (mamasakhlisi) of the Mtskheta tribe and descendant of Kartlos, an eponymous father of Kartli/Iberia. Azo, an officer of Alexander the Great, killed Samara and massacred hi
1 CONC s family and many of his people, and seized the power in the area. Little Pharnavaz survived however. He later served to Azo, setting a plan of his people's liberation. Fortune was good to him, and he found a huge amount of a trea
1 CONC sury while hunting in a forest. He used this to create an army and rose against the tyrant. Azo died on the battlefield and Pharnavaz was crowned the king of Iberia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Alexander’s invasion of Georgia is completely rejected by modern historians, they believe one of the diadochoi from Asia Minor could have sent in an army to conquer the area. The story might also reflect a struggle for po
1 CONC wer for the title of tribal leader. The local patrician Pharnavaz could have won this struggle to become a king, the fact that seems to have been recognized by the Seleucids. He also established friendly relations with the neighbo
1 CONC uring North Caucasian mountainous people Dzurdzuks, thought to be the ancestors of Vainakh peoples, and married their princess.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon his ascent to the throne, Pharnavaz divided his kingdom into several counties (saeristavo), including Egrisi, Argveti, Odzrkhe, Klarjeti, Tsunda, Samshvilde, Khunani, Kakheti, and a special military district (saspaspeto) of K
1 CONC artli. Of them, Egrisi (part of Colchis) was given to a local prince, Kuji, his borther-in-law and ally. Now Pharnavaz focused on social projects, including the creation of a new official cult of Armazi to whom a colossus was erec
1 CONC ted at the citadel of Armaztsikhe, and the restoration of towns and villages.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tradition ascribes to him a substantial reform of the Georgian alphabet and written language. Although the most ancient Georgian inscriptions known date back to the 5th century A.D., recently found inscriptions at Nekresi site are
1 CONC  now claimed to be even older dating from the 1st to the 3rd A.D.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Georgian chronicles, he died at the age of 92, in ca 237 BC, to be succeeded by his son Saurmag I.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Bibliography
1 CONT Pavle Ingorokva. Old Georgian chronicle "Moktsevay Kartlisay" and the List of the Kings of Iberia. In: Bulletin of the State Museum of Georgia, vol. XI-B, Tbilisi, 1942, pp. 259-320 (in Georgian) 
1 CONT Leonti Mroveli. "Tskhovreba Kartvelta Mepeta". In: "Kartlis Tskhovreba" (edited by Simon Kaukhchishvili), Tbilisi, 1942, pp. 14-19 (in Georgian) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
