1 CONT The Georgian Chronicle 
1 CONT Article by Giorgi L. Kavtaradze 
1 CONT P'arnawaz I, King of Kartli-Iberia 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT History of Georgia 
1 CONT Georgian alphabet 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT — King of Iberia
1 CONT c. 302 – c. 237 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Saurmag I 
0 @N6000000001353851845@ NOTE Vologeses V (or IV) (fl. 2nd and 3rd centuries), king of Parthia who reigned 191-208/209. He first appeared in 191 as a rebel against his father Vologeses III, whom he succeeded in 192. In 193 he stirred up a rebellion in the Roma
1 CONC n client kingdoms of Osroene and Adiabene, but in195 the Romans under Septimius Severus recovered the districts. Afterthe Roman army departed, Vologeses again swept through Mesopotamia and restored his suzerainty over Adiabene. Su
1 CONC bsequently Septimius Severus launched another campaign (197/198-202), advancing into Mesopotamia, occupying Nisibis, and plundering Ctesiphon. He failed, however, inhis attempts to conquer the frontier fortress of Hatra. Little mo
1 CONC re is known of Vologeses' reign; he was succeeded by his son Vologeses VI(or V) in 209.
0 @N6000000001354417840@ NOTE Vologeses V (or IV) (fl. 2nd and 3rd centuries), king of Parthia who reigned 191-208/209. He first appeared in 191 as a rebel against his father Vologeses III, whom he succeeded in 192. In 193 he stirred up a rebellion in the Roma
1 CONC n client kingdoms of Osroene and Adiabene, but in195 the Romans under Septimius Severus recovered the districts. Afterthe Roman army departed, Vologeses again swept through Mesopotamia and restored his suzerainty over Adiabene. Su
1 CONC bsequently Septimius Severus launched another campaign (197/198-202), advancing into Mesopotamia, occupying Nisibis, and plundering Ctesiphon. He failed, however, inhis attempts to conquer the frontier fortress of Hatra. Little mo
1 CONC re is known of Vologeses' reign; he was succeeded by his son Vologeses VI(or V) in 209.
