0 @N6000000002187718468@ NOTE Herod Philip
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Herod Philip (4 BC–34 AD) was son of Herod the Great and half-brother of Herod Antipas and Herod Archelaus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip inherited the northeast part of his father's kingdom and is mentioned briefly in the Bible by Luke (3,1): Herod was ruler of Galilee, and his brother Philip ruler of the region of Ituraea and Trachonitis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip also appears in the Bible in connection with the execution of John the Baptist. John reproaches Herod Antipas for marrying Herodias, the wife of his brother Herod Philip. There is some confusion between Herod Philip, the so
1 CONC n of Mariamne II, and Philip the tetrarch who was the son of Cleopatra of Jerusalem.
0 @N6000000002187597552@ NOTE Antipater the Idumaean
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antipater the Idumaean, also known as Antipas, as was his father and his grandson Herod Antipas, was the founder of the Herodean dynasty and father of Herod the Great. He was a half-Arab, Hellenized client of Pompey. Antipater cam
1 CONC e from Idumaea, the then-current name for the land of Edom of the Hebrew Bible, which lay southeast of Judea, between the Dead Sea and the Gulf of Aqaba.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antipater was married to Cypros, a Nabataean noblewoman. His genealogy was more a matter of public relations than confirmed history: although some list Antipater as descending from the Jewish remnant that returned from the Babylon
1 CONC ian Captivity, it is more likely he was an Edomite. Idumeans had been forcibly converted to Judaism, so both Antipater and Herod the Great were of the Jewish faith, but were resented by the observant and nationalist Jews of Judea 
1 CONC for their non-Jewish and Arab ancestry, their Hellenized culture, and their collusion with the kittim, the Roman invaders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antipater laid the foundation for Herod's ascension to the throne of Judea partly through his activities in the court of the Hasmoneans, the heirs of the Maccabees, who were the hereditary leaders of the Jews, and partly by curryi
1 CONC ng favor with the Romans, who made him the first Roman procurator of Judea. Antipater and Cypros had four sons: Phasael, Herod, Joseph, and Pheroras. They also had a daughter, Salome, one of several Salomes among the Herodians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antipater was a wealthy man, and was favored by his fellow Idumeans. Josephus – from whom the details of Judean politics in this period are derived – described him as seditious in his nature. Antipater insinuated himself into the 
1 CONC party of Hyrcanus II in his contest for power with his brother Aristobulus; both were Hasmonean princes. Their father, Alexander, had made Antipater general of all of Judea, for he had a rapport with the Arabians. Hyrcanus succeed
1 CONC ed his mother as ruler, but bowed out in favor of his younger brother, Aristobulus. But Antipater continued to support Hyrcanus, and he advised Hyrcanus to put himself under the protection of the Arabian King Aretas III in Petra. 
1 CONC Together they would attack Aristobulus in Jerusalem. Pompey put down the trouble, and made Hyrcanus the ethnarch of Judea. Antipater remained in charge of affairs of the state.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Roman province of Judea in Syria was split, and Idumaea was eventually given to Antipater to govern. These divisions would later be seats of power for the Herodean descendants of Antipater. When Caesar defeated Pompey, Antipat
1 CONC er aided Caesar in Alexandria, and was made chief minister of Judea, with the right to collect taxes. Antipater eventually made his son, Phasael, governor of Jerusalem, and Herod became governor of Galilee. After the assassination
1 CONC  of Caesar, Antipater was forced to side with Cassius against Marc Antony. The pro-Roman politics of Antipater led to his increasing unpopularity among the devout, non-Hellenized Jews, and he was poisoned.
1 CONT
1 CONT The diplomacy and artful politics of Antipater, as well as his insinuation into the Hasmonean court, paved the way for the rise of his son Herod the Great, who used this position to marry the Hasmonean princess Mariamne, endear hi
1 CONC mself to Rome, and usurp the Judean throne, to become king of Judea under Roman influence.
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: The number of Antipaters, Antipases, Herods, Philips, Marys, Josephs, Salomes, etc., at this time in history, makes differentiating among them quite a challenge.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Josephus, Flavius. William Whistom, translator. (2003) The Works of Josephus: Complete and Unabridged, Updated Edition(17th printing). The Antiquities of the Jews.The Wars of the Jews. Peabody, MA: Hendrickson Publishers. ISBN 1-5
1 CONC 6563-167-6 
1 CONT Eisenman, Robert, 1997. James, the Brother of Jesus. Political background of Judea.
