1 CONT
1 CONT Death: Executed by Nero
0 @N6000000002187712433@ NOTE Valeria Messalina
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Valeria Messalina[1], sometimes spelled Messallina, (c. 17/20 – 48) was Roman Empress as the third wife of Emperor Claudius. A powerful and influential woman with a slatternly reputation, she conspired against her husband and was 
1 CONC executed when the plot was discovered.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family and early life 
1 CONT 2 Marriage to Claudius 
1 CONT 3 Roman Empress 
1 CONT 4 Reputation 
1 CONT 5 Downfall, death and aftermath 
1 CONT 6 In fiction 
1 CONT 7 Sources 
1 CONT 8 References 
1 CONT 9 Notes 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and early life
1 CONT Messalina was the daughter of Domitia Lepida and Marcus Valerius Messalla Barbatus[2][3]. Messalina's father was the son of Marcus Valerius Messala Barbatus Appianus [4], a Claudius Pulcher by birth (son of Appius Claudius Pulcher
1 CONC , cos. 38 BC) adopted by Marcus Valerius Messala, cos. suff. 32 BC[5][6]. His mother was Claudia Marcella Minor. Her mother, Domitia Lepida, was the youngest child of consul Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus and Antonia Major. Messalina
1 CONC ’s grandmothers were half sisters and nieces of Rome's first Emperor Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born no later than 12 BC and on the basis on his family distinction, Messalina's father could have expected a consulship by 23. Since he didn't become consul, it has been suggested that he must have died before that date.[7] Her m
1 CONC other then married consul Faustus Cornelius Sulla Lucullus III, great-grandson of Roman Dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla. Faustus and Lepida had a son circa 22, Faustus Cornelius Sulla Felix, Messalina's half brother. Messalina was
1 CONC  most probably born and raised in Rome. Very little is known on her early life.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage to Claudius
1 CONT Either in 37 or 38, Messalina married her second cousin Claudius who was about 48 years old. During the reign of another second cousin of hers, the unstable Roman Emperor Caligula (reigned 37-41), Messalina was very wealthy, an in
1 CONC fluential figure and a regular at Caligula’s court. Claudius was Caligula’s paternal uncle and was becoming influential and popular. Claudius probably married her to strengthen ties within the imperial family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Messalina bore Claudius two children, a daughter Claudia Octavia (born 39 or 40), who was a future empress and first wife to future emperor Nero, and a son, Britannicus (born 41). On 24 January 41, Caligula and his family were mur
1 CONC dered and later that day the Praetorian Guard proclaimed Claudius the new emperor and Messalina became the new empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Roman Empress
1 CONT
1 CONT Messalina holding her son Britannicus, LouvreMessalina became the most powerful woman in the Roman Empire. Claudius bestowed various honors on her: her birthday was officially celebrated, statues of her were erected in public plac
1 CONC es and she was given the privilege of occupying the front seats at the theatre along with the Vestal Virgins. The Roman Senate wanted Messalina to have the title of "Augusta", however Claudius refused.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 43 Claudius held a triumphant military parade to celebrate the successful campaign in Britain. Messalina followed his chariot in a covered carriage and behind her marched the generals.
1 CONT
1 CONT Through her status, she became very influential, however in character was very insecure. Claudius, as an older man, could have died at any moment and Britannicus would have become the new emperor. To improve her own security and e
1 CONC nsure the future of her children, Messalina sought to eliminate anyone who was a potential threat to her and her children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Among those who were loyal to Messalina was consul Lucius Vitellius. He begged her as a tremendous privilege for him to remove Messalina’s shoes. Vitellius would nurse her right shoe between his toga and tunic and would sometimes 
1 CONC take the shoe out and kiss it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Because of Claudius' devotion to her, Messalina was able to manipulate him into ordering the exile or execution of various people: Roman Historian Seneca the Younger; Claudius’ nieces Julia Livilla and Julia; Marcus Vinicius (husb
1 CONC and of Julia Livilla); consul Gaius Asinius Pollio II (see Vipsania Agrippina), the elder Poppaea Sabina (mother of Empress Poppaea Sabina, second wife of Nero), consul Decimus Valerius Asiaticus and Polybius (freedman). Claudius 
1 CONC had the reputation of being easily controlled by his wives and freedmen.
1 CONT
1 CONT A well known example of Messalina trying to eliminate her rivals was when Agrippina the Younger returned from exile after January 41. Agrippina was a niece to Claudius, a daughter of Claudius’ late brother Germanicus. Messalina re
1 CONC alised that Agrippina’s son Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus (the future Nero) was a threat to her son’s position and sent assassins to strangle Nero during his siesta. When they approached his couch, they saw what appeared to be a sna
1 CONC ke near his pillow and fled in terror. The apparent snake was actually a sloughed-off snake skin.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reputation
1 CONT The ancient Roman sources (particularly Tacitus and Suetonius), portray Messalina as insulting, disgraceful, cruel, avaricious, and a foolish nymphomaniac. Many women of her age and status enjoyed festivities and parties, but the 
1 CONC two historians contended that Messalina unwisely combined her zest for meeting people with a sexual appetite. A widely reported tale was of Messalina’s challenge to a notorious Roman prostitute named Scylla of an all-night sex com
1 CONC petition. Scylla gave up at dawn when each woman had taken 25 lovers, but Messalina saw no reason to stop copulating until well into the morning. It is said that she was exhausted, but not satisfied.
1 CONT
1 CONT Roman sources claim that Messalina used sex to enforce her power and control politicians, that she had a brothel under an assumed name and organised orgies for upper class women and that she participated much in politics and sold 
1 CONC her influence to Roman nobles or foreign notables.
1 CONT
1 CONT Juvenal is also highly critical of her in his Satire VI (first translation by Peter Green and second translation from wikisource):
1 CONT
1 CONT Then consider the God's rivals, hear what Claudius 
1 CONT had to put up with. The minute she heard him snoring 
1 CONT his wife - that whore-empress - who­ dared to prefer the mattress 
1 CONT of a stews to her couch in the Palace, called for her hooded 
1 CONT night-cloak and hastened forth, with a single attendant. 
1 CONT Then, her black hair hidden under an ash-blonde wig, 
1 CONT she'd make straight for her brothel, with its stale, warm coverlets, 
1 CONT and her empty reserved cell. Here, naked, with gilded 
1 CONT nipples, she plied her trade, under the name of 'The Wolf-Girl', 
1 CONT parading the belly that once housed a prince of the blood. 
1 CONT She would greet each client sweetly, demand cash payment, 
1 CONT and absorb all their battering - without ever getting up. 
1 CONT Too soon the brothel-keeper dismissed his girls: 
1 CONT she stayed right till the end, always last to go, 
1 CONT then trailed away sadly, still | with burning, rigid vulva, 
1 CONT exhausted by men, yet a long way from satisfied, 
1 CONT cheeks grimed with lamp-smoke, filthy, carrying home 
1 CONT to her Imperial couch the stink of the whorehouse. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Then look at those who rival the Gods, and hear what Claudius 
1 CONT endured. As soon as his wife perceived that her husband was asleep, 
1 CONT this august harlot was shameless enough to prefer a common mat 
1 CONT to the imperial couch. Assuming night-cowl, and attended by a single maid, 
1 CONT she issued forth; then, having concealed her raven locks under a light-coloured peruque, 
1 CONT she took her place in a brothel reeking with long-used coverlets. 
1 CONT Entering an empty cell reserved for herself, she there took her stand, under the feigned name of Lycisca, 
1 CONT her nipples bare and gilded, and exposed to view the womb that bore thee, O nobly-born Britannicus! 
1 CONT Here she graciously received all comers, asking from each his fee; 
1 CONT and when at length the keeper dismissed his girls, 
1 CONT she remained to the very last before closing her cell, 
1 CONT and with passion still raging hot within her went sorrowfully away. 
1 CONT Then exhausted by men but unsatisfied, 
1 CONT with soiled cheeks, and begrimed with the smoke of lamps, 
1 CONT she took back to the imperial pillow all the odours of the stews.   
1 CONT According to Satire VI, Messalina worked in a brothel under the assumed name Lycisca, 'The Wolf-Girl'. Etching by Agostino Carracci, late 16th century. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Downfall, death and aftermath
1 CONT
1 CONT Valeria Messalina and her children Britannicus and Claudia OctaviaDuring the Secular games in 47, at the performance of the Troy Pageant, Messalina attended the event with her son Britannicus. Also present was Agrippina the Younge
1 CONC r with her son Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus (Nero). Agrippina and Nero received a greater acclamation from the audience than Messalina and Britannicus did. Many people began to show pity and sympathy to Agrippina, due to unfortunat
1 CONC e circumstances that occurred in her life. This is probably a first sign of Messalina's declining popularity.
1 CONT
1 CONT Later that year, Messalina became interested in the attractive Roman Senator Gaius Silius who was happily married to the aristocratic woman Junia Silana (sister of Caligula’s first wife). Messalina and Silius became lovers and Mes
1 CONC salina forced Silius to divorce his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Silius realised the danger that he put himself in. Messalina and Silius plotted to kill the weak emperor and Messalina would make him the new emperor. Silius was childless and wanted to adopt Britannicus. They had committed bigamy
1 CONC : Messalina and Silius married in a full ceremony, in front of witnesses and had signed marriage contracts while Messalina was still legally married to Claudius.
1 CONT
1 CONT While Claudius was in Ostia, inspecting construction work done on the harbour, his freedman Narcissus, advised him of Messalina’s and Silius’ plot to him. Messalina travelled to Ostia with her children hoping to speak to Claudius,
1 CONC  however the emperor left Ostia before she was able to. Narcissus delayed Messalina, preventing her from seeing Claudius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Claudius ordered the deaths of Messalina and Silius in 48. In Messalina’s final hours, she was in the Gardens of Lucullus. Messalina and her mother, were preparing a petition for Claudius. At the height of Messalina’s influence an
1 CONC d prosperity, Lepida and Messalina had argued and became estranged. Apparently overcome by pity, Lepida stayed with her daughter. Lepida's last words to her were ‘Your life is finished. All that remains is to make a decent end’. M
1 CONC essalina was reputedly weeping and moaning. She finally realised the situation in which she had put herself.
1 CONT
1 CONT An officer and a former slave arrived together to witness Messalina’s death. The former slave verbally insulted her while the officer stood by in silence. Messalina was offered the choice of killing herself, but was too afraid to 
1 CONC do so, so the officer stabbed Messalina with a dagger. Her dead body was left with her mother. At the time of Messalina's death, Claudius was attending a dinner. When Messalina's death was announced to him, Claudius showed no emot
1 CONC ion but asked for more wine.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the days after her death, Claudius gave no sign of hatred, anger, distress, satisfaction or any other human passion. The only ones who mourned for Messalina were her children. The Roman Senate ordered Messalina’s name removed f
1 CONC rom all public or private places and all statues of her were removed.
1 CONT
1 CONT On New Year’s Day in 49, Claudius married as his fourth wife Agrippina the Younger, who went on to remove from the imperial court anyone she considered loyal to the memory of Messalina. Agrippina’s son Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus 
1 CONC was adopted by Claudius as his son and heir; he became known as Nero Claudius Caesar Drusus Germanicus and succeeded Claudius as emperor instead of Messalina's son Britannicus. Nero married Messalina’s daughter. Messalina’s name i
1 CONC s now often used as a synonym for sexual promiscuity, manipulativeness, and treachery.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] In fiction
1 CONT Carlo Pallavicino's Venetian opera Messalina of 1680 deals with Valeria Messalina.
1 CONT
1 CONT Messalina was featured prominently in Robert Graves' novels I, Claudius and Claudius the God. In keeping with the historical views at the time the novels were written (1934-35), Messalina is portrayed as a young teenager at the ti
1 CONC me of her marriage. She is also credited with all the actions mentioned in the ancient sources. This character was played by Sheila White in the 1976 BBC television adaptation of the two books, and was to have been played by Merle
1 CONC  Oberon in Josef von Sternberg's 1937 film of I, Claudius.
1 CONT
1 CONT Besides the adaptation of Graves' work, the character of Messalina has been portrayed many times elsewhere in movies and television films or miniseries. Here are some of the other actresses who have played Messalina:
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria Caserini in the 1910 Italian silent film Messalina, directed by Enrico Guazzoni. 
1 CONT Rina De Liguoro in the 1922 Italian silent film Messalina, directed by Enrico Guazzoni. 
1 CONT Maria Felix in the 1951 Italian film Messalina, directed by Carmine Gallone. 
1 CONT Susan Hayward in the 1954 Biblical Epic Demetrius and the Gladiators. 
1 CONT Belinda Lee in the 1960 film Messalina, Venere imperatrice. 
1 CONT Anneka Di Lorenzo in the 1979 film Caligula. 
1 CONT Jennifer O'Neill in the 1985 TV series A.D. Anno Domini. 
1 CONT Sonia Aquino in the 2004 TV movie Imperium: Nero. 
1 CONT The French writer Alfred Jarry based his novel Messalina (or The Garden of Priapus in Louis Colman's English translation) on the myths surrounding the subject. She is referred to in his book Le Surmâle (in English the Supermale); 
1 CONC these two books are offered as diametrically opposed entities in his 'pataphysical œuvre. The Messalinas of these books are highly fictionalized and subject to Jarry's fanciful and extravagant imagination.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Robert Jordan's The Wheel of Time, the Forsaken Mesaana is named after Messalina. In Mikhail Bulgakov's The Master and Margarita, Messalina is a guest at Satan's ball. In Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre, Mr. Rochester refers to hi
1 CONC s first wife as his Indian Messalina. In Leopold von Sacher-Masoch's Venus in Furs, the protagonist's aunt, who 'first aroused [his] desire for women' is referred to as a Messalina. Mario Puzo's The Last Don revolves around a film
1 CONC  called "Messalina" based on the notorious all night exploits of the empress.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
