0 @N6000000002187718049@ NOTE Claudia Marcella
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Claudia Marcella was the name of both daughters of Octavia the sister of Caesar Augustus, and her first husband, the consul Gaius Claudius Marcellus.
1 CONT
1 CONT The elder, Claudia Marcella Major (b. 44 BC), was married to Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa in 28 BC to cement a political alliance between Agrippa and her uncle. Their daughter Vipsania Marcella was born in Rome in 27 BC. In 21 BC Agri
1 CONC ppa divorced Claudia to marry Augustus' daughter Julia Caesaris. She then married Iullus Antonius, the son of Mark Antony, who was later exiled for adultery with Julia. Their son Lucius Antonius was born in 20 or 19 BC. After Iull
1 CONC us' exile, Claudia married Sextus Appuleius, the consul of AD 14. In AD 17 their daughter Appuleia Varilla was charged with adultery and speaking insultingly about the imperial family. Although she was acquitted, her loved Manlius
1 CONC  was banished from Italy and Africa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her younger sister, Claudia Marcella Minor (b. 43 BC), was married first to the consul Aemilius Lepidus Paullus. Their daughter Claudia Pulchra was born in 14 BC. After Paullus' death in 13 BC, Claudia married the consul Marcus Va
1 CONC lerius Messalla Messallinus. Through their son, Marcus Valerius Messalla Barbatus, she was the grandmother of the emperor Claudius' wife Messalina.
0 @N6000000002188289313@ NOTE Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa is the father of another Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa, Roman Politician and General Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa and distinguished Roman woman Vipsania Polla.
1 CONT
1 CONT The family of Lucius Vipsanius originated from the Italian countryside and were of humble and plebs origins. The family was of equestrian rank and were wealthy. However, the Roman aristocracy considered them undistinguished and un
1 CONC sophisticated. Little is known of the life of Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Pantheon, built by the Roman Emperor Hadrian in Rome in 118, was based on the design by his son Marcus. The name of Lucius Vipsanius and his son are inscribed on the building.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Source
1 CONT http://www.livius.org/vi-vr/vipsanius/agrippa.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucius_Vipsanius_Agrippa"
0 @N6000000002188289328@ NOTE Vipsania Polla
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vipsania Polla was the daughter of Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa, (a man of equestrian rank) and sister to another Lucius Vipsanius Agrippa and Roman General and Politician Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa. Polla lived during the late Roman Re
1 CONC public and in the reign of Rome’s first Emperor Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although this Roman woman was a distinguished woman, little is known on the life of Vipsania Polla. All we know is that Polla finished the construction of a monument called the Porticus Vipsania. The Porticus Vipsania, was a map o
1 CONC f the Roman Empire, which was engraved in Marble. Marcus Agrippa started the construction of this map, before his death in 12 BC. The Porticus Vipsania was once displayed in Rome, not far the Via Flaminia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although the Porticus Vipsania hasn’t survived, the description of this map is mentioned in Natural History by Pliny the Elder and through the Peutinger Map.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Source:
1 CONT http://www.livius.org/vi-vr/vipsanius/agrippa.html
0 @N6000000002187718060@ NOTE Gaius Claudius Marcellus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Gaius Claudius Marcellus was the name of several men in ancient Rome. Two of the most prominent bearers of this name were first cousins and held the consulship in successive years (50-49 BC).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT C. Claudius Marcellus Major was son of another Gaius and first cousin of the C. Claudius Marcellus Min. (below). He was consul in 49 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT C. Claudius Marcellus Minor (d. 40 BC) was a member of the Claudian Family and a direct descendant of the consul Marcus Claudius Marcellus. His father was also named Marcus, and his mother was named Junia.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was consul in 50 BC. He attempted to recall Julius Caesar from his ten-year governorship in Gaul two years early. Failing this, he called unsuccessfully upon Caesar to resign. He also obstructed Caesar from standing for a secon
1 CONC d consulship in absentia, insisting that he should return to Rome to stand, thereby forgoing the protection of his armies in Gaul. When Caesar finally invaded Italy, Marcellus, unlike his brother and nephew, did not take up arms a
1 CONC gainst him. Caesar subsequently pardoned him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcellus married in an arranged ceremony Octavia Thurinia Minor, a great-niece of the Julius Caesar and sister of Octavian. They had three children: two daughters, both named Claudia Marcella and born in Rome, and a son, Marcus, 
1 CONC born in Baia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Caesar's assassination, Marcellus became a supporter of Octavian. He died in May 40 BC. Five months later, his widow married Mark Antony.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Roman General Publius Quinctilius Varus and his 2 sisters were grandchildren from his first marriage.
0 @N6000000002187712722@ NOTE Atia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Atia Balba is the name of the three daughters of Julia (the second eldest sister of Julius Caesar) and Marcus Atius Balbus, one of whom was the mother of Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Atia Balba Prima 
1 CONT 2 Atia Balba Caesonia 
1 CONT 2.1 Popular culture 
1 CONT 3 Atia Balba Tertia 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Atia Balba Prima
1 CONT Atia Balba Prima was the mother of Quintus Pedius, the suffect consul of 43 BC. Her grandson, also named Quintus Pedius, was a deaf painter beloved by Augustus (a portrait of the deaf painter, who died in 13, can be seen at [1].
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Atia Balba Caesonia
1 CONT Atia Balba Caesonia (85 BC-43 BC) married the Macedonian governor and senator Gaius Octavius. Their children were Octavia Thurina Minor and Gaius Octavius Thurinus, later Augustus. In 59 BC, Octavius died on his way to Rome to sta
1 CONC nd for the consulship, and Atia married Lucius Marcius Philippus, the consul of 56 BC and a supporter of Julius Caesar. He loved raising his stepchildren alongside his own son and daughter from a previous marriage and arranged Oct
1 CONC avia's first marriage, to the consul and senator Gaius Claudius Marcellus Minor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Atia was a religious and caring matron. Tacitus considered her as an ideal Roman matron. Suetonius' account of Augustus, mentions the divine omens she experienced before and after his birth:
1 CONT
1 CONT When Atia had come in the middle of the night to the solemn service of Apollo, she had her litter set down in the temple and fell asleep, while the rest of the matrons also slept. On a sudden a serpent glided up to her and shortly
1 CONC  went away. When she awoke, she purified herself, as if after the embraces of her husband, and at once there appeared on her body a mark in colours like a serpent, and she could never get rid of it; so that presently she ceased ev
1 CONC er to go to the public baths. In the tenth month after that Augustus was born and was therefore regarded as the son of Apollo. Atia too, before she gave him birth, dreamed that her vitals were borne up to the stars and spread over
1 CONC  the whole extent of land and sea, while Octavius dreamed that the sun rose from Atia's womb. 
1 CONT The day he was born the conspiracy of Catiline was before the House, and Octavius came late because of his wife's confinement; then Publius Nigidius, as everyone knows, learning the reason for his tardiness and being informed also
1 CONC  of the hour of the birth, declared that the ruler of the world had been born. 
1 CONT Clause 94.
1 CONT
1 CONT She had doubts about her son's legitimacy as Caesar's heir, and attempted to dissuade him from accepting his inheritance. She died during her son's first consulship, in August/September 43 BC. Octavian gave her the highest honours
1 CONC  at her funeral. Philippus later married one of her sisters.
1 CONT
1 CONT A portrait of Atia Balba Caesonia can be seen under Section 4. Parents at [2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Popular culture
1 CONT A highly fictionalized version of Atia is a major character in the HBO/BBC 2 television series Rome. Rather than a pious and loving individual, she is portrayed as a licentious, self-absorbed, and manipulative schemer whose sexual
1 CONC  escapades included Mark Antony.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Atia Balba Tertia
1 CONT Atia Balba Tertia was the mother of Lucius Pinarius.
