0 @N6000000002187597696@ NOTE John Hyrcanus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Hyrcanus (Yohanan Girhan) (reigned 134 BCE - 104 BCE, died 104 BCE) was a Hasmonean (Maccabeean) leader of the 2nd century BC. Apparently the name "Hyrcanus" was taken by him as a reignal name upon his accession to power.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Simon Maccabaeus and hence the nephew of Judas Maccabaeus, Jonathan Maccabaeus and their siblings, whose story is told in the deuterocanonical books of 1 Maccabees and 2 Maccabees, and in the Talmud. John was not
1 CONC  present at a banquet at which his father and his two brothers were murdered, purportedly by his brother-in-law Ptolemy. He attained to his father's former offices, that of high priest and king (although some Jews never accepted a
1 CONC ny of the Hasmoneans as being legitimate kings, as they were not lineal descendants of David).
1 CONT
1 CONT John Hyrcanus apparently combined an energetic and able style of leadership with the zeal of his forebears. He was known as a brave and brilliant military leader. He is credited with the forced conversion of the Idumeans to Judais
1 CONC m, which was unusual for a Jewish leader; Judaism was not typically spread by the sword. He also set out to resolve forcibly the religious dispute between the Jews and the Samaritans; during his reign he destroyed the Samaritan te
1 CONC mple on Mount Gerizim (although their descendants still worship among its ruins), which served further to deepen the already-historic hatred and rivalry between the two groups. Many historians believe that the apocryphal book of J
1 CONC ubilees was written during his reign; some would suggest even at his behest. Some writers, particularly Christian ones, have dated the division of Judaism into the parties of Pharisees and Sadducees to his era; most Jewish writers
1 CONC  and some Christian ones suggest that this split actually well predates him. Some historians would go so far as to identify him, as a priest, predominantly with the Sadducee party, which was closely associated with the Temple wors
1 CONC hip and the priestly class.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Hyrcanus represented in some ways the highest point of the Hasmonean Dynasty. The restored Jewish "kingdom" approached its maximum limits of both territory and prestige. Upon his death, his offices were divided among his heir
1 CONC s; his son Aristobulus succeeded him as high priest; his wife as "Queen regnant". The son, however, soon came to desire the essentially unchecked power of his father; he shortly ordered his mother and his brothers imprisoned. This
1 CONC  event seems to mark the beginning of the decline of the Hasmonean Dynasty; in just over four decades they were removed from power by the Roman Republic and none of them ever began to approach the level of power or prestige that h
1 CONC ad pertained to John Hyrcanus or his predecessors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Simon Hasmonean Ruler and High Priest of Judaea
1 CONT 134–104 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Aristobulus 
0 @N6000000002187597715@ NOTE Aristobulus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT For other people with this name, see Aristobulus (disambiguation) 
1 CONT Aristobulus (reigned 104-103 BC) was a king of the Hebrew Hasmonean Dynasty, and the eldest of the five sons of King John Hyrcanus. He was the first of the Hasmonean rulers to call himself "king." According to the Hebrew Scripture
1 CONC s, only descendants of Judah, or, more specifically, the House of David, were qualified to be kings of Israel. All of Aristobulus' predecessors used the title of "nasi"/"prince".
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the directions of John Hyrcanus, the government of the country after his death was to be placed in the hands of his wife, and Aristobulus was originally to receive only the high-priesthood. He was not however satisfie
1 CONC d with this, so he cast his mother into prison and allowed her to starve there. By this means he came into the possession of the throne, which, however, he did not long enjoy, as after a year's reign he died of a painful illness (
1 CONC 103 BC). He was hostile to the Pharisees and pursued them with ruthlessness.
1 CONT
1 CONT Aristobulus' successor was his eldest brother, Alexander Jannæus, who, together with his two brothers, was freed from prison by Queen Shelomit [Salome] Alexandra, the widow of Aristobulus and sister to Shimon ben Shetach (the lead
1 CONC ing Jewish sage of that time).
0 @N6000000000437066039@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> Iii, Of Armenia
0 @N6000000003949970637@ NOTE Armenian: ?????? ?, Khosrov Ben), the son of Tiridates III, was a member of the Arshakuni Dynasty and the king of Armenia from 330 – 339 AD. He was a man of short stature, thus his name. He founded the city of Dvin which later bec
1 CONC ame the Armenian capital.
1 CONT During his reign, two generals, Vatche Mamikonian and Vahan Amatuni, distinguished themselves for their valor in battle, often coming to help the king. During these years, pro-Sassanid and anti-Mamikonian sentiment grew in Armenia
1 CONC  and so did anti-Roman sentiment. Pro-Sassanid groups gained popularity so much so that they were successful in assassinating Catholicos St. Aristaces I, son of Gregory the Illuminator.
1 CONT Shapur II, Sassanid king of the Persians, invaded Armenia twice and did gain some territory. Vatche Mamikonian was killed in those battles and was later named a saint by the Armenian Apostolic Church for his sacrifice.
0 @N6000000002187821910@ NOTE Bagrat I Bagratuni
1 CONT b. circa 290, #6396
1 CONT Pedigree 
1 CONT       Presiding Prince of the Bagratids.. Sources: 1. Toumanoff, C. 'The Orontids of Armenia' in 'Studies in Christian Caucasian History' (1963) pp.337-342. ; 2. Toumanoff, C. 'The Early Bagratids' in La Museon, (1949), pp.21-54. 
1 CONC Bagrat I Bagratuni was born circa 290. He was the son of Smbat I Bagratuni. Great prince of the aspetut'iwn in 353.1 He was selected by the unanimous consent of the bishops, the king and the ashxarhax, to be a delegate in a group 
1 CONC of nobles to accompany the newly appointed Catholicos, Nerses, to the capital city of Cappadocia, Caesarea in 353.2  
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT      Child of Bagrat I Bagratuni: 
1 CONT   Smbat II Bagratuni+   b. c 315, d. a 374 
1 CONT
1 CONT [S327] Robert Bedrosian (translator), editor, P'awstos Buzandac'i's The History of the Armenians (23 Washington Street, Long Branch, New Jersey 07740-5931: Sources of the Armenian Tradition, 1985), pg. 86. Hereinafter cited as HoA
1 CONC  : Buzandac'i's (5th C). 
1 CONT [S327] Robert Bedrosian (translator), HoA : Buzandac'i's (5th C), Ch. 1
0 @N6000000002187726290@ NOTE Abgar II of Osroene
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Abgar II (ruled 68–53 BC) was an Arab prince of Edessa in Osroene (today part of eastern Turkey). In 64 BC He sided with the Romans helping Pompey's legate Lucius Afranius when the latter occupied northern Mesopotamia, but it is a
1 CONC lleged that he helped to betray Marcus Crassus by leading him out onto an open plain, resulting in 53 BC in the Battle of Carrhae, which destroyed an entire Roman army. What is certain is that he gained no benefits from the battle
1 CONC , since shortly after he was deposed by Orodes II in a move to strengthen Parthian contol over the region. He is called Augarus by Dio Cassius1, Acbarus the phylarch of the Arabians in the Parthian history ascribed to Appian2, and
1 CONC  Ariamnes by Plutarch.3
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Han J. Drijvers & John F. Healey; The Old Syriac Inscriptions of Edessa and Osrhoene (1999) 
1 CONT Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Abgarus (1)", Boston, (1867) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 Dio Cassius, Roman History, xl. 20 
1 CONT 2 Appian, The Foreign Wars, "The Parthian Wars" 
1 CONT 3 Plutarch, Lives, "Crassus", 21 
0 @N6000000003949970843@ NOTE Abgar II (ruled 68–53 BC) was an Arab prince of Edessa in Osroene (today part of eastern Turkey). In 64 BC He sided with the Romans helping Pompey's legate Lucius Afranius when the latter occupied northern Mesopotamia, but it is a
1 CONC lleged that he helped to betray Marcus Crassus by leading him out onto an open plain, resulting in 53 BC in the Battle of Carrhae, which destroyed an entire Roman army. What is certain is that he gained no benefits from the battle
1 CONC , since shortly after he was deposed by Orodes II in a move to strengthen Parthian contol over the region. He is called Augarus by Dio Cassius1, Acbarus the phylarch of the Arabians in the Parthian history ascribed to Appian2, and
1 CONC  Ariamnes by Plutarch.3
1 CONT References:  Han J. Drijvers & John F. Healey; The Old Syriac Inscriptions of Edessa and Osrhoene (1999)    Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Abgarus (1)", Boston, (1867) 
0 @N6000000002187705548@ NOTE Ninurta-apal-Ekur
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ninurta-apal-Ekur was a King of Assyria. There is some dispute as to how long he reigned, based on disputes among various copies of the Assyrian King List. The Nassouhi King List, sometimes considered to be older than the other ve
1 CONC rsions of the King List we have, gives him 13 years of reign, but the other king lists give him only 3. More recent scholarship has tended to support the shorter reign, in which case he reigned from 1182 to 1179 BC (alternately, h
1 CONC e reigned from 1193 to 1180 BC). He was a usurper, the great-great-great grandson of Adad-nirari I. He seized the throne from Enlil-kudurri-usur, a son of the powerful king Tukulti-Ninurta I. After his reign of either three or thi
1 CONC rteen years, Ninurta-apal-Ekur was succeeded by his son, Ashur-dan I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Enlil-kudurri-usur King of Assyria
1 CONT 1182–1179 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashur-dan I 
0 @N6000000002188286992@ NOTE Lucius Scribonius Libo
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT There were three men of plebs status called Lucius Scribonius Libo who lived in the Roman Republic and Roman Empire who were members of the gens Scribonia:
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo (flourished 1st century BC) was a member of a Roman Senatorial family. Lucius had married Cornelia Sulla, the daughter of Pompeia Magna (daughter of triumvir Pompey from his third marriage to Mucia Tertia) a
1 CONC nd senator Faustus Cornelius Sulla (son of dictator Lucius Cornelius Sulla). Cornelia bore Lucius two children: a daughter called Scribonia (the second wife of future Roman Emperor Augustus) and a son of the same name; 
1 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo - (flourished 1st century BC) son to the above. He was the maternal uncle to consul Publius Cornelius Scipio, Cornelia Scipio and Julia the Elder. Lucius served as a consul of 34 BC. Lucius had died after hi
1 CONC s consulship. His wife was a member of the gens Sulpicius, the family that the Roman Emperor Galba, had descended from his paternal side. Lucius and wife had three children, two sons: Lucius Scribonius Libo (below) and Marcus Scri
1 CONC bonius Libo Drusus and a daughter Scribonia (she married her great great uncle the political rebel Sextus Pompeius. He was the maternal uncle of her paternal grandmother). Their sons had died from violent deaths during Roman Emper
1 CONC or Tiberius' first treason trials in 16. 
1 CONT Lucius Scribonius Libo - (flourished 1st century BC & 1st century, died 16) son of the above. He was a consul in 16. This nobleman had planned to revolt against the Roman Emperor Tiberius. The Emperor had tried him in a Senatorial
1 CONC  Court. Lucius had pleaded to the Emperor for the support of Tiberius‘ son Julius Caesar Drusus but the emperor rejected this. Lucius and Tiberius took part in a sacrifice among the priests. During the ceremony, the Emperor had as
1 CONC ked Lucius for assistance, when the ceremony was over, Tiberius stabbed him with a knife. This occurred after the trial. Lucius had married Cornelia Pompeia Magna a distant relative, who was the daughter of Pompeia Magna from her 
1 CONC second marriage to consul Lucius Cornelius Cinna. Pompeia bore Lucius, a daughter and only child Scribonia. Scribonia married the consul Marcus Licinius Crassus Frugi and had children. 
0 @N6000000002187718211@ NOTE Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (triumvir)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT This page is about the contemporary of Caesar Augustus. For other generations of his family see Marcus Aemilius Lepidus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marcus Aemilius Lepidus (Latin: M·AEMILIVS·M·F·Q·N·LEPIDVS¹), died 13 BC, was a patrician Roman politician of the 1st century BC. Known simply as Lepidus he was the son of Marcus Aemilius Lepidus and Appuleia, the daughter of the 
1 CONC rebellious Lucius Appuleius Saturninus. His father was also involved in a rebellion against the Republic and died because of it. He was a member of the second triumvirate and pontifex maximus. Lepidus was among Julius Caesar's gre
1 CONC atest supporters. He started his cursus honorum as a praetor in 49 BC, and was rewarded with the consulship in 47 BC, after the defeat of the Pompeians in the East. Following Caesar's assassination in March 15, 44 BC (the Ides of 
1 CONC March), Lepidus allied himself with Mark Antony in a joint bid for power. But Caesar had left an heir: Octavian, his great-nephew and adopted son, had a matching ambition and the popularity with which to climb the hierarchy. Toget
1 CONC her they formed the second triumvirate, legalized with the name of Triumvirs for the Organization of the People by the Lex Titia of 43 BC. The triumvirate had a legal life span of five years and was renewed in 38 BC with the Treat
1 CONC y of Tarentum for an equal period of time.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the pacification of the East and the defeat of the assassins faction, during which Lepidus remained in Rome, he went to rule the western provinces of Hispania and Africa. Somehow, Lepidus managed to remain aside the frequent
1 CONC  quarrels between his colleagues Antony and Octavian. However, in 36 BC, an ill-judged political move was the excuse that Octavian needed. Lepidus was accused of usurping power in Sicily and attempt of rebellion and was forced to 
1 CONC exile. All of his offices were taken away, except the one of pontifex maximus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lepidus died peacefully in 13 BC. Lepidus was the brother of Lucius Paullus and father to Lepidus the Younger.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1- English: "Marcus Aemilius Lepidus, son of Marcus, nephew of Quintus".
0 @N6000000000437011702@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Praetor Of Rome
0 @N6000000000437364882@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> Praetor Of Rome
0 @N6000000002187712744@ NOTE Marcus Atius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Marcus Atius Balbus (105 - 51BC) was the son and heir of an elder Balbus (148 - 87 BC) and Pompeia Strabo. Strabo was sister to Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo, father of Pompey the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT The family of the elder Balbus came from a senatorial family from Aricia (modern Ariccia, Italy). In villas, relatives would display masks in memory of their ancestors.
1 CONT
1 CONT Balbus married Julia Caesaris, sister of Julius Caesar. They had 3 daughters Atia Balba Prima, Atia Balba Caesonia (mother of Caesar Augustus) and Atia Balba Tertia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Balbus served as a praetor and under Caesar was appointed as a commissioner under Julian Law, to divide the estates in Campania among the commoners.
0 @N6000000003319677691@ NOTE
1 CONT wtf 4:565 Marcus Atius Balbus
