0 @N6000000002188289207@ NOTE Pompey
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gnaeus Pompeius Magnus 
1 CONT
1 CONT Born September 29, 106 BC
1 CONT Rome 
1 CONT Died September 28, 48 BC
1 CONT Egypt
1 CONT
1 CONT Occupation Politician and military commander 
1 CONT Spouse Antistia
1 CONT Aemilia Scaura
1 CONT Mucia Tertia
1 CONT Julia
1 CONT Cornelia Metella 
1 CONT Pompey, Pompey the Great or Pompey the Triumvir [1] (Classical Latin abbreviation: CN·POMPEIVS·CN·F·SEX·N·MAGNVS[2], Gnaeus or Cnaeus Pompeius Magnus) (September 29, 106 BC–September 28, 48 BC), was a distinguished military and po
1 CONC litical leader of the late Roman Republic. Hailing from an Italian provincial background, after military triumphs he established a place for himself in the ranks of Roman nobility, and was given the cognomen of Magnus—the Great—by
1 CONC  Lucius Cornelius Sulla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pompey was a rival of Marcus Licinius Crassus and an ally to Gaius Julius Caesar. The three politicians dominated the Late Roman republic through a political alliance called the First Triumvirate. After the death of Crassus, Pompe
1 CONC y and Caesar became rivals, disputing the leadership of the Roman state in what is now called Caesar's civil war. Pompey fought on the side of the Optimates, the conservative faction in the Roman Senate, until he was defeated by C
1 CONC aesar. He then sought refuge in Egypt, where he was assassinated.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life and political debut 
1 CONT 2 Sicily and Africa 
1 CONT 3 Quintus Sertorius and Spartacus 
1 CONT 4 Rome's new frontier on the East 
1 CONT 4.1 Campaign against the Pirates 
1 CONT 4.2 Pompey in the East 
1 CONT 5 Pompey’s return to Rome 
1 CONT 6 Caesar and the First Triumvirate 
1 CONT 7 Confrontation to war 
1 CONT 8 Civil War and assassination 
1 CONT 9 Historic view 
1 CONT 10 Marriages and offspring 
