0 @N6000000002187712560@ NOTE Fulvia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Fulvia (died 40 BC) was a Roman matron remembered by her ambition and political activity, in a time when women were expected to stay home and live with virtue and modesty, according to Roman morals. She was the first non mythologi
1 CONC cal woman represented in Roman coins.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulvia was born in the 1st century BC in an uncertain date. She was daughter of Marcus Fulvius Flaccus Bambulus (a member of a senatorial family) and Sempronia, daughter of Gaius Gracchus. Her maternal great-grandmother was Cornel
1 CONC ia Africana, who lived her life according to all the traditions that Fulvia was about to break. As heiress to the Gracchi estate, after the death of her grandfather and Tiberius Gracchus, Fulvia was a very wealthy woman. Her famil
1 CONC y was not patrician but highly respected by Roman elite.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her first husband was Publius Clodius Pulcher, a demagogue politician famous for causing instability in Rome's internal affairs, often involved in conspiracies and known to resort to violence. It is said that Fulvia financially su
1 CONC pported her husband's career and inspired most of his actions. Clodius was (killed by Milo in a battle that erupted between these sworn enemies and their retainers in a chance encounter at Bovillae outside Rome) in 52 BC, leaving 
1 CONC Fulvia a widow. Not for long. Afterwards, she married Gaius Scribonius Curio, an influential and talented tribune whose defection to Caesar in exchange for an enormous bribe swung the balance in Caesar's favor in his struggle with
1 CONC  the Senate in 50 BC. At the outbreak of the Civil War Caesar entrusted Curio with an expedition to conquer Africa, but through overconfidence he allowed himself to be outmaneuvered by King Juba and he and his troops were annihila
1 CONC ted--the only serious defeat suffered by Caesar's forces during the Civil War.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulvia's own political career started with her third marriage, to Mark Antony. Plutarch said that she needed husbands with an active political profile and the ambitious Antony was highly qualified. As Clodius had done previously, 
1 CONC Antony was happy to accept her money to boost his career.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following Julius Caesar's assassination in March 15, 44 BC, Antony formed the second triumvirate with Octavian and Lepidus and embarked on a savage proscription. To solidify the political alliance, Fulvia offered her daughter, Clo
1 CONC dia, to young Octavian as wife. Antony pursued his political enemies, chief among them being Marcus Tullius Cicero, who had criticized him openly for abusing his powers as consul after Caesar's assassination. In the proscription, 
1 CONC Antony dispatched search parties to Cicero's country homes to track him down. He was found and beheaded by a Roman centurion, Herennius, whom Cicero had previously defended successfully in a murder trial, after his whereabouts wer
1 CONC e revealed by a young slave to whom Cicero had shown special favor. Antony exhibited Cicero's head and hands at the rostra in the Forum.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulvia was happy to take revenge against Cicero for Antony's sake, but also in revenge for Publius Clodius Pulcher, her first husband, also an earlier victim of Cicero's sharp rhetoric. Plutarch and other sources describe the joy 
1 CONC with which she pierced the tongue of the dead Cicero with her golden hairpins, as a final revenge against Cicero's power of speech.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shortly afterwards, triumvirs then distributed the provinces among them. Lepidus took the west and Antony went to the province of Egypt, where he met Cleopatra. Octavian remained in Italy, where he was busy taking lands from Itali
1 CONC ans and giving them to the triumvirate veterans.
1 CONT
1 CONT These actions caused political and social unrest, but when Octavian asked for a divorce from Clodia, Fulvia herself decided to take action. Together with Lucius Antonius, her brother-in-law, she raised eight legions in Italy to fi
1 CONC ght for Antony's rights against Octavian. The army occupied Rome for a short time, but eventually retreated to Perusia (modern Perugia). Octavian besieged Fulvia and Lucius Antonius in the winter of 41 - 40 BC, starving them into 
1 CONC surrender. Fulvia was exiled to Sicyon, where she died of a sudden illness, while Antony was en route to meet her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her death opened a space for Octavian and Antony to reconcile. Now a widower, Antony married Octavian's sister Octavia. Later it would be Octavia who took care of Fulvia's children.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Fulvia's marriages and descendants
1 CONT First marriage, Publius Clodius Pulcher 
1 CONT Clodia Pulchra, briefly married to Octavian 
1 CONT Second marriage, Gaius Scribonius Curio 
1 CONT Third marriage, Mark Antony 
1 CONT Marcus Antonius Antyllus, executed by Octavian in 31 BC 
1 CONT Iullus Antonius 
1 CONT See also: Women in Rome - Scipio-Paullus-Gracchus family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT  This biographical article related to the military is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT   This feminism-related article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
0 @N6000000002188289047@ NOTE Lucius Cornelius Sulla
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Dictator of the Roman Republic 
1 CONT In office
1 CONT 82 or 81 BC – 81 BC 
1 CONT Preceded by Publius Sulpicius Galba Maximus in 203 BC 
1 CONT Succeeded by Gaius Julius Caesar in 49 BC 
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Consul of the Roman Republic 
1 CONT In office
1 CONT 88 BC – 88 BC 
1 CONT Preceded by Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo and Lucius Porcius Cato 
1 CONT Succeeded by Lucius Cornelius Cinna and Gnaeus Octavius 
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Consul of the Roman Republic 
1 CONT In office
1 CONT 80 BC – 80 BC 
1 CONT Preceded by Gnaeus Cornelius Dolabella and Marcus Tullius Decula 
1 CONT Succeeded by Appius Claudius Pulcher and Publius Servilius Vatia 
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Born ca. 138 BC
1 CONT Rome 
1 CONT Died 78 BC
1 CONT Puteoli 
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla Felix (Latin: L•CORNELIVS•L•F•P•N•SVLLA•FELIX)[1] (c. 138 BC – 78 BC), usually known simply as Sulla, was a Roman general, consul and dictator.
1 CONT
1 CONT A gifted and effective general, Sulla marched his armies on Rome twice; enjoying the absolute power of a dictator. Though he resigned his complete command of the Republic, he is often seen as having hastened the end of the Republi
1 CONC c by his example.
1 CONT
1 CONT His character was once described as being half fox, and half lion, due to his perceived cunning and bravery; Machiavelli would later allude to this description of Sulla in outlining the most desirable characteristics of a prince.[
1 CONC 2]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla was described by ancient sources as having golden-reddish hair, and piercing gray eyes that could intimidate all but the sternest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 1.1 Early years 
1 CONT 1.1.1 Africa and the capture of Jugurtha 
1 CONT 1.1.2 Cimbri and the Teutones 
1 CONT 1.2 Praetorship and Cilician governorship 
1 CONT 1.3 Social War 
1 CONT 1.3.1 Grass Crown 
1 CONT 1.4 Consul and first march on Rome 
1 CONT 1.5 First Mithridatic War and the siege of Athens 
1 CONT 1.5.1 Battle of Chaeronea 
1 CONT 1.5.2 Battle of Orchomenos 
1 CONT 1.6 Second March on Rome 
1 CONT 1.7 Dictator of Rome 
1 CONT 1.8 Retirement 
1 CONT 2 Sulla's legacy 
1 CONT 3 The name "Sulla" 
1 CONT 4 Marriages and issue 
1 CONT 5 Chronology 
1 CONT 6 References 
1 CONT 7 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early years
1 CONT Sulla was born into a branch of the patrician Cornelii gens, but his family had fallen to an impoverished condition at the time of his birth. Lacking ready money, Sulla spent his youth amongst Rome’s low-lifes—comics, actors, lute
1 CONC -players and dancers—at which time he met the Roman actor Metrobius, whom Plutarch describes as a female impersonator. Sulla remained romantically attached to him throughout his life, and even when both were quite old, he never at
1 CONC tempted to conceal this sentimental attachment[3].
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite his early waywardness, it seems certain that Sulla received a good education. He was fluent in Greek, a sure sign in Rome of an educated man, and Sallust declares him well-read and intelligent. The means by which Sulla att
1 CONC ained the fortune which later would enable him to ascend the ladder of Roman politics, the Cursus honorum, are not clear, although Plutarch refers to two inheritances; one from his stepmother and the other from a low-born but rich
1 CONC  unmarried lady[4].
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Africa and the capture of Jugurtha
1 CONT In 107 BC, Sulla was nominated quaestor to Gaius Marius, who had been elected consul for that year. Marius was taking control of the Roman army in the war against King Jugurtha of Numidia in northern Africa.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Jugurthine War had started in earlier 112 BC, but Roman legions under Quintus Caecilius Metellus had been taking what was seen as an excessive amount of time. Gaius Marius, a lieutenant and client of Metellus, saw an opportuni
1 CONC ty to usurp his commander and fed rumors of incompetence and delay to the publicanii (merchants) in the region. These machinations caused calls for Metellus's removal; despite delaying tactics by Metellus, Marius returned to Rome 
1 CONC to stand for the consulship and took over the campaign.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla Capturing JugurthaUnder Marius, the Roman forces followed a very similar plan as under Metellus and ultimately defeated the Numidians in 106 BC, thanks in large part to Sulla's initiative in capturing the Numidian king. He h
1 CONC ad persuaded King Bocchus of Mauretania, a nearby kingdom, to betray Jugurtha, who had fled to Mauretania for refuge. It was a dangerous operation from the first, with King Bocchus weighing up the advantages of handing Jugurtha ov
1 CONC er to Sulla or Sulla over to Jugurtha[5]. The publicity attracted by this feat boosted Sulla's political career. Much to the annoyance of Marius, a gilded equestrian statue of Sulla donated by King Bocchus was erected in the Forum
1 CONC  to commemorate his accomplishment.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Cimbri and the Teutones
1 CONT The next threat to Rome proved to be much more serious. In 104 BC the migrating Germanic tribes of the Cimbri and the Teutones seemed headed for Italy. Sulla continued to serve on Marius' staff during this campaign. Due to the imm
1 CONC ediate threat facing the city, Marius was elected Consul an unprecedented 5 years in a row. Finally, with his consular colleague Catulus, the Roman forces faced the tribes at the battle of Vercellae in 101 BC. Sulla had by this ti
1 CONC me transferred to the army of Quintus Lutatius Catulus Caesar. Sulla is credited as being the prime mover in the defeat of the tribes (Catulus being a hopeless general and quite incapable of cooperating with Marius). Marius and Ca
1 CONC tulus were both granted triumphs as the co-commanding generals.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The migrations of cimbri and teutones.
1 CONT [edit] Praetorship and Cilician governorship
1 CONT Returning to Rome, Sulla was elected 'Praetor urbanus' in 97 BC. According to rumour, this was done through massive bribery. The next year he was appointed pro consule to the province of Cilicia (in Anatolia). While in the East, S
1 CONC ulla was the first Roman magistrate to meet a Parthian ambassador, Orobazus, and by taking the seat between the Parthian ambassador and the ambassador from Pontus (the center seat being the place of honour), he sealed, perhaps uni
1 CONC ntentionally, the Parthian ambassador's fate. Orobazus was executed upon his return to Parthia for allowing Sulla to outmaneuver him. It was at this meeting he was told by a Chaldean seer that he would die at the height of his fam
1 CONC e and fortune. This prophecy was to have a powerful hold on Sulla throughout his life. Later around 93 BC Sulla left the East and returned to Rome, where he aligned himself with the Optimates in opposition to Gaius Marius. In 92 B
1 CONC C Sulla repulsed Tigranes the Great of Armenia from Cappadocia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Social War
1 CONT The Social War (91–88 BC) was fought against the Socii, Rome's Italian allies, and resulted from Rome's intransigence in regarding the civil liberties of its own citizens (Romans) as superior to those of citizens of the rest of It
1 CONC aly. Subjects of the Roman Republic, these Italian provincials might be called to arms in its defence or might be subjected to extraordinary taxes, but they had no say in the expenditure of these taxes or in the uses of the armies
1 CONC  that might be raised in their territories. The Social War was, in part, caused by the assassination of Marcus Livius Drusus the Younger. His reforms were intended to grant Roman Citizenship to their allies, which would have given
1 CONC  these "provincials" (a provincial Roman) a say in the external and internal policies of the Roman Republic. When Drusus was assassinated, most of his reforms addressing these grievances were declared invalid. This greatly angered
1 CONC  the Roman provincials, and in consequence, most allied against Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the beginning of the Social War, the Roman aristocracy and Senate were beginning to fear Gaius Marius's ambition, which had already given him 5 consulships in a row, from 104 BC to 100 BC. They were determined that he should no
1 CONC t have overall command of the war in Italy. In this last rebellion of the Italian allies, Sulla served with brilliance as a general. He outshone both Marius and the consul Gnaeus Pompeius Strabo (the father of Pompey Magnus). For 
1 CONC example, in 89 BC Sulla captured Aeclanum, the chief town of Hirpini, by setting the wooden breastwork on fire. As a result of his success in bringing the Social War to a successful conclusion, he was elected consul for the first 
1 CONC time in 88 BC, with Quintus Pompeius Rufus (soon his daughter's father-in-law) as his colleague.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Grass Crown
1 CONT Sulla served not only with brilliance as a general during the Social War, but also with immense personal bravery. At Nola he was awarded a Corona Obsidionalis ("Obsidional or Blockade Crown"), also known as a Corona Graminea ("Gra
1 CONC ss Crown"), the highest Roman military honor, awarded for personal bravery to a commanding general that saves a Roman legion or army in the field. Unlike all other Roman military honors, it was awarded by acclamation of the soldie
1 CONC rs of the rescued army, and consequently very few were ever awarded. The crown, by tradition, was woven from grasses and other plants taken from the actual battlefield. [6]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Consul and first march on Rome
1 CONT Further information: Sulla's first civil war 
1 CONT As the consul of Rome, Sulla prepared to depart once more for the East, to fight the first Mithridatic War, by the appointment of the Senate. But he would leave trouble behind him. Marius was now an old man, but he still had the a
1 CONC mbition to lead the Roman armies against King Mithridates VI of Pontus. Marius convinced the tribune Publius Sulpicius Rufus to call an assembly and revert the Senate's decision on Sulla's command. Sulpicius also used the assembli
1 CONC es to eject Senators from the Roman Senate until there were not enough senators to form a quorum. Violence in the Forum ensued, and the efforts of the nobles to effect a public lynching similar to that which had happened to the br
1 CONC others Gracchi and Saturninus were smashed by the gladitatorial bodyguard of Sulpicius. Sulla was forced to take refuge in Marius's house, and possibly made a personal plea to stop the violence, which was ignored. Sulla's own son-
1 CONC in-law was killed in those riots.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla fled Rome and went to the camp of his victorious Social War veterans, waiting to cross to Greece from the south of Italy. He announced the measures that had been taken against him, and his soldiers stoned the envoys of the a
1 CONC ssemblies who came to announce that the command of the Mithridatic War had been transferred to Marius. Sulla then took six of his most loyal legions and marched on Rome. This was an unprecedented event. No general before him had e
1 CONC ver crossed the city limits, the pomoerium, with his army. It was so unethical that most of his commanders (with the exception of one, Lucullus) refused to accompany him. Sulla justified his actions on the grounds that the Senate 
1 CONC had been neutered and the mos maiorum ("The way things were done", which amounted to a Roman constitution though none of it was codified as such) had been offended by the Senate's negation of the rights of the year's consuls to fi
1 CONC ght the year's wars. Armed gladiators were unable to resist organized Roman soldiers; and although Marius offered freedom to any slave that would fight with him against Sulla, he and his followers were forced to flee the city.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla consolidated his position, declared Marius and his allies hostis, and addressed the Senate in harsh tones, portraying himself as a victim, presumably to justify his violent entrance into the city. After restructuring the cit
1 CONC y's politics and strengthening the Senate's power, Sulla returned to his camp and proceeded with the original plan of fighting Mithridates in Pontus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulpicius was betrayed and killed by one of his slaves, whom Sulla subsequently freed and then executed. Marius, however, fled to safety in Africa. With Sulla out of Rome, Marius plotted his return. During his period of exile Mari
1 CONC us became determined that he would hold a seventh consulship, as foretold by the Sybil decades earlier. By the end of 87 BC Marius returned to Rome with the support of Lucius Cornelius Cinna and, in Sulla's absence, took control o
1 CONC f the city. Marius declared Sulla's reforms and laws invalid and officially exiled Sulla. Marius and Cinna were elected consuls for the year 86 BC. Marius died a fortnight after, and Cinna was left in sole control of Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] First Mithridatic War and the siege of Athens
1 CONT Main article: First Mithridatic War
1 CONT
1 CONT Asia Minor just before the first mithridatic warIn the spring of 87 BC Sulla landed at Dyrrachium, Greece. Asia was occupied by the forces of Mithridates under the command of Archelaus. Sulla’s first target was Athens, ruled by a 
1 CONC Mithridatic puppet; the tyrant Aristion. Sulla moved southeast, picking up supplies and reinforcements as he went. Sulla’s chief of staff was Lucullus, who went ahead of him to scout the way and negotiate with Bruttius Sura, the e
1 CONC xisting Roman commander in Greece. After speaking with Lucullus, Sura handed over the command of his troops to Sulla. At Chaeronea, ambassadors from all the major cities of Greece (except Athens) met with Sulla, who impressed on t
1 CONC hem the Rome's determination to drive Mithridates from Greece and Asia Province. Sulla then advanced on Athens.
1 CONT
1 CONT On arrival, Sulla threw up a siege encompassing not only Athens but also the port of Piraeus. At the time Archelaus had command of the sea, so Sulla sent Lucullus to raise a fleet from the remaining Roman allies in the eastern Med
1 CONC iterranean. His first objective was Piraeus, as without it Athens could not be re-supplied. Huge earthworks were raised, isolating Athens and its port from the land side. Sulla needed wood, so he cut down everything, including the
1 CONC  sacred groves of Greece, up to 100 miles from Athens. When more money was needed he “borrowed” from temples and Sybils alike. The currency minted from this treasure was to remain in circulation for centuries and prized for its qu
1 CONC ality.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite the complete encirclement of Athens and its port, and several attempts by Archelaus to raise the siege, a stalemate seemed to have developed. Sulla, however, patiently bided his time. Soon Sulla's camp was to fill with ref
1 CONC ugees from Rome, fleeing the massacres of Marius and Cinna. These also included his wife and children, as well as those of the Optimate party who had not been killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Athens by now was starving, and grain was at famine levels in price. Inside the city, the population was reduced to eating shoe leather and grass. A delegation from Athens was sent to treat with Sulla, but instead of serious negot
1 CONC iations they expounded on the glory of their city. Sulla sent them away saying: “I was sent to Athens, not to take lessons, but to reduce rebels to obedience.”
1 CONT
1 CONT His spies then informed him that Aristion was neglecting the Heptachalcum. Sulla immediately sent sappers to undermine the wall. Nine hundred feet of wall was brought down between the Sacred and Piraeic gates on the southwest side
1 CONC  of the city. A midnight sack of Athens began, and after the taunts of Aristion, Sulla was not in a mood to be magnanimous. Blood literally flowed in the streets, it was only after the entreaties of a couple of his Greek friends (
1 CONC Midias and Calliphon) and the pleas of the Roman Senators in his camp that Sulla decided enough was enough. He then concentrated his forces on the Port of Pireaus and Archelaus, seeing his hopeless situation, withdrew to the citad
1 CONC el and then abandoned the port to join up with his forces under the command of Taxiles. Sulla, as yet not having a fleet, was powerless to prevent Archelaus’ escape. Before leaving Athens, he burnt the port to the ground. Sulla th
1 CONC en advanced into Boeotia to take on Archelaus's armies and remove them from Greece.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Battle of Chaeronea
1 CONT Main article: Battle of Chaeronea (86 BC)
1 CONT Sulla lost no time in moving to intercept the Pontic army and moved to occupy a hill called Philoboetus that branched off Mount Parnassus. It overlooked the Elatean plain and had plentiful supplies of wood and water. The army of A
1 CONC rchelaus, presently commanded by Taxiles, had to approach from the north and proceed along the valley towards Chaeronea. Over 120,000 strong, it outnumbered Sulla's forces by at least 3 to 1. Archelaus was in favor of a policy of 
1 CONC attrition with the Roman forces, but Taxiles had orders from Mithridates to attack at once. In the meantime, Sulla got his men digging. Next, Sulla occupied the ruined city of Parapotamii. It was impregnable and commanded the ford
1 CONC s on the road to Chaeronea. He then made a move that looked to Archelaus like a retreat. He abandoned the fords and moved in behind an entrenched palisade. Behind the palisade were the field artillery from the siege of Athens. Arc
1 CONC helaus advanced across the fords and tried to outflank Sulla’s men, only to be hurled back on the Mithridatic right wing, causing even more confusion. Archelaus’s chariots then charged the Roman center, only to be destroyed on the
1 CONC  palisades. Next came the phalanxes: they too found the palisades impassable, added to the fact that they were receiving withering fire from the Roman field artillery. Next Archelaus flung his right wing at the Roman left; Sulla, 
1 CONC seeing the danger of this maneuver, raced over from the Roman right wing to help. Sulla stabilized the situation, at which point Archelaus flung in more troops from his right flank. This destabilized the Pontic army, giving it a s
1 CONC lew towards its right flank. Sulla dashed back to his own right wing and ordered the general advance. The legions, supported by cavalry, dashed forward and Archelaus’ army folded in on itself, like closing a pack of cards. The sla
1 CONC ughter was terrible, and some reports estimate that only 10,000 men of Mithridates' original army survived. Chaeronea was one of the great battles of history: Sulla had defeated a vastly superior force in terms of numbers; it was 
1 CONC also the first recorded time that battlefield entrenchments were used.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Battle of Orchomenos
1 CONT Main article: Battle of Orchomenos
1 CONT The government of Rome (i.e., Cinna) then sent out Lucius Valerius Flaccus with an army to relieve Sulla of command in the east. Flaccus had been given as second in command a certain Gaius Flavius Fimbria, an individual that histo
1 CONC ry records had few virtues. (He was to eventually agitate against his commanding officer and incite the troops to murder Flaccus). In the meantime, the two Roman armies camped next to each other; and Sulla, not for the first time,
1 CONC  encouraged his soldiers to spread dissension among Flaccus’ army. Many deserted to Sulla before Flaccus packed up and moved on north to threaten Mithridates’ northern dominions. In the meantime, Sulla moved to intercept the new P
1 CONC ontic army. He chose the site of the battle to come—Orchomenos. Not only was it a natural place for a smaller army to meet a much larger one, due to its natural defenses, but it afforded Sulla the ideal terrain to expand on his in
1 CONC novative use of entrenchment. This time the Pontic army was in excess of 150,000, and it encamped itself in front of the busy Roman army, next to a large lake. It soon dawned on Archelaus what Sulla was up to. Sulla had not only b
1 CONC een digging trenches but also dykes, and before long he had the Pontic army in deep trouble. Desperate sallies by the Pontic forces were repulsed by the Romans and the dykes moved onward. On the second day, Archeleus made a determ
1 CONC ined effort to escape Sulla’s web of dykes—the entire Pontic army was hurled at the Romans—but the Roman legionaries were pressed together so tightly that their short swords were like an impenetrable barrier, through which the ene
1 CONC my could not escape. The battle turned into a rout; and once again the slaughter was on an immense scale. Plutarch notes that two hundred years later, armor and weapons from the battle were still being found. The battle of Orchome
1 CONC nos was another of the world's decisive battles. It determined that the fate of Asia Minor lay with Rome and her successors for the next millennium.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Second March on Rome
1 CONT Determined to regain control of Rome, Sulla returned to Italy. With the support of Metellus Pius and others, Sulla's armies marched up Italy from the port of Brundisium. He chased the remnants of the Marians, led by Gaius Marius t
1 CONC he Younger, into Praeneste and bottled them up. Shortly afterwards, following a mad-dash march to Rome, Sulla's army defeated the Samnite forces of Pontius Telesinus in November, 82 BC, at the Battle of Colline Gate. The strength 
1 CONC of the right wing, commanded by Marcus Licinius Crassus, proved crucial in securing victory. Sulla also had the aid of the young Pompey, who defeated Gnaeus Papirius Carbo's supporters in Sicily and Africa.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Dictator of Rome
1 CONT
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla - a denarius portrait issued by his grandson. Look at the moustache he's wearing; this was very unusual in Roman times.At the end of 82 BC or the beginning of 81 BC, Sulla was appointed dictator, rei publica
1 CONC e constituendae causa, by the Senate and subsequently ratified by the "Assembly of the People", with no limit on time in office. Sulla had total control of the city and empire of Rome, except for Spain (which Marius's general Quin
1 CONC tus Sertorius had established as an independent state). This unusual honour (used hitherto only in times of extreme danger to the city, such as the Second Punic War, and only for 6 month periods) represented an exception to Rome's
1 CONC  policy of not giving total power to a single individual. Sulla can be seen as setting the precedent for Julius Caesar's dictatorship, and the eventual end of the Republic under Augustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT In total control of the city and its affairs, Sulla instituted a reign of terror, akin to and in response to, that which Marius and Cinna had implemented while they were in control during Sulla's absence. Proscribing or outlawing 
1 CONC every one of his political opponents, Sulla ordered some 1,500 Roman nobles (i.e., senators and equites) executed, although it is estimated that as many as 9,000 people were killed[7]. The bloodbath went on for several months. Rom
1 CONC ans were executed for any reason or none at all. Helping or sheltering a person who was proscribed was also punishable by death. The State confiscated the wealth of the outlawed and then auctioned it off, making Sulla and his supp
1 CONC orters vastly rich. (One option commonly taken by those who had been proscribed was suicide, which under Roman law allowed their property to be inherited by their families, and not as spoils to Sulla.) The sons and grandsons of th
1 CONC e proscribed were banned from future political office, a restriction not removed for over 30 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT The young Caesar, as Cinna's son-in-law, was one of Sulla's targets and fled the city. He was saved through the efforts of his relatives, many of whom were Sulla's supporters, but Sulla noted in his memoirs that he regretted spari
1 CONC ng Caesar's life, because of the young man's notorious ambition. The historian Suetonius records that when agreeing to spare Caesar, Sulla warned those who were pleading his case that he would become a danger to them in the future
1 CONC , saying "In this Caesar there are many a Marius."
1 CONT
1 CONT Only Quintus Sertorius, the last supporter of Marius, held out against Sulla's armies under Metellus Pius in distant Hispania.
1 CONT
1 CONT Without any political obstacle, Sulla enacted a series of reforms to put control of the State firmly in the hands of the Senate. He arranged that the number of senators be doubled from 300 to 600 and that membership was automatic 
1 CONC on election to the office of quaestor instead of at the discretion of the censors. He also increased the number of quaestors from eight to twenty and the number of praetors from six to eight, ensuring the numbers in the Senate and
1 CONC  giving them a larger base of experienced politicians to draw from. He regulated the courts and re-passed a law regulating the cursus honorum, giving specific ages for each magistracy, and forbidding the repetition of any office w
1 CONC ithin ten years. He also reduced the tribunes' political power by restricting their veto and blocking them from further office, and limited the Assembly's ability to pass laws without the Senate's approval. This was to ensure that
1 CONC  no man who had real political ambition would run for the tribunate. Sulla remembered the tribunates of the Gracchi and how they had caused upheaval throughout the Roman world with their radical policies. Now, only second-rate pol
1 CONC iticians would want to run for the office of tribune, securing the preeminence of the Senate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, in a demonstration of his absolute power, he expanded the "Pomerium", the sacred boundary of Rome, untouched since the time of the kings. In this act, Sulla was trying to invoke the memory of Servius Tullius, the last man
1 CONC  believed to have performed this ceremony. Servius Tullius had done much to shape the Roman constitution, and Sulla was comparing himself to this great leader. Many of Sulla's reforms looked to the past (often re-passing former la
1 CONC ws), but he also regulated for the future, particularly in his redefinition of maiestas (treason) laws.
1 CONT
1 CONT Near the end of 81 BC, Sulla resigned his dictatorship, disbanded his legions and reestablished normal consular government (he had already been elected consul for the following year, 80 BC). He dismissed his lictors and walked ung
1 CONC uarded in the forum, offering to give account of his actions to any citizen. In a manner that the historian Suetonius thought arrogant[8], Caesar later ridiculed Sulla for resigning, stating "Sulla did not know his political ABCs"
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Retirement
1 CONT After his second consulship, he withdrew to his country villa near Puteoli. From this distance, he remained out of the day-to-day political activities in Rome, intervening only a few times when his policies were involved (e.g., Th
1 CONC e Granius episode).
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla's goal now was to write his memoirs, which he finished just before before his death; unfortunately they are now lost. He ended up surrounded by a troupe of actors and dancers. Amongst them was Metrobius. In his last address 
1 CONC to the Senate, Sulla was keen to acknowledge him as his lifetime lover, to the dismay of the audience.[citation needed] With this merry company, Sulla died after a brief illness in 78 BC. The symptoms described in contemporary acc
1 CONC ounts indicate that the cause of death was liver failure,[citation needed] brought on by a lifetime of heavy drinking. His funeral was on a scale unmatched until that of Augustus in 14AD.[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sulla's legacy
1 CONT Even though Sulla's laws concerning qualification for admittance to the Senate and regulations of governorships, among others, remained on Rome's statutes for some time, some of his legislation was repealed less than a decade afte
1 CONC r his death. The veto power of the tribunes and their legislating authority were soon reinstated, ironically during the consulships of Pompey and Crassus. However, Sulla's biggest oversight was his failure to make any provision to
1 CONC  limit the power of the army, allowing it to remain under the control of generals such as himself. While he did pass laws to limit the actions of generals in their provinces (laws that remained in effect well into the imperial per
1 CONC iod), they did not prevent really determined generals such as Pompey and Julius Caesar from using their armies for personal ambition. This highlighted the weakness of the Senate in controlling its most ambitious members.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla is seen to have provided the example that led Caesar to cross the Rubicon, and also provided the precursor of Caesar's Dictatorship. Cicero comments that Pompey once said "If Sulla could, why can't I?". Sulla's example prove
1 CONC d that it could be done, and therefore inspired others to attempt it; he has been seen as another step in the Republic's fall.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sulla's descendents continued to be prominent in Roman politics into the imperial period. His son Faustus Cornelius Sulla issued denarii bearing his ancestors' name, as did another grandson, Quintus Pompeius Rufus. Yet another gra
1 CONC ndson, Faustus (II) Cornelius Sulla was Tiberius' legate in Germany and Consul of AD 7, but was executed for attempting to assassinate Augustus in AD 9. His great-grandson Lucius Cornelius Sulla Faustus was a friend of Germanicus 
1 CONC and appointed consul by Augustus at age 30 . Augustus's daughter Julia was Sulla's great great granddaughter via her mother Scribonia, daughter of Sulla's granddaughter Cornelia. The husband of the emperor Claudius' daughter Claud
1 CONC ia Antonia was Faustus Cornelius Sulla Felix (consul of 52), whose father had been consul in 31.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is the subject of two Italian operas, both of which take considerable liberties with history and change his name to "Lucio Silla": Lucio Silla by Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart and the little-known Silla by Georg Friederich Handel. In
1 CONC  each he is portrayed as a bloody, womanizing, ruthless tyrant who eventually repents his ways and steps down from the throne of Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The name "Sulla"
1 CONT In older sources, his name may be found as Sylla. This is a Hellenism, like sylva for classical Latin silva, reinforced by the fact that our two major sources, Plutarch and Appian, wrote in Greek, and call him S???a.[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and issue
1 CONT First wife, Ilia (possibly Julia (Julilla) Minor, Aunt of Gaius Julius Caesar) 
1 CONT Cornelia Sulla, married first with Gnaeus Pompeius Rufus and later with Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus, mother of Pompeia, second wife of Julius Caesar 
1 CONT Lucius Cornelius Sulla, died young 
1 CONT Second wife, Aelia. Sulla divorced her due to sterility. 
1 CONT Third wife Caecilia Metella Dalmatica 
1 CONT Faustus Cornelius Sulla 
1 CONT Fausta Cornelia Sulla, married Titus Annius Milo (praetor in 54 BC) 
1 CONT Fourth wife, Valeria Messala 
1 CONT Postuma Cornelia Sulla (delivered after Sulla's death) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Chronology
1 CONT around 138 BC – Born in Rome 
1 CONT 107 BC – Quaestor to Gaius Marius 
1 CONT 106 BC – End of Jugurthine War 
1 CONT 104/103 BC – Legatus to Marius in Further Gaul 
1 CONT 103 BC – Legatus to Quintus Lutatius Catulus Caesar in Further Gaul 
1 CONT 101 BC – Defeats the Cimbrii in the battle of Vercellae 
1 CONT 94 BC – Praetor urbanus 
1 CONT 93 BC – Governor of Cilicia 
1 CONT 91/88 BC – General in the Social War 
1 CONT 88 BC – 
1 CONT Holds the consulship (for the first time) with Quintus Pompeius Rufus as colleague 
1 CONT Invades Rome and outlaws Caius Marius the elder 
1 CONT 87 BC – Command of Roman armies to fight King Mithridates of Pontus 
1 CONT 86 BC – Sack of Athens, Battle of Chaeronea 
1 CONT 85 BC – Battle of Orchomenus 
1 CONT 84 BC – Expulsion of Mithridates' forces from Western Asia Minor & reorganization of Asia province 
1 CONT 83 BC – Returns to Italy and invades Rome for the second time 
1 CONT 83-82 BC – War with the followers of Caius Marius the younger and Cinna 
1 CONT 82/1 BC – Appointed "Dictator - rei publicae constituendae causa" 
1 CONT 81 BC – Resigns the dictatorship before the end of the year 
1 CONT 80 BC – Holds the consulship (for the second time) with Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius as colleague 
1 CONT 79 BC – Retires completely from political life 
1 CONT 78 BC – Dies of an intestinal ulcer. Funeral held in Rome 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ Official name of Sulla. The meaning in English is "Lucius Cornelius Sulla, son of Lucius, grandson of Publius, the lucky." His agnomen Felix — the fortunate — was attained later in life, as the Latin equivalent of the Greek nick
1 CONC name he had acquired during his campaigns - epaf??d?t?? , epaphroditus, beloved-of-Aphrodite or (to Romans who read Sulla's Greek title) Venus, due to his skill and luck as a general. 
1 CONT ^ "because the lion cannot defend himself against snares and the fox cannot defend himself against wolves."[1]. 
