0 @N6000000002187705653@ NOTE Ashur-uballit I
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ashur-uballit I, or Assur-uballit I, was king of the Assyrian empire (c. 1365 BC to 1330 BC). His reign marks Assyria's independence from the kingdom of Mitanni, by defeating Shuttarna II; and the beginning of Assyria's emergence 
1 CONC as a powerful empire. Later on, due to disorder in Babylonia following the death of Burnaburiash II, Ashur-uballit established his grandson Kurigalzu II on the Babylonian throne, in the first of what would become a series of Assyr
1 CONC ian interventions in Babylonian affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the Amarna letters, a series of diplomatic letters from various Middle Eastern monarchs to Amenhotep III and Akhenaten of Egypt, we find two letters from Ashur-uballit I, the second being a follow-up letter to the first. In t
1 CONC he letters, Ashur-uballit refers to his second predecessor Ashur-nadin-ahhe II as his "father" or "ancestor," rather than his actual father, Eriba-Adad I, which has led some critics of conventional Egyptian chronology, such as Dav
1 CONC id Rohl, to claim that the Ashur-uballit of the Amarna letters was not the same as Ashur-uballit I. This, however, ignores the fact that monarchs in the Amarna letters frequently refer to predecessors as their "father," even if th
1 CONC ey were not their biological sons. In this case, Ashur-uballit presumably referred to Ashur-nadin-ahhe because the latter, unlike Eriba-Adad I, had previously corresponded with the Egyptian court.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Eriba-Adad I King of Assyria
1 CONT 1365–1330 BCE Succeeded by:
1 CONT Enlil-nirari 
